\id MAT Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Matthew
\toc1 The Holy Gospel of Jesus Christ according to Matthew.
\toc2 Matthew
\toc3 MAT
\mt1 The Holy Gospel of Jesus Christ according to Matthew.
\is The Argument of S. Matthew's Gospel.
\ip S. Matthew's Gospel may be well divided into five parts.
\ip The first part, as touching the Infancy of our Lord Jesus: Chap. 1. and 2.
\ip The second, of the preparation that was made to his manifestation: chap. 3. and a piece of the 4. The third, of his manifesting of himself by preaching and miracles, and that in Galilee: the other piece of the 4. chap. unto the 19.
\ip The fourth, of his coming into Jury, toward his Passion: chap. 19. and 20.
\ip The fifth, of the Holy Week of his Passion in Hierusalem: chap. 21. unto the end of the book.
\ip Of S. Matthew we have Mat. 9. Mar. 2. Lu. 5: How being before a Publican, he was called of our Lord, and made a Disciple. Then Luk. 6. Mar. 3. Mat. 10: How out of the whole number of the Disciples he was chosen to be one of the twelve Apostles. And out of them again he was chosen (and none but he and S. John) to be one of the four Evangelists. Among which four also, he was the first that wrote, about 8 or 10 years after Christ's Ascension.
\is The Sum of the four Gospels.
\ip THE Gospels do tell historically the life of our Lord Jesus, shewing plainly, that he is Christ or the king of the Jews, whom until then, all the time of the Old Testament, they had expected: and withal, that they of their own mere malice and blindness (the iniquity beginning of the Seniors, but at the length the multitude also consenting) would not receive him, but ever sought his death: which for the Redemption of the world, he at length permitted them to compass, they deserving thereby most justly to be refused of him, and so his Kingdom or Church to be taken away from them, and given to the Gentiles. For the gathering of which Church after him, he chooseth Twelve, and appointeth one of them to be the chief of all, with instructions both to them and him accordingly.
\ip The story hereof is written by four: who in Ezechiel and in the Apocalypse are likened to four living creatures, every one according as his book beginneth. S. Matthew to a Man, because he beginneth with the pedigree of Christ as he is man. S. Mark to a Lion, because he beginneth with the preaching of S. John Baptist, as it were the roaring of a lion in the wilderness. S. Luke to a Calf, because he beginneth with a priest of the Old Testament (to wit, Zacharie the father of S. John Baptist) which Priesthood was to sacrifice calves to God. S. John to an Eagle, because he beginneth with the Divinity of Christ, flying so high as more is not possible.
\ip The first three do report at large what Christ did in Galilee, after the imprisonment of S. John Baptist. Wherefore S. John the Evangelist writing after them all, doth omit his doings in Galilee (save only one, which they had not written of, the wonderful bread which he told the Capharnaites he could and would give, Io. 6.) and reporteth first, what he did whiles John Baptist as yet was preaching and baptizing: then, after John's imprisoning, what he did in Jury every year about Easter. But of his Passion all four do write at large.
\ip Where it is to be noted, that from his baptizing (which is thought to have been upon Twelfth-day, what time \it he was beginning to be about 30 years old\it*, Luk. 3.) unto his passion, are numbered three months and three years, in which there were also 4. Easters.
\is THE SUM OF THE NEW TESTAMENT.
\ip THAT which was the sum of the Old Testament, to wit, \it Christ and his Church\it*, as S. Augustine saith\f - \ft Aug. de cat. rud. cap. 3. 4.\f* catechizing the ignorant: the very same is the sum of the New Testament also. For (as the same S. Augustine saith again) \it In the Old Testament there is the occultation of the New: and in the New Testament there is the manifestation of the Old\it*. And in another place\f - \ft Super Exod. q. 73.\f*: \it In the Old doth the New lie hidden, and in the New doth the Old lie open\it*. And thereupon our Saviour said: \f - \ft Mat. 5.\f*\it I am not come to break the Law or the Prophets, but to fulfill them. For assuredly I say unto you, till heaven and earth pass, one jot or one tittle shall not pass of the Law, till all be fulfilled\it*. In which words he sheweth plainly, that the new Testament is nothing else but the fulfilling of the old. Therefore to come to the parts: \it The Gospels\it* do tell of Christ himself (of whom the Old testament did foretell) and that even from his coming into the world, unto his going out thereof again. \it The Acts of the Apostles\it* do tell of his Church beginning at Hierusalem the head-city of the Jews, and of the propagation thereof to the Gentiles and their head-city Rome. And \it the Apocalypse\it* doth prophesy of it, even to the consummation thereof, which shall be in the end of the world. \it The Epistles of the Apostles\it* do treat partly of such questions as at that time were moved, partly of good life and good order.
\c 1
\cd The pedigree of Jesus, to shew that he is Christ, promised to Abraham and David. 18. That he was conceived and born of a Virgin, as Esay prophesied of him. Gen. 12. 22. 2. Reg. 7. Psal. 131. \f - \ft THE FIRST part of this Gospel, of the Infancy of our Saviour Christ.\f* \f - \ft Gen. 12. 22. 2. Reg. 7. Psal. 131.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* \x - \xt Luc. 3, 31.\x* book of the \f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft This Gospel is most solemnly sung in some Churches after Matins upon Christmas day. As also it is the Gospel of the \+it Conception, Nativity,\+it* and \+it Presentation\+it* of our B. Lady: because here is declared the pedigree of her also.\f* generation of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
\v 2 \x - \xt Gen. 21. 25. 29. 38.\x* Abraham begat Isaac, And Isaac begat Jacob. And Jacob begat Judas and his brethren:
\v 3 And Judas begat Phares and Zaram of Thamar. \x - \xt 1 Par. 2, 5. Ruth. 4. 18.\x* And Phares begat Esron. And Esron begat Aram. \ef - \fr 1.3 \fq Thamar. \ft Christ abhorred not to take flesh of some that were ill, as he chose Judas among his Apostles: Let not us disdain to receive our spiritual birth and sustenance of such as be not always good.\ef*
\v 4 And Aram begat Aminadab. And Aminadab begat Naasson. And Naasson begat Salmon.
\v 5 And Salmon begat Booz of Rahab. And Booz begat Obed of Ruth. And Obed begat Jesse.
\v 6 And Jesse begat David the king. And \x - \xt 2. Reg. 12, 24.\x* David the king begat Salomon of her that was the wife of Urias.
\v 7 And \x - \xt 1 Par. 3, 10.\x* Salomon begat Roboam. And Roboam begat Abia. And Abia begat Asa.
\v 8 And Asa begat Josaphat. And Josaphat begat Joram. And Joram begat Ozias.
\v 9 And Ozias begat Joatham. And Joatham begat Achaz. And Achaz begat Ezechias.
\v 10 And Ezechias begat Manasses. And Manasses begat Amon. And Amon begat Josias.
\v 11 And Josias begat Jechonias and his brethren \x - \xt 4 Reg. 24. 2. Par. 36.\x* in the Transmigration of Babylon.
\v 12 And after the Transmigration of Babylon, \x - \xt 1 Par. 3.\x* Jechonias begat Salathiel. \x - \xt 1 Esd. 3.\x* And Salathiel begat Zorobabel.
\v 13 And Zorobabel begat Abiud. And Abiud begat Eliacim. And Eliacim begat Azor.
\v 14 And Azor begat Sadoc. And Sadoc begat Achim. And Achim begat Eliud.
\v 15 And Eliud begat Eleazar. And Eleazar begat Mathan. And Mathan begat Jacob.
\v 16 And Jacob begat Joseph the husband of \sc Mary\sc*: \f 1 \fr 1.16 \ft \+it de qua.\+it*\f* of whom was born \sc Jesus\sc*, who is called \sc Christ\sc*. \ef - \fr 1.16 \fq Joseph. \ft Joseph marrying our Lady as near of kin (for so was the ¹ law) by his pedigree sheweth hers, and consequently Christ's pedigree from David. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Nu. 36.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.16 \fq Husband. \ft True and perfect marriage, and continual living in the same, without carnal copulation. \+it Aug. lib. 2. Consen. Evang. c. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 Therefore all the generations from Abraham unto David, fourteen generations. And from David to the Transmigration of Babylon, fourteen generations. And from the Transmigration of Babylon unto \sc Christ\sc*, fourteen generations.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 1.18 \ft The Gospel upon Christmas eve. and Upon S. Joseph's day the 19. of March.\f* And the generation of \sc Christ\sc* was in this wise. When his mother \sc Mary\sc* was spoused to Joseph, before they came together, she was found to be with child, by the Holy Ghost.
\v 19 Whereupon Joseph her husband for that he was \x - \xt Deu. 24, 1.\x* a just man, & would not \f 2 \fr 1.19 \ft This word \+it just,\+it* sounding that a man is \+it just\+it* indeed, & not only so imputed, Protestants translate, \+it Righteous\+it* in this and diverse other places.\f* put her to open shame: was minded secretly to dismiss her.
\v 20 But as he was thus thinking, behold the Angel of our Lord appeared to him in sleep saying: Joseph son of David, fear not to take \sc Mary\sc* thy wife, for that which is born in her, is of the Holy Ghost. \ef - \fr 1.20 \fq Born in her. \ft The triple good or perfection of marriage accomplished in the parents of Christ, to wit, Issue, Fidelity, Sacrament. \+it Aug. de nup. & conc. li. 1. c. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 21 And she shall bring forth a Son: and thou shalt call his name \f 1 \fr 1.21 \ft JESUS an Hebrew word, in English SAVIOUR.\f* \sc Jesus\sc*. For he shall save his people from their sins.
\v 22 And all this was done that it might be fulfilled which our Lord spake by the Prophet saying.
\v 23 \it Behold a Virgin shall be with child, & bring forth a son, and they shall call his name Emmanuel,\it* \x - \xt Esa. 7. 14.\x* which being interpreted is, God with us. \ef - \fr 1.23 \fq A Virgin. \ft ¹ Our Saviour born in marriage, but yet of a Virgin, would honour both states: and withal, teacheth us against Jovinian the old Heretic and these of our time, that virginity and the continent life are preferred before marriage that hath carnal copulation. \+it See S. Hierom. adv. Iovin. and S. Greg. Nazianz. Ser. 20 de studio in pauperes, in initio.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Virginity preferred.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.23 \fq A Virgin. \ft As our Lady both a virgin and a mother, brought forth Christ the head corporally: so the Church a virgin and a mother, bringeth forth the members of his head spiritually. \+it Aug. li. de Virg. ca. 2.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.23 \fq And bring forth. \ft ¹ The Heretic Iovinian is here refuted, holding that her virginity was corrupted in bringing forth Christ. \+it Aug. hær. 28. Li. 1. cont. Iulian. c. 2.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Our B. Lady's perpetual virginity.\ef*
\v 24 And Joseph rising up from sleep, did as the Angel of our Lord commanded him, & took his wife.
\v 25 And he knew her not till she brought forth her first born Son: & called his name \sc Jesus\sc*. \ef - \fr 1.25 \fq Till, First-born. \ft ¹ Helvidius of old abused these words, \+it till\+it*, and, \+it first-born\+it*, against the perpetual virginity of our B. Lady. \+it Hiero. cont. Helv.\+it* Which truth though not expressed in Scripture, yet our Adversaries also do grant, and Helvidius for denial thereof was condemned for an heretic by tradition only. \+it Aug. hær. 84.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Tradition.\ef*
\c 2
\cd The Gentiles come unto Christ with their offerings, and that so openly, that the Jews cannot pretend ignorance. 3 The Jews with Herod conspire against him. 13 He thereupon fleeth from them into Ægypt. 16 They afterward, seeing their subtlety prevailed not, imagined to oppress him by open persecution. 19 But they at length died, and he returneth to the land of Israel: all according to the Scriptures.
\p
\v 1 \sc When \x - \xt Luc. 2, 7.\x* Jesus\sc* therefore was \f 2 \fr 2.1 \ft The holy feast of the \+it Epiphany\+it* called \+it Twelfth\+it* -day the 6. of January. Upon which day this is the Gospel.\f* born in Bethlehem of Juda in the days of Herod the king, behold there came Sages from the East to Jerusalem, \ef - \fr 2.1 \fq Behold. \ft Our Lord's apparition or Epiphany to these Sages, being Gentiles, their Pilgrimage to him, and in them the first homage of Gentility done unto him the twelfth day after his Nativity: and therefore is \+it Twelfth-day\+it* highly celebrated in the Catholic Church for joy of the calling of us Gentiles. His baptism also and first miracle are celebrated on the same day.\ef*
\v 2 Saying, where is he that is born King of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the East, and are come to adore him. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq Star. \ft Christ's Nativity depended not upon this star, as the Priscillianists falsely surmised: but the star upon his Nativity, for the service whereof it was created. \+it Grego. Ho. 10.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq Come to adore. \ft This coming so far of devotion to visit and adore Christ in the place of his birth, was properly a ¹ Pilgrimage to his person: and warranteth the faithful in the like kind of external worship done to holy persons, places, and things. \fq ¹ \ft Pilgrimage.\ef*
\v 3 And Herod the king Hearing this, was troubled, & all Jerusalem with him.
\v 4 And assembling together all the high Priests and the Scribes of the people, he inquired of them where Christ should be born. \ef - \fr 2.4 \fq Inquired of them. \ft The high Priests were rightly consulted in question of their law and religion, and be they never so ill, are often forced to say the truth by privilege of their unction: as here and after, they did concerning the true Messias.\ef*
\v 5 But they said to him: In Bethlehem of Juda. For so it is written by the Prophet:
\v 6 \x - \xt Mich. 5, 2.\x* \it And thou Bethlehem the land of Juda, art not the least among the Princes of Juda: for out of thee shall come forth the Captain that shall rule my people Israel.\it*
\v 7 Then Herod secretly calling the Sages, learned diligently of them the time of the star which appeared to them;
\v 8 and sending them into Bethlehem, said: Go, & inquire diligently of the child; and when you shall find him, make report to me, that I also may come and adore him.
\v 9 Who having heard the king, went their way; and behold the star which they had seen in the East, went before them, until it came and stood over, where the child was.
\v 10 And seeing the star they rejoiced with exceeding great joy.
\v 11 And entering into the house, they found the child with \sc Mary\sc* his mother, & falling down adored him; and opening their treasures, they offered to him \x - \xt Psa. 71, 10.\x* gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh. \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Adored him \ft ¹ This body \+it (saith S. Chrysostom.)\+it* the Sages adored in the crib. Let us at the least imitate them: thou seest him not now in the crib, but on the altar: not a woman holding him, but the Priest present, and the Holy Ghost poured out aboundantly upon the sacrifice. \+it Ho. 24. in 1. Cor. Ho. 7. in Mt. Ho. de sancto Philogonio.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Adoration of the B. Sacrament.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Treasures. \ft These treasures are as it were the first fruits of those riches and gifts, which (according to the Prophecies of David ¹ and Esay ² ) Gentility should offer to Christ and his Church, and now have offered, specially from the time of Constantine the Great. As also these three Sages, being principal men of their Country, represent the whole state of Princes, kings, and Emperors, that were (according to the said Prophecies) to believe in Christ, to humble themselves to his cross, to foster, enrich, adorn and defend his Church. Whereupon it is also a very convenient and agreeable tradition of antiquity, and a received opinion among the faithful, ³ not lacking testimonies of ancient writers, and much for the honour of our Saviour, ⁴ that these three also were kings: to wit, either according to the state of those Countries, ⁵ where the Princes were \+it Magi\+it*, and ⁶ \+it Magi\+it* the greatest about the Prince: or as we read in the Scriptures, of Melchisedec king of Salem, and many other kings that dwelt within a small compass: or as ⁷ Job's three friends are called kings. These are commonly called the three kings of Colen ⁸, because their bodies are there, translated thither from the East Country: their names are said to have been Gaspar, Melchior, Baltasar. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Psal. 71.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Esa. 60.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Chrys. homil. 27. in Mat. ho. 1. Theophyl. Cic. de Divinat.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft The three kings. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Plin. li. 20.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Esther 1, 13, 14.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Tob. 2.\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Amb. 1. Off. c. 12.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 2.11 \fq Gifts. \ft These sages were three, and their gifts three, and each one offered every of the three, to express our faith of the Trinity. The Gold, to signify that he was a King: the frankincense, that he was God: the myrrh, that he was to be buried as man. \+it Aug. ser. 1. de. Epiph.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 And having received an answer in sleep that they should not return to Herod, they went back another way into their country.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft The Gospel of \+it Childermas\+it* day.\f* And after they were departed, Behold an Angel of our Lord appeared in sleep to Joseph, saying: Arise, & take the child and his mother, and fly into Ægypt; and be there until I shall tell thee. For it will come to pass that Herod will seek the child to destroy him.
\v 14 Who arose, & took the child and his mother by night, and retired into Ægypt: and he was there until the death of Herod:
\v 15 that it might be fulfilled which was spoken of our Lord by the Prophet, saying: \it Out of Ægypt have I called my Son.\it* \x - \xt Os. 11, 2.\x* \ef - \fr 2.15 \fq Out of Ægypt. \ft This place of the Prophet (and the like in the new Testament) here applied to Christ, whereas in the letter it might seem otherwise, teacheth us how to interpret the old Testament, and that the principal sense is of Christ and his Church.\ef*
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 2.16 \ft The Martyrdom of the holy \+it innocents,\+it* whose holy-day\f* Then Herod perceiving that he was deluded by the Sages, was exceeding angry; and sending murdered all the men children that were in Bethlehem, & in all the borders thereof, from two year old and under, according to the time which he had diligently sought out of the Sages. \ef - \fr 2.16 \fq Murdered. \ft By this example we learn how great credit we owe to the Church in Canonizing Saints ¹, and celebrating their holy-days: by whose only warrant, without any word of Scripture, these holy Innocents have been honoured for Martyrs, and their holy-day kept ever since the Apostles' time, although they died not voluntarily, nor all perhaps circumcised, and some the children of Pagans. \+it Aug. ep. 28. Orig. ho. 3. in diversos.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Canonizing of Saints.\ef*
\v 17 Then was fulfilled that which was spoken by Jeremy the Prophet saying:
\v 18 \it A voice in Rama was heard, crying out & much wailing: Rachel bewailing her children, & would not be comforted, because they are not.\it* \x - \xt Iere. 31, 15.\x*
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 2.19 \ft The Gospel on Twelfth eve.\f* But when Herod was dead, behold an Angel of our Lord appeared in sleep to Joseph in Ægypt.
\v 20 saying: Arise, and take the child & his mother, and go into the land of Israel. For they are dead that sought the life of the child.
\v 21 Who arose, & took the child and his mother, and came into the land of Israel.
\v 22 But hearing that Archelaus reigned in Jewry for Herod his father, he feared to go thither: and being warned in sleep retired into the quarters of Galilee.
\v 23 And coming he dwelt in a city called Nazareth: that it might be fulfilled which was said by the Prophets: That he shall be called a Nazarite.
\c 3
\cd John Baptist by his Eremite's life, by his preaching and baptism, calleth all unto penance, to prepare them to Christ. 10 He preacheth to the Pharisees and Sadducees, threatening to them (unless they truly do penance) reprobation here and damnation hereafter: and for salvation sendeth them to Christ and his baptism. Which being far more excellent than John's, yet Christ himself among those penitents vouchsafeth to come unto John's baptism. Where he hath testimony from heaven also.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 1, 4. Luc. 3, 3.\x* in those days \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft THE SECOND part of this Gospel, Of the Preparation that was made to the manifestation of Christ\f* cometh John the Baptist preaching in the desert of Jewry, \ef - \fr 3.1 \fq Desert. \ft ¹ Of this word desert (in Greek \+it eremus\+it* ) cometh the name \+it Eremitages\+it*, and \+it Eremites\+it*, that live a religious and austere life in deserts and solitary places, by the example of S. John Baptist, whom the holy Doctors therefore call the Prince and as it were the author of such profession. \+it S. Chrys. ho. 1. in Marcum & ho. de Io. Baptista. Hiero. ad Eustoch. de custod. virg. Isid. li. 2. c. 15 de divi. off. Bernardus de excel. Io. Baptistæ.\+it* Wherewith the Protestants are so offended that ² they say, S. Chrysostom spake rashly and untruly. And no marvel, for whereas the Evangelist himself in this place maketh him a perfect pattern of penance and Eremitical life, for desert or wilderness, for his rough and rude apparel, for abstaining from all delicate meats (according to our Saviour's testimony also of him Mt. 11, 8. Luc. 7, 33) they are not ashamed to pervert all with this strange commentary, that it was a desert ³ full of towns and villages, his garment was ⁴ camlet, his meat ⁵ such as the country gave and the people there used: to make him thereby but a common man like to the rest, in his manner of life: clean against Scriptures, Fathers, and reason. ⁶ \fq ¹ \ft Eremites. \fq ² \ft \+it Magdeb. Cent. 5. c. 6. pag. 711.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Cent. 1. li. 1. c. 10.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Cythræus in 3. cap. Mat.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Bucerus ibid.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft See Canis. de verb. Dei corruptelis li. 1. c. 2. 3. 4.\ef*
\v 2 & saying: Do penance: for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand. \ef - \fr 3.2 \fq Do penance. \ft ¹ So is the Latin, word for word, so readeth all antiquity, namely S. Cyprian ep. 52. often, and S. Augustine li. 13. Confes. c. 12. and it is a very useful speech in the New Testament, specially in the preaching of S. John Baptist, ² Christ himself, and ³ the Apostles: to signify perfect repentance, which hath not only confession and amendment, but contrition or sorrow for the offence, and painful satisfaction: such as S. Cyprian speaketh of in all the foresaid epistle. But the Adversaries of purpose (as ⁴ namely Beza protesteth) mislike that interpretation, because it favoureth Satisfaction for sin, which they cannot abide. Where if they pretend the ⁵ Greek word, we send them to these places Mat. 11, 21. Lu. 10, 13. 2. Cor. 7, 9. Where it must needs signify, sorrowful, painful, and satisfactory repentance. We tell them also that ⁶ S. Basil a Greek Doctor calleth the Ninivites' repentance with fasting and haircloth and ashes, by the same Greek word \+it μετάνοιαν\+it*. And more we will tell them in other places. \fq ¹ \ft Penance. \fq ² \ft \+it Mt. 4, 17. Lu. 13, 3. 5. Lu. 24, 47.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 2, 38. 26, 20.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Annot. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Μετανοεῖν. Μετάνοια.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Serm. in fam. & siccit.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 For this is he that was spoken of by Esay the Prophet, saying: \x - \xt Esa. 40, 3.\x* \it A voice of one crying in the desert, prepare ye the way of our Lord, make straight his paths.\it*
\v 4 And the said John had his garment of camels' hair, and a girdle of a skin about his loins: and his meat was locusts & wild honey.
\v 5 Then went forth to him Jerusalem and all Jewry, and all the country about Jordan:
\v 6 & were baptized of him in Jordan, confessing their sins. \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq Confessing their sins. \ft John did prepare the way to Christ and his Sacraments, not only by his baptism, but by inducing the people to confession of their sins. Which is not to acknowledge themselves in general to be sinners, but also to utter every man his sins.\ef*
\v 7 And seeing many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his Baptism, he said to them. Ye viper's brood, who hath shewed you to flee from the wrath to come?
\v 8 Yield therefore fruit worthy of penance. \ef - \fr 3.8 \fq Fruit worthy. \ft He preacheth satisfaction by doing worthy fruits or works of penance, which are (as S. Hierom saith in 2. Joel) fasting, praying, alms, and the like.\ef*
\v 9 And delight not to say within yourselves, we have Abraham to our father. For I tell you that God is able of these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
\v 10 For now the axe is put to the root of the trees. Every tree therefore that doth \f 1 \fr 3.10 \ft It is not only damnable, to do ill: but also, not to do good. \+it Aug. Ser. 6. de temp.\+it*\f* not yield good fruit, shall be cut down, & cast into the fire. \ef - \fr 3.10 \fq The axe. \ft Here preachers are taught to dehort from doing evil for fear of Hell, and to exhort to do good in hope of heaven: which kind of preaching our Adver. do condemn.\ef*
\v 11 \x - \xt Mr. 1, 8. Lu. 3, 16. Io. 1, 26. Act. 11, 16. 19, 4.\x* I indeed baptize you in water unto penance, but he that shall come after me, is stronger than I, whose shoes I am not worthy to bear, he shall baptize you in the Holy Ghost & fire. \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq In Water. \ft ¹ John's baptism did not remit sins, nor was comparable to Christ's Baptism, as here it is plain and in many other places. \+it Hiero. adv. Lucifer. Aug. de Bapt. cont. Donat. li. 5. c. 9. 10. 11.\+it* Yet is it an article of our Adv. that the one is no better than the other. Which they say not to extol John's, but to derogate from Christ's baptism, so far, that they make it of no more valure or efficacy for remission of sins, and grace, and justification, than was John's: thereby to maintain their manifold heresies, that Baptism taketh not away sins, that a man is no cleaner nor juster by the Sacrament of Baptism than before, that it is not necessary for children unto salvation, but it is enough to be born of Christian parents, and such like erroneous positions well known among the Calvinists. \fq ¹ \ft John's baptism and Christ's.\ef*
\v 12 Whose fan is in his hand, and he shall clean purge his floor: and he will gather his wheat into the barn, but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire. \ef - \fr 3.12 \fq Floor. \ft This floor is his Church militant here in earth, wherein are both good and bad (here signified by corn and chaff) till the separation be made in the day of judgement: contrary to the doctrine of the Heretics, that hold, the Church to consist only of the good.\ef*
\v 13 Then cometh \sc Jesus\sc* from Galilee to Jordan, unto John, to be baptized of him.
\v 14 But John stayed him, saying: I ought to be baptized of thee, and comest thou to me?
\v 15 And \sc Jesus\sc* answering, said to him: Suffer me for this time. For so it becometh us to fulfill all justice. Then he suffered him.
\v 16 And \sc Jesus\sc* being baptized, forthwith came out of the water: and lo the Heavens were opened to him: & he saw the Spirit of God descending as a dove, & coming upon him. \ef - \fr 3.16 \fq Opened. \ft To signify that heaven was shut in the old law, till Christ by his Passion opened it, and so by his Ascension was the first that entered into it: contrary to the doctrine of the Heretics. \+it See Hebr. 9, 8.\+it* and \+it 11, 40.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 And behold a voice from Heaven saying: This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.
\c 4
\cd Christ going into the desert to prepare himself before his Manifestation, overcometh the Devil's tentations. 12 Beginning in Galilee, as the Prophet said he should: 18 he calleth four Disciples: and with his preaching and miracles draweth unto him innumerable followers.
\p
\v 1 \sc Then \x - \xt Mr. 1, 12. Lu. 4, 1.\x* \f 2 \fr 4.1 \ft The Gospel upon the first Sunday in Lent.\f* Jesus\sc* was led of the Spirit into the desert, to be tempted of the devil. \ef - \fr 4.1 \fq Desert. \ft As John the Baptist, so our Saviour by going into the desert, and there living in contemplation even among brute beasts, and subject to the assaults of the Devil for our sins, ¹ giveth a warrant and example to such holy men as have lived in wilderness for penance and contemplation, called Eremites. \fq ¹ \ft Eremites.\ef*
\v 2 And when he had fasted forty days and forty nights, afterward he was hungry. \ef - \fr 4.2 \fq Fasted forty days. \ft Elias and Moyses (saith S. Hierom) by the fast of 40 days were filled with the familiarity of God, and our Lord himself in the wilderness fasted as many, ¹ to leave unto us the solemn days of fast, (that is, Lent.) \+it Hiero. in c. 58. Esa.\+it* S. Augustine also hath the very like words ep. 119. And generally all the ancient Fathers that by occasion or of purpose speak of the Lent-fast, make it not only an imitation of our Saviour's fast, but also an Apostolical tradition, and of necessity to be kept. \+it Contemn not Lent\+it* (saith S. Ignatius ² ) \+it for it containeth the imitation of our Lord's conversation.\+it* And S. Ambrose ³ saith plainly, that \+it it was not ordained by men, but consecrated by God: nor invented by any earthly cogitation, but commanded by the heavenly Majesty.\+it* And again, that it is sin not to fast all the Lent. S. Hierom's words ⁴ also be most plain: \+it we\+it* (saith he) \+it fast forty days, or, make one Lent in a year, according to the tradition of the Apostles, in time convenient.\+it* This time most convenient is (as S. Augustine saith ep. 119.) immediately before Easter, thereby to communicate with our Saviour's Passion: and (as other writers do add) thereby to come the better prepared and more worthily, to the great solemnity of Christ's Resurrection: beside many other goodly reasons in the ancient Fathers which for brevity we omit. See (good Christian Reader) 12 notable sermons of S. Leo the Great \+it de Quadragesima,\+it* of Lent: namely Ser. 6. and 9. where he calleth it the Apostle's ordinance by the doctrine of the Holy Ghost. See S. Ambrose from the 23. sermon forward: in S. Bernard 7 sermons, and in many other Fathers the like. Last of all, note well the saying of S. Augustine ⁵, who affirmeth that by due observation thereof, the wicked be separated from the good, infidels from Christians, Heretics from faithful Catholics. \fq ¹ \ft The Lent-fast. \fq ² \ft \+it Igna. ep. 5.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Ambr. de Quadrag. ser. 36. 34.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Hier. ep. 54. ad Marcel. adv. Montanum.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Aug. Ser. 69. de temp.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And the tempter approached and said to him: If thou be the Son of God, command that these stones be made bread.
\v 4 Who answered and said: It is written, \x - \xt Deu. 8, 3.\x* \it Not in bread alone doth man live, but in every word that proceedeth from the mouth of God.\it*
\v 5 Then the devil took him up into the holy city, and set him upon the pinnacle of the Temple,
\v 6 and said to him: If thou be the Son of God, cast thyself down, for it is written: \x - \xt Ps. 90, 12.\x* \it That he will give his Angels charge of thee, & in their hands shall they hold thee up, lest perhaps thou knock thy foot against a stone.\it* \ef - \fr 4.6 \fq It is written. \ft Heretics allege scriptures, as here the Devil doth, in the false sense: the Church useth them, as Christ doth, in the true sense, and to confute their falsehood. \+it Aug. cont. lit. Petil. li. 2. c. 51. to. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 7 \sc Jesus\sc* said to him again: It is written, \x - \xt Deu. 6, 16.\x* \it Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God.\it*
\v 8 Again the devil took him up into a very high mountain: and he shewed him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them,
\v 9 and said to him: All these will I give thee, if falling down thou wilt adore me.
\v 10 Then \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Avaunt Satan; for it is written, \x - \xt Deu. 6, 13.\x* \it The Lord thy God shalt thou adore, and him only shalt thou serve.\it* \ef - \fr 4.10 \fq Him only serve. \ft It was not said, saith S. Augustine, The Lord thy God only shalt thou adore, as it was said, Him only \+it shalt thou serve\+it*: in Greek, λατρεύσεις. \+it Aug. sup. Gen. q. 61.\+it* Whereupon the Catholic Church hath always used this most true and necessary distinction ¹: that there is an honour due to God only, which to give unto any creature, were idolatry: and there is an honour due to creatures also according to their dignity, as to Saints, holy things, and holy places. See Euseb. \+it Hist. Ec. li. 4. c. 14. S. Hierom. cont. Vigil. ep. 53. Aug. li. 10. Civit. c. 1. Li. 1. Trin. c. 6. Conc. Nic. 2. Damasc. li. 1. de Imag. Bed. in 4. Luc.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Latria. Dulia.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 Then the devil left him; and behold Angels came, and ministered to him.
\v 12 \x - \xt Mr. 1, 14.\x* And \x - \xt Luc. 4, 14.\x* when \sc Jesus\sc* had heard that John was delivered up, he retired into Galilee \f 3 \fr 4.12 \ft THE THIRD part of the Gospel, of Christ's manifesting himself by preaching, and that in Galilee.\f*:
\v 13 and leaving the city Nazareth, came and dwelt in Capharnaum a sea town, in the borders of Zabulon & Nephthali;
\v 14 that it might be fulfilled which was said by Esay the Prophet.
\v 15 \x - \xt Esa. 9, 1.\x* \it Land of Zabulon & land of Nephthali, the way of the sea beyond Jordan of Galilee, of the Gentiles:\it*
\v 16 \it the people that sat in darkness, hath seen great light:\it* & to them that sat in a country of the shadow of death, \it light is risen to them.\it*
\v 17 From that time \sc Jesus\sc* began to preach, and to say: \x - \xt Mr. 1, 15.\x* Do penance, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand. \ef - \fr 4.17 \fq Do penance. \ft That penance is necessary also before baptism, for such as be of age: as John's, so our Saviour's preaching declareth, both beginning with penance.\ef*
\v 18 \x - \xt Lu. 5, 1.\x* And \sc Jesus\sc* \f 2 \fr 4.18 \ft The Gospel upon S. Andrew's day.\f* walking by the sea of Galilee, saw two brethren, Simon who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother, casting a net into the sea (for they were fishers)
\v 19 and he saith to them: Come ye after me, and I will make you to be fishers of men.
\v 20 But they incontinent leaving the nets, followed him.
\v 21 And going forward from thence, he saw \x - \xt Mar. 1, 19. Luc. 5, 10.\x* other two brethren, James of Zebedee, and John his brother, in a ship with Zebedee their father, repairing their nets: and he called them.
\v 22 And they forthwith left their nets and father and followed him.
\v 23 And \sc Jesus\sc* went round about all Galilee, teaching in their Synagogues, & preaching the Gospel of the Kingdom: and healing every malady, and every infirmity, in the people. \ef - \fr 4.23 \fq Healing every malady. \ft Christ \+it (saith S. Augustine)\+it* by miracles gat authority, by authority found credit, by credit drew together a multitude, by a multitude obtained antiquity, by antiquity fortified a Religion, which not only the most fond new rising of Heretics using deceitful wiles, but neither the drowsy old errors of the very Heathen with violence setting against it, might in any part shake and cast down. \+it Aug. de util. cred. c. 14.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 And the bruit of him went into all Syria, and they presented to him all that were ill at ease, diversely taken with diseases and torments, and such as were possest, and lunatics, and sick of the palsy, and he cured them:
\v 25 And much people followed him from Galilee, and Decapolis, and Jerusalem, and from Jury and from beyond Jordan.
\c 5
\cd First, 3. he promiseth rewards, 13. and he layeth before the Apostles their offices. 17. Secondly, he protesteth unto us that we must keep the commandments, and that more exactly than the Scribes and Pharisees, whose justice was counted most perfit: but yet that it was unsufficient, he sheweth in the precepts of 21. Murder, 27. Adultery, 31. Divorce, 33. Swearing, 38. Revenge, 42. Usury, 43. Enemies. \f - \ft THE SERMON of Christ upon the Mount: containing the pattern of a Christian life, in these three chapters following. Whereof S. Augustine hath two goodly books to. 4.\f*
\p
\v 1 And seeing the multitudes, he \x - \xt Luc. 6, 20.\x* went up into a mountain: and when he was set, his Disciples came unto him,
\v 2 and opening his mouth he taught them, saying.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 5.3 \ft THE EIGHT Beatitudes: which are a part of the Catechism. The Gospel upon Allhallows' day, and upon the feasts of many Martyrs.\f* Blessed are the poor in spirit: for theirs is the Kingdom of Heaven.
\v 4 Blessed are the meek: for they shall possess the land.
\v 5 Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted.
\v 6 Blessed are they that hunger and thirst after justice: for they shall have their fill.
\v 7 Blessed are the merciful: for they shall obtain mercy.
\v 8 Blessed are the clean of heart: for they shall see God.
\v 9 Blessed are the peace-makers: for they shall be called the children of God.
\v 10 Blessed are they that suffer persecution for justice: for theirs is the Kingdom of Heaven. \ef - \fr 5.10 \fq For justice. \ft ¹ Heretics and other malefactors sometime suffer willingly and stoutly: but they are not blessed, because they suffer not for justice. For (saith S. Aug.) they cannot suffer for justice, that have divided the Church, and, where sound faith or charity is not, there cannot be justice. \+it Cont. ep. Parm. li. 1. c. 9. Ep. 50. Psal. 34. Conc. 3.\+it* And so by this scripture are excluded all false Martyrs, as S. Augustine often declareth, and S. Cypr. \+it de Unit. Eccl. nu. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft False Martyrs.\ef*
\v 11 Blessed are ye when they shall revile you, and persecute you, & speak all that naught is against you, untruly, for my sake:
\v 12 be glad & rejoice, for your reward is very great in Heaven. For so they persecuted the Prophets, that were before you. \ef - \fr 5.12 \fq Reward. \ft In Latin and Greek the word ¹ signifieth very wages and hire due for works, and so presupposeth a meritorious deed. \fq ¹ \ft Merces. \+it Μισθὸς.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 \x - \xt Mr. 9, 50. Luc. 14, 34.\x* You are the \f 2 \fr 5.13 \ft The Gospel on the feasts of Doctors.\f* salt of the earth.
\v 14 But if the salt leese his virtue, wherewith shall it be salted? It is good for nothing any more but to be cast forth, and to be trodden of men.
\v 15 You are the light of the world. A city cannot be hid, situated on a mountain. \ef - \fr 5.15 \fq The light. \ft This light of the world, and city on a mountain, and candle upon a candlestick, signify the Clergy, and the whole Church, so built upon Christ the mountain, that it must needs be visible ¹, and cannot be hid nor unknown. \+it Aug. cont. Fulg. Donat. c. 18. Li. 16. cont. Faust. c. 17.\+it* And therefore, the Church being a candle not under a bushel, but shining to all in the house (that is) in the world, what shall I say more (saith S. Augustine) than that they are blind which shut their eyes against the candle that is set on the candlestick? \+it Tract. 2. in ep. Io.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Church visible.\ef*
\v 16 Neither do men light a \x - \xt Mr. 4, 21. Lu. 8, 16. 11, 33.\x* candle and put it under a bushel, but upon a candlestick, that it may shine to all that are in the house.
\v 17 So let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in Heaven. \ef - \fr 5.17 \fq Your light. \ft The good life of the Clergy edifieth much, and is God's great honour: whereas the contrary dishonoureth him.\ef*
\v 18 Do not think that I am come to break the Law, or the Prophets. I am not come to break, but to fulfill.
\v 19 For assuredly I say unto you, \x - \xt Luc. 16, 17.\x* till Heaven and earth pass, one jot, or one tittle shall not pass of the Law, till all be fulfilled.
\v 20 He therefore that shall \x - \xt Ia. 2, 10.\x* break one of these least commandments, and shall so teach men, shall be called the least in the Kingdom of Heaven. But he that shall do and teach, he shall be called great in the Kingdom of Heaven. \ef - \fr 5.20 \fq One of these. \ft Behold how necessary it is, not only to believe, but to keep all the commandments, even the very least.\ef*
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 5.21 \ft The Gospel upon the fifth Sunday after Pentecost.\f* For I tell you, that unless your justice abound more than that of the Scribes and Pharisees, you shall not enter into the Kingdom of Heaven. \ef - \fr 5.21 \fq Your justice. \ft ¹ It is our justice, when it is given us of God. \+it Aug. in Ps. 30. Conc. 1. De Sp. & lit. c. 9.\+it* So that Christians are truly just, and have in themselves inherent justice, by doing God's commandments. Without which justice of works no man of age can be saved. \+it Aug. de fid. & oper. c. 16.\+it* Whereby we see salvation, justice, and justification, not to come of only faith, or imputation of Christ's justice. \fq ¹ \ft True inherent justice.\ef*
\v 22 You have heard that it was said to them of old: \x - \xt Exo. 20, 13. Deut. 5, 17.\x* Thou shalt not kill. And whoso killeth, shall be in danger of judgement.
\v 23 But I say to you, that whosoever is angry with his brother, shall be in danger of judgment. And whosoever shall say to his brother, Raca, shall be in danger of a council. And whosoever shall say, Thou fool, shall be guilty of the Hell of fire. \ef - \fr 5.23 \fq Hell of fire. \ft ¹ Here is a plain difference of sins, some mortal that bring to Hell, some less, and less punishment, called venial. \fq ¹ \ft Venial sins.\ef*
\v 24 If therefore thou offer thy gift at the Altar, and there thou remember that thy brother hath ought against thee; \ef - \fr 5.24 \fq Gift at the altar. \ft Beware of coming to the holy altar or any Sacrament out of charity. But be first reconciled to thy brother, and much more to the Catholic Church, which is the whole brotherhood of Christian men, \+it Heb. 13, 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 25 leave there thy offering before the Altar, and go first to be reconciled to thy brother: and then coming thou shalt offer thy gift.
\v 26 \x - \xt Luc. 12, 58.\x* Be at agreement with thy adversary betimes, whiles thou art in the way with him; lest perhaps the adversary deliver thee to the judge, and the judge deliver thee to the officer, and thou be cast into \f 2 \fr 5.26 \ft This Prison is taken of very ancient Fathers, for Purgatory: namely \+it S. Cypr. ep. 52. ad Anton. nu. 6.\+it*\f* prison.
\v 27 Amen I say to thee, thou shalt not go out from thence till thou repay the last farthing.
\v 28 You have heard that it was said to them of old: \x - \xt Exo. 20, 14.\x* Thou shalt not commit adultery.
\v 29 But I say to you, that whosoever shall see a woman to lust after her, hath already committed adultery with her in his heart.
\v 30 And if thy right eye scandalize thee, pluck it out, & cast it from thee. For it is expedient for thee that one of thy limbs perish, rather than thy whole body be cast into hell.
\v 31 And if thy right hand scandalize thee, cut it off, and cast it from thee: for it is expedient for thee that one of thy limbs perish rather than that thy whole body go into Hell.
\v 32 It was said also, \x - \xt Deut. 24, 1. Mt. 19, 7.\x* whosoever shall dismiss his wife, let him give her a bill of divorcement.
\v 33 But I say to you, whosoever shall dismiss his wife, excepting the cause of fornication, maketh her to commit adultery: And he that shall marry her that is dismissed; committeth adultery. \ef - \fr 5.33 \fq Excepting the cause of fornication. \ft This exception is only to shew, that for this one cause a man may put away his wife for ever: but not that he may marry another: as it is most plain in S. Mark and S. Luke, who leave out this exception, saying: ¹ ² \+it Whosoever dismisseth his wife and marrieth another, committeth adultery.\+it* See the Annot. Luc. 19, 9. But if both parties be in one and the same fault, then can neither of them not so much as divorce or put away the other. \fq ¹ \ft Marriage a Sacrament, and is not dissolved by divorce. \fq ² \ft \+it Mr. 10, 11. Lu. 16, 18.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.33 \fq Committeth adultery. \ft The knot of Marriage is a thing of so great a Sacrament, that not by separation itself of the parties it can be loosed, being not lawful neither for the one part nor the other, to marry again upon divorce. \+it Aug. de bo. Coniug. c. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 34 Again you have heard that it was said to them of old, \x - \xt Exo. 20, 7. Lev. 19, 11.\x* Thou shalt not commit perjury: but thou shall perform thy oaths to our Lord.
\v 35 But I say to you not to swear at all: neither by heaven, because it is the throne of God: neither by the earth, because it is the foot-stool of his feet: neither by Jerusalem, because it is the city of the great King. \ef - \fr 5.35 \fq Not to swear. \ft The Anabaptists here not following the Church's judgement, but the bare letter (as other Heretics in other cases) hold that there is no oath lawful, no not before a judge. Whereas Christ speaketh against rash and usual swearing in common talk, when there is no cause.\ef*
\v 36 Neither shalt thou swear by thy head, because thou canst not make one hair white or black.
\v 37 Let your talk be yea, yea: no, no: and that which is over & above these, is of evil.
\v 38 You have heard that it was said, \x - \xt Exo. 21, 24.\x* An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth.
\v 39 But I say to you not to resist evil: but if one strike thee on thy right cheek, turn to him also the other: \ef - \fr 5.39 \fq Not to Resist evil. \ft Here also the Anabaptists gather of the letter, that it is not lawful to go to law for our right; as Luther also upon this place held, that Christians might not resist the Turk. Whereas by this, as by that which followeth, patience only is signified and a will to suffer more, rather than to revenge. For neither did Christ nor S. Paul follow the letter by turning the other cheek. \+it Io. 18. Act. 23.\+it*\ef*
\v 40 and to him that will contend with thee in judgement, and take away thy coat, let go thy cloak also unto him.
\v 41 and whosoever will force thee one mile, go with him other twain.
\v 42 He that asketh of thee, give to him: and \x - \xt Deu. 15, 7.\x* to him that would borrow of thee, turn not away.
\v 43 \x - \xt Lev. 19, 18.\x* You have heard that it was said, \f 2 \fr 5.43 \ft The Gospel upon the Friday after Ashwednesday.\f* Thou shalt love thy neighbour, & \f 3 \fr 5.43 \ft So taught the Pharisees, not the Law.\f* hate thine enemy.
\v 44 But I say to you, love your enemies, do good to them that hate you: and pray for them that persecute and abuse you:
\v 45 that you may be the children of your father which is in heaven, who maketh his sun to rise upon good & bad, and raineth upon just and \f 1 \fr 5.45 \ft We see then that the temporal prosperity of persons and countries is no sign of better men or truer religion.\f* unjust.
\v 46 For if you love them that love you, what reward shall you have? Do not also the Publicans this?
\v 47 And if you salute your brethren only, what do you more, do not also the Heathen this?
\v 48 Be you perfect therefore, as also your heavenly Father is perfect.
\c 6
\cd In this second chapter of his Sermon, he Controlleth the Pharisees' justice (that is, their alms, prayer, and fasting) for the scope and intention thereof, which was vain glory. 19 Their end also was to be rich, but ours must not be so much as in necessaries.
\p
\v 1 \sc Take\sc* good heed that you do not your justice before men, to be seen of them: otherwise reward you shall not have with your father which is in heaven. \ef - \fr 6.1 \fq Justice. \ft ¹ Hereby it is plain that good works be justice, and that man doing them doth justice, and is thereby just and justified, and not by faith only. All which justice of a Christian man our Saviour here compriseth in these three works, in Alms, fasting, and prayers. \+it Aug. li. perf. just. c. 8.\+it* So that to give alms, is to do justice, and the works of mercy are justice. \+it Aug. in Ps. 49. v. 5.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Good works justify.\ef*
\v 2 Therefore when thou \f 1 \fr 6.2 \ft The first work of justice.\f* doest an alms-deed, sound not a trumpet before thee, as the Hypocrites do in the Synagogues and in the streets, that they may be honoured of men: Amen I say to you, they have received their reward.
\v 3 But when thou doest an alms-deed, let not thy left hand know what thy right hand doeth:
\v 4 that thy alms-deed may be in secret, and thy father which seeth in secret, will repay thee. \ef - \fr 6.4 \fq Repay. \ft ¹ This repaying and rewarding of good works in heaven, often mentioned here by our Saviour, declareth that the said works are meritorious, and that we may do them in respect of that reward. \fq ¹ \ft Merits.\ef*
\v 5 And when ye \f 1 \fr 6.5 \ft The second work of justice.\f* pray, you shall not be as the hypocrites, that love to stand and pray in the Synagogues & corners of the streets, that they may be seen of men: Amen I say to you, they have received their reward. \ef - \fr 6.5 \fq Hypocrites. \ft ¹ Hypocrisy is forbidden in all these three works of justice, and not the doing of them openly to the glory of God and the profit of our neighbour, and our own salvation: for Christ before (c. 5.) biddeth, saying: \+it Let your light so shine before men &c.\+it* And in all such works S. Gregory's rule is to be followed, \+it The work so to be in public, that the intention remain in secret. Ho. 11. in Evang. c. 10.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Hypocrisy.\ef*
\v 6 But thou when thou shalt pray, enter into thy chamber, and having shut the door, pray to thy father in secret: and thy father which seeth in secret, will repay thee.
\v 7 And when you are praying, speak not much, as the Heathen. For they think that in their much-speaking they may be heard. \ef - \fr 6.7 \fq Much speaking. \ft Long prayer is not forbid, for Christ ¹ himself spent whole nights in prayer, and he saith, ² we must pray always, and ³ the Apostle exhorteth to pray without intermission, and the holy Church ⁴ from the beginning hath had her Canonical hours of prayer: but idle and voluntary babbling, either of the heathens to their gods, or of Heretics, that by long Rhetorical prayers think to persuade God: whereas the Collects of the Church are most brief and most effectual. \+it See S. Augustine ep. 121, c. 8. 9. 10.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Luc. 6, 12.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it 18, 1. 21, 36.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it 1 Thes. 5, 17.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Cypr. de orat. Do. in fine.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 Be not you therefore like to them, for your father knoweth what is needful for you, before you ask him.
\v 9 Thus therefore shall you pray. \x - \xt Luc. 11, 2.\x*\f 2 \fr 6.9 \ft The PATER NOSTER.\f* \it \+sc Our Father\+sc* which art in heaven, sanctified be thy name.\it*
\v 10 \it Let thy Kingdom come. Thy will be done, as in heaven, in earth also.\it*
\v 11 \it Give us today our \it* \f 1 \fr 6.11 \ft In S. Luke, the Latin is, \+it Panem quotidianum, daily bread,\+it* the Greek being indifferent to both, \+it τὸν ἐπιούσιον.\+it*\f* \it supersubstantial bread.\it* \ef - \fr 6.11 \fq Supersubstantial bread. \ft By this bread so called here according to the Latin word and the Greek ¹, we ask not only all necessary sustenance for the body, but much more all spiritual food, namely the blessed Sacrament itself ², which is Christ the true bread that came from heaven, and the bread of life to us that eat his body. \+it Cypr. de orat. Do. Aug. ep. 121. c. 11.\+it* And therefore it is called here Supersubstantial, that is, the bread that passeth and excelleth all creatures. \+it Hiero. in 2. Tit. In. 6. Mat. Amb. li. 5. de Sacr. c. 4. Aug. ser. 28. de verb. Do. sec. Mat. S. Germanus in Theoria.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it τὸν ἐπιούσιον.\+it* \fq ² \ft The B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 12 \it And forgive us our debts, as we also forgive our debtors,\it* \ef - \fr 6.12 \fq Debts. \ft ¹ These debts do signify not only mortal sins, but also venial, as S. Augustine often teacheth: and therefore every man, be he never so just, yet because he cannot live without venial sins, may very truly and ought to say this prayer. \+it Aug. cont. duas ep. Pelag. li. 1. c. 14. li. 21. de Civit. c. 27.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Venial sins.\ef*
\v 13 \it And lead us not into tentation. But deliver us from evil. Amen.\it* \ef - \fr 6.13 \fq Lead us not. \ft ¹ S. Cypr. ² readeth, \+it Ne patiaris nos induci.\+it* Suffer us not to be led, as S. Augustine noteth \+it li. de do. persev. c. 6.\+it* and so the holy Church understandeth it, because God (as S. James saith) tempteth no man: though for our sins, or for our probation and crown, he permit us to be tempted. Beware then of Beza's exposition upon this place, who (according to the Calvinists' opinion) saith, that God leadeth them into tentation, into whom himself bringeth in Satan for to fill their hearts: so making God the author of sin. \fq ¹ \ft God is not author of evil. \fq ² \ft \+it In Expos. orat. Do. Iac. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 14 For if you will \x - \xt Mr. 11, 25.\x* forgive men their offences, your heavenly father will forgive you also your offences. \ef - \fr 6.14 \fq If you forgive. \ft This point, of forgiving our brother, when we ask forgiveness of God, our Saviour repeateth again, as a thing much to be considered: and therefore commended in the parable also of the servant that would not forgive his fellow servant, \+it Mat. 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 But if you will not forgive men, neither will your father forgive you your offences.
\v 16 And when you \f 1 \fr 6.16 \ft The third work of justice.\f* fast, be not as the hypocrites, sad. For they disfigure their faces, that they may appear unto men to fast. Amen I say to you, that they have received their reward. \ef - \fr 6.16 \fq Fast. \ft ¹ He forbiddeth not open and public fasts, which in the ² Scriptures were commanded and proclaimed to the people of God, and the Ninivites by such fasting appeased God's wrath: but to fast for vain glory and praise of men, and to be desirous by the very face and look to be taken for a faster, that is forbidden, and that is hypocrisy. \fq ¹ \ft Public fast. \fq ² \ft \+it Iud. 20, 26. 2. Esd. 9. Ioel. 2, 15. Ion. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 \f 1 \fr 6.17 \ft The Gospel upon Ashwednesday.\f* But thou when thou doest fast, anoint thy head, and wash thy face:
\v 18 that thou appear not to men to fast, but to thy father which is in secret: and thy father which seeth in secret, will repay thee.
\v 19 \x - \xt Luc. 12, 33.\x* Heap not up to yourselves treasures on the earth: where the rust and moth do corrupt, & where thieves dig through and steal.
\v 20 But heap up to yourselves treasures in heaven: where neither the rust nor moth doth corrupt, and where thieves do not dig through nor steal. \ef - \fr 6.20 \fq Treasures in heaven. \ft ¹ Treasures laid up in heaven, must needs signify, not faith only, but plentiful alms, and deeds of mercy, and other good works. which God keeping as in a book, will reward them accordingly: as of the contrary the Apostle saith, \+it He that soweth sparingly, shall reap sparingly.\+it* 2. Cor. 9. \fq ¹ \ft Meritorious works.\ef*
\v 21 For where thy treasure is, there is thy heart also.
\v 22 \x - \xt Luc. 11, 34.\x* The candle of thy body is thine eye. If thine eye be simple, thy whole body shall be lightsome.
\v 23 But if thine eye be naught: thy whole body shall be darksome. If then the light that is in thee, be darkness: the darkness itself how great shall it be?
\v 24 \x - \xt Luc. 16, 13.\x* No man can \f 2 \fr 6.24 \ft The Gospel on the 14 Sunday after Pentecost.\f* serve two masters. For either he will hate the one, and love the other: or he will sustain the one, and contemn the other. You cannot serve God and Mammon. \ef - \fr 6.24 \fq Two Masters. \ft Two religions, God and Baal, Christ and Calvin, Mass and Communion, the Catholic Church and Heretical Conventicles. Let them mark this lesson of our Saviour, that think they may serve all masters, all times, all religions. Again, these two masters do signify, God and the world, the flesh and the spirit, justice and sin.\ef*
\v 25 Therefore I say to you, \x - \xt Luc. 12, 22.\x* be not careful for your life, what you shall eat, neither for your body what raiment you shall put on. Is not the life more than the meat: and the body more than the raiment? \ef - \fr 6.25 \fq Careful. \ft Prudent provision is not prohibited, but too much doubtfulness and fear of God's provision for us: to whom we ought with patience to commit the rest, when we have done sufficiently for our part.\ef*
\v 26 Behold the fowls of the air, that they sow not, neither reap, nor gather into barns: and your heavenly father feedeth them.
\v 27 And which of you by caring, can add to his stature one cubit?
\v 28 And for raiment why are you careful? Consider the lilies of the field how they grow: they labour not, neither do they spin.
\v 29 But I say to you, that neither Salomon in all his glory was arrayed as one of these.
\v 30 And if the grass of the field, which today is, and tomorrow is cast into the oven, God doth so clothe: how much more you, O ye of very small faith?
\v 31 Be not careful therefore, saying, what shall we eat, or what shall we drink, or wherewith shall we be covered?
\v 32 for all these things the \f 1 \fr 6.32 \ft They seek temporal things only, and that not of the true God, but of their idols, or by their own industry.\f* Heathen do seek after. For your father knoweth that you need all these things.
\v 33 Seek therefore first the Kingdom of God, and the justice of him, and all these things shall be given you besides.
\v 34 Be not careful therefore for the morrow; for the morrow day will be careful for itself. Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof.
\c 7
\cd In this third and last Chapter of his Sermon, because we know not men's ends, he biddeth us beware of judging: 6 and nevertheless to take open dogs (so he calleth them) as they be. 7 If these works of justice seem too hard, we must pray instantly to him that giveth them. 12 In the conclusion, he giveth one short rule of all justice. 13 and then he exhorteth with all vehemency to the strait way both of the Catholic faith, 21 and also of good life: because only faith will not suffice.
\p
\v 1 \sc Judge\sc* not, that you be not judged. \ef - \fr 7.1 \fq Judge not. \ft It is no Christian part to judge ill of men's acts which be in themselves good and may proceed of good meaning, or of man's inward meanings and intentions which we cannot see: of which fault they must beware that are too suspicious and given to deem always the worst of other men. But to say, that Judas, or an Heretic evidently known to die obstinately in heresy, is damned, and in all other plain and manifest cases, to judge, is not forbidden.\ef*
\v 2 For \x - \xt Luc. 6, 37. Mr. 4, 24.\x* in what judgement you judge, you shall be judged: and in what measure you mete, it shall be measured to you again.
\v 3 And why seest thou the mote that is in thy brother's eye: and the beam that is in thine own eye thou seest not?
\v 4 Or how sayest thou to thy brother: Let me cast out the mote of thine eye; and behold a beam is in thine own eye?
\v 5 Hypocrite, cast out first the beam out of thine own eye; and then shalt thou see to cast out the mote out of thy brother's eye.
\v 6 Give not that which is holy to dogs: neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest perhaps they tread them with their feet, & turning, all to tear you. \ef - \fr 7.6 \fq Holy to dogs. \ft ¹ No holy Sacrament and specially that of our Saviour's blessed body, must be given wittingly to the unworthy, that is, to them that have not by confession of all mortal sins examined and proved themselves. See the Annot. \+it 1. Cor. 11, 27. 28. 29.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Worthy receiving.\ef*
\v 7 \x - \xt Lu. 11, 9.\x* Ask, and it shall be given you: seek, and you shall find, knock, & it shall be opened to you.
\v 8 For every one that asketh, receiveth: and that seeketh, findeth: and to him that knocketh, it shall be opened. \ef - \fr 7.8 \fq Every one that asketh. \ft All things that we ask necessary to salvation with humility, attention, continuance, and other due circumstances, God will undoubtedly grant when it is best for us.\ef*
\v 9 Or what man is there of you, whom if his child shall ask bread, will he reach him a stone?
\v 10 Or if he shall ask him fish, will he reach him a serpent?
\v 11 If you then being naught, know how to give good gifts to your children: how much more will your Father which is in Heaven, give \f 1 \fr 7.11 \ft These good things are grace and all spiritual gifts, and what soever pertaineth to the health of the soul.\f* good things to them that ask him?
\v 12 \x - \xt Luc. 6, 31.\x* All things therefore whatsoever you will that men do to you, do you also to them. For this is the Law and the Prophets.
\v 13 \x - \xt Luc. 13, 24.\x* Enter ye by the narrow gate: because broad is the gate, and large is the way that leadeth to perdition, and many there be that enter by it.
\v 14 How narrow is the gate, and strait is the way that leadeth to life: & few there are that find it!
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 7.15 \ft The Gospel on the 7. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Take ye great heed of false Prophets, which come to you in the clothing of sheep, but inwardly are ravening wolves. \ef - \fr 7.15 \fq Clothing of sheep. \ft ¹ Extraordinary appearance of zeal and holiness is the sheep's cote in some Heretics: but these of this time wear not that garment much, being men of unsatiable sin. This is rather their garment, common to them with all other Heretics, to crake much of the word of the Lord, and by pretensed allegations and ² sweet words of benediction, and specially by promise of knowledge, light and liberty of the Gospel, to seduce the simple and the sinful. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics wolves in sheep-skins. \fq ² \ft \+it Ro. 16, 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 By their \x - \xt Luc. 6, 44.\x* fruits you shall know them. Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles? \ef - \fr 7.16 \fq Fruits. \ft ¹ These are the fruits which Heretics are known by, division from the whole Church, division among themselves, taking to themselves new names and new masters, inconstancy in doctrine, disobedience both to others and namely to spiritual officers, love and liking of themselves, pride and intolerable vaunting of their own knowledge above all the holy Doctors, corruption, falsification, and quite denying of the parts of Scriptures that specially make against them, and these be common to all Heretics lightly. Othersome are more peculiar to these of our time, as Incestuous marriages of vowed persons, Spoil of Churches, Sacrilege and profanation of all holy things, and many other special points of doctrine, directly tending to the corruption of good life in all states. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics known by their fruits.\ef*
\v 17 Even so every good tree yieldeth good fruits, and the evil tree yieldeth evil fruits.
\v 18 A good tree cannot yield evil fruits, neither an evil tree yield good fruits.
\v 19 Every tree that yieldeth not good fruit, shall be cut down, and shall be cast into fire.
\v 20 Therefore by their fruits you shall know them.
\v 21 Not every one that sayeth to me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the Kingdom of Heaven: but he that doth the will of my Father which is in Heaven, he shall enter into the Kingdom of Heaven. \ef - \fr 7.21 \fq Lord, Lord. \ft These men have faith, otherwise they could not invocate, \+it Lord, Lord: Ro. 10.\+it* But here we see that to believe is not enough, and that not only infidelity is sin, as Luther teacheth. ¹ Yea Catholics also that work true miracles in the name of our Lord, and by never so great faith, yet without the works of justice shall not be saved. \+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it* Again, consider here, who they are that have so often in their mouth, \+it The Lord, the Lord,\+it* and how little it shall avail them, that set so little \+it Esa. 53, 4. 1. Pet. 2, 24.\+it* \+it Lu. 9, 57.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith.\ef*
\v 22 Many shall say to me in that day: Lord, Lord, have not we prophesied in thy name, and in thy name cast out devils, and in thy name wrought many miracles?
\v 23 And then I will confess unto them, That I never knew you: depart from me you that work iniquity.
\v 24 \x - \xt Luc. 6, 47.\x* Every one therefore that heareth these words, and doeth them, shall be likened to a wise man that built his house upon a rock,
\v 25 and the rain fell, and the floods came, and the winds blew, and they beat against that house, and it fell not, for it was founded upon a rock.
\v 26 And every one that heareth these my words, and doeth them not, shall be like a foolish man that built his house upon the sand.
\v 27 and the rain fell, and the floods came, and the winds blew, and they beat against that house, and it fell, & the fall thereof was great.
\v 28 And it came to pass, when \sc Jesus\sc* had fully ended these words, the multitude were in \x - \xt Mar. 1, 22. Luc. 4, 32.\x* admiration upon his doctrine.
\v 29 For he was teaching them as having power, and not as their Scribes and Pharisees.
\c 8
\cd Immediately after his Sermon (to confirm his doctrine with a miracle) he cureth a Leper. 5. But above him and all other Jews, he commendeth the faith of the Centurion, who was a Gentile: and foretelleth by that occasion, the vocation of the Gentiles, and reprobation of the Jews. 14 In Peter's house he sheweth great grace. 18 In the way to the sea he speaketh with two, of following him: 23 and upon the sea commandeth the tempest: 28 and beyond the sea he manifesteth the devil's malice against man, in an herd of swine.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft The Gospel on the 3. Sunday after the Epiphany.\f* when he was come down from the mountain, great multitudes followed him:
\v 2 And \x - \xt Mr. 1, 40. Lu. 5, 12.\x* behold a Leper came and adored him saying: Lord, if thou wilt, thou canst make me clean.
\v 3 And \sc Jesus\sc* stretching forth his hand, touched him, saying: I will. Be thou made clean. And forthwith, his leprosy was made clean.
\v 4 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: See thou tell nobody: but go, \x - \xt Lev. 14, 2.\x* shew thyself to the Priest, and offer the gift which Moyses commanded for a testimony to them. \ef - \fr 8.4 \fq Priest. \ft The Priests of the old law (saith S. Chrysostome) had authority and privilege only to discern who were healed of leprosy, and to denounce the same to the people: but the Priests of the new law have power to purge in very deed the filth of the soul ¹. Therefore whosoever despiseth them, is more worthy to be punished than the rebel Dathan and his complices. \+it S. Chryso. li. 3. de Sacerd.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Priests forgive sins.\ef* \ef - \fr 8.4 \fq Gift \ft Our Saviour willeth him to go and offer his gift or sacrifice according as Moyses prescribed in that case, because the other sacrifice being the holiest of all holies, which is his body, was not yet begun. So saith \+it S. Aug. li. 2. q. Evang. q. 3. & Cont. Advers. leg. & Proph. li. 1. c. 19. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 \x - \xt Lu. 7, 1.\x* And \f 2 \fr 8.5 \ft The Gospel upon the Thursday after Ashwednesday. And also in Mass for the sick.\f* when he was entered into Capharnaum, there came to him a Centurion, beseeching him,
\v 6 and saying: Lord my boy lieth at home sick of the palsy, and is sore tormented.
\v 7 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: I will come, and cure him.
\v 8 And the Centurion making answer, said: Lord, I am not worthy that thou shouldest enter under my roof: but only say the word, & my boy shall be healed. \ef - \fr 8.8 \fq Not worthy. \ft \+it Orig. ho. 5. in divers.\+it* When thou eatest (saith he) and drinkest the body and blood of our Lord, he entereth under thy roof. Thou also therefore humbling thyself, say: ¹ Lord I am not worthy. &c. so said ² S. Chrysostom in his Mass, and so doth the Cath. Church use at this day in every Mass. See S. Augustine \+it ep. 118. ad Ianu.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft DOMINE, non sum dignus. \fq ² \ft \+it * Liturg. S. Chrys. Græc. sub finem.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 For I also am a man subject to authority, having under me soldiers; and I say to this go, and he goeth, and to another, come, and he cometh; and to my servant, do this, and he doeth it.
\v 10 And \sc Jesus\sc* hearing this, marveled: and said to them that followed him: Amen I say to you, I have not found so great faith in Israel.
\v 11 And I say to you that many shall come from the East and West, and shall sit down with Abraham, and Isaac, & Jacob in the Kingdom of Heaven:
\v 12 but the children of the Kingdom shall be cast out into the exterior darkness: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
\v 13 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to the Centurion: Go, and as thou hast believed, be it done to thee. And the boy was healed in the same hour.
\v 14 And \x - \xt Mr. 1, 29. Lu. 4, 38.\x* when \sc Jesus\sc* was come into Peter's house, he saw his wife's mother laid, and in a fit of a fever: \ef - \fr 8.14 \fq His wife's mother. \ft ¹ Of Peter specially among the rest, it is evident that he had a wife, but (as S. Hierom saith) after they were called to be Apostles, they had no more carnal company with their wives, as he proveth there by the very words of our Saviour, ² \+it He that hath left wife &c.\+it* And so in the Latin Church hath been always used, that married men may be and are daily made Priests, either after the death of the wife, or with her consent to live in perpetual continency. And if the Greeks have Priests that do otherwise, S. Epiphanius a Greek Doctor telleth them, that they do it against the ancient Canons, and ³ Paphnutius plainly signifieth the same in the first Councel of Nice. But this is most plain, that there was never either in the Greek Church or the Latin, authentical example of any that married after Holy Orders. \fq ¹ \ft Priests' marriage. \fq ² \ft \+it Lib. 1. adv. Iov. C. 14. Mt. 19, 29. Epiph. hæ. 59.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Sozom. li. 1. c. 22. Socrat. li. 1. c. 8.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 and he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she arose, and ministered to him.
\v 16 And when evening was come, they brought to him many that had devils: and he cast out the spirits with a word: and all that were ill at ease he cured:
\v 17 that it might be fulfilled, which was spoken, by Esay the Prophet, saying: \x - \xt Esa. 53, 4. 1. Pet. 2, 24. Lu. 9, 57.\x* \it He took our infirmities, and bare our diseases.\it*
\v 18 And \sc Jesus\sc* seeing great multitudes about him, commanded to go beyond the water.
\v 19 And a certain Scribe came, and said to him: Master, I will follow thee whithersoever thou shalt go.
\v 20 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: The foxes have holes, and the fowls of the air nests: but the Son of man hath not where to lay his head.
\v 21 And \x - \xt Luc. 9, 59.\x* another of his Disciples said to him: Lord, permit me first to go and bury my father.
\v 22 But \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Follow me, and let the dead bury the dead. \ef - \fr 8.22 \fq Let the dead. \ft By this we see that not only no worldly or carnal respect, but no other laudable duty toward our parents, ought to stay us from following Christ, and choosing a life of greater perfection.\ef*
\v 23 \x - \xt Mar. 4, 36. Lu. 8, 22.\x* And \f 2 \fr 8.23 \ft The Gospel on the 4. Sunday after the Epiphany.\f* when he entered into the boat, his Disciples followed him:
\v 24 and lo a great tempest arose in the sea, so that the boat was covered with waves, but he slept.
\v 25 And they came to him, and raised him, saying: Lord, save us, we perish.
\v 26 And he saith to them: Why are you fearful, O ye of little faith? Then rising up he commanded the winds, and the sea, and there ensued a great calm. \ef - \fr 8.26 \fq He commanded. \ft The Church (here signified by the boat or ship) and Catholics, are often tossed with storms of persecution, but Christ who seemed to sleep in the mean time, by the Church's prayers awaketh, and maketh a calm.\ef*
\v 27 Moreover the men marveled, saying: What an one is this, for the winds and the sea obey him.
\v 28 And \x - \xt Mr. 5, 1. Luc. 8, 26.\x* when he was come beyond the water, into the country of the Gerasens, there met him two that had devils, coming forth out of the sepulchres, exceeding fierce, so that none could pass by that way.
\v 29 And behold they cried saying: What is between us & thee \sc Jesus\sc* the Son of God? art thou come hither to torment us before the time.
\v 30 And there was not far from them an herd of many swine feeding.
\v 31 And the devils besought him saying: If thou cast us out, send us into the herd of swine.
\v 32 And he said to them: Go. But they going forth went into the swine, and behold the whole herd went with a violence, headlong into the sea: and they died in the waters.
\v 33 And the swineherds fled: and coming into the city, told all, and of them that had been possessed of devils.
\v 34 And behold the whole city went out to meet \sc Jesus\sc*, and when they saw him, they besought him that he would pass from their quarters.
\c 9
\cd The Masters of the Jews he confuteth both with reasons and miracles: 2 defending his remitting of sins, 9 his eating with sinners, 14 and his condescending to his weak Disciples until he have made them stronger. 18 shewing also in two miracles, the order of his providence about the Jews and Gentiles, leaving the one when he called the other. 27 he cureth two blind men, and one possessed. 35 And having with so many miracles together confuted his enemies, and yet they worse and worse, upon pity toward the people, he thinketh of sending true pastors unto them.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 9.1 \ft The Gospel upon the 18. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* entering into a boat, he passed over the water, and came into his own city.
\v 2 And \x - \xt Mr. 2, 3. Luc. 5, 18.\x* behold they brought to him one sick of the palsy lying in bed. And \sc Jesus\sc* \f 2 \fr 9.2 \ft We see that the faith of one helpeth to obtain for another.\f* seeing their faith, said to the sick of the palsy: Have a good heart son, thy sins are forgiven thee.
\v 3 And behold certain of the Scribes said within themselves: He blasphemeth. \ef - \fr 9.3 \fq He blasphemeth. \ft When the Jews heard Christ remit sins, they charged him with blasphemy, as Heretics now charge his priests of the new Testament, for that they remit sins: to whom he said, \+it Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven &c. Io. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 And \sc Jesus\sc* seeing their thoughts, said: Wherefore think you evil in your hearts?
\v 5 Whether is easier, to say, thy sins are forgiven thee: or to say, arise and walk. \ef - \fr 9.5 \fq Whether is easier. \ft ¹ The faithless Jews thought (as Heretics now a days) that to forgive sins was so proper to God, that it could not be communicated unto man: but Christ sheweth that as to work miracles is otherwise proper to God only, and yet this power is communicated to men, so also to forgive sins. \fq ¹ \ft Men have power to forgive sins.\ef*
\v 6 But that you may know that the Son of man hath power in earth to forgive sins, (then said he to the sick of the palsy,) Arise, take up thy bed, and go into thy house. \ef - \fr 9.6 \fq The son of man in earth. \ft Christ had power to remit sins, and often executed the same, not only as he was God, but also as he was a man, because he was head of the Church, and our chief Bishop and Priest according to his manhood, in respect whereof all power was given him in heaven and earth. \+it Mat. 28. v. 18.\+it*\ef*
\v 7 And he arose, and went into his house.
\v 8 And the multitudes seeing it, were afraid, and glorified God that gave such power to men. \ef - \fr 9.8 \fq Glorified. \ft The faithful people did glorify God that gave such power to men for to remit sins and to do miracles, knowing that that which God committeth to men, is not to his derogation, but to his glory, himself only being still the principal worker of that effect, men being only his ministers, substitutes, and working under him and by his commission and authority.\ef* \ef - \fr 9.8 \fq To men. \ft Not only Christ as he was man, had this power to forgive sins, but by him and from him the Apostles, and consequently Priests. Mat. 28. \+it All power is given me.\+it* Mat. 18. \+it Whatsoever you shall loose in earth, shall be loosed in heaven.\+it* Joan. 20. \+it Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 \x - \xt Mar. 2, 14. Luc. 5, 27.\x* And \f 2 \fr 9.9 \ft The Gospel upon S. Matthew's day. Sept. 21.\f* when \sc Jesus\sc* passed forth from thence, he saw a man sitting in the custom-house, named Matthew; And he saith to him: Follow me. And he arose up, and followed him.
\v 10 And it came to pass as he was sitting at meat in the house, behold many Publicans and sinners came, and sat down with \sc Jesus\sc* and his Disciples.
\v 11 And the Pharisees seeing it, said to his disciples: why doth your master eat with Publicans and sinners?
\v 12 But \sc Jesus\sc* hearing it, said: They that are in health, need not a physician, but they that are ill at ease.
\v 13 But go your ways & learn what it is, \x - \xt Ose. 6, 6.\x* \it I will mercy, & not sacrifice.\it* For I am not come to call the just, but sinners. \ef - \fr 9.13 \fq Not sacrifice. \ft ¹ These are the words of the Prophet, who spake them even then when sacrifices where offered by God's commandment: so that it maketh not against sacrifice, but he saith that sacrifice only without mercy and charity, and generally with mortal sin, is not acceptable. The Jews offered their sacrifices duly, but in the mean time they had no pity nor mercy on their brethren: that is it which God misliketh. \fq ¹ \ft External Sacrifice.\ef*
\v 14 Then \x - \xt Mar. 2, 18. Lu. 5, 33.\x* came to him the Disciples of John, saying: Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but thy Disciples do not fast? \ef - \fr 9.14 \fq Fast often. \ft ¹ By the often fasting of S. John's disciples, we may easily gather that he appointed them a prescript manner of fasting: as it is certain he taught them a form of prayer. \+it Lu. 5.\+it* and \+it 11.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Fasting.\ef*
\v 15 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Can the children of the Bridegroom mourn, as long as the Bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the Bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and \f 1 \fr 9.15 \ft Christ signifieth that the Church shall use fasting days after his Ascension. \+it Epiph. in Comp. fid. Cath. Aug. ep. 80.\+it*\f* then they shall fast.
\v 16 And nobody putteth a piece of raw cloth to an old garment. For he taketh away the piecing thereof from the garment, and there is made a greater rent.
\v 17 Neither do they put new wine into old bottles. Otherwise the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish. But new wine they put into new bottles: and both are preserved together. \ef - \fr 9.17 \fq New wine. \ft By this new wine, he doth plainly here signify fasting and the straiter kind of life: by the old bottles, them that cannot away therewith.\ef*
\v 18 \x - \xt Mar. 5, 22. Luc. 8, 41.\x*\f 2 \fr 9.18 \ft The Gospel upon the 23. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* As he was speaking this unto them, behold a certain Governor approached, and adored him, saying: Lord, my daughter is even now dead; but come, lay thy hand upon her, and she shall live.
\v 19 And \sc Jesus\sc* rising up followed him, and his Disciples.
\v 20 And behold a woman which was troubled with an issue of blood twelve years, came behind him, and touched the hem of his garment. \ef - \fr 9.20 \fq Twelve years. \ft This woman a Gentile, had her disease twelve years, and the Governer's daughter a Jew (which is here raised to life) was twelve years old, \+it Luc. 8.\+it* Mark then the allegory hereof in the Jews and Gentiles. As that woman fell sick when the wench was born, so the Gentiles went their own ways into idolatry, when the Jews in Abraham believed. Again, as Christ here went to raise the wench, and by the way the woman was first healed, and then the wench revived: so Christ came to the Jews, but the Gentiles believed first and were saved, and in the end the Jews shall believe also. \+it Hiero. in Mat.\+it*\ef*
\v 21 For she said within herself: If I shall touch only his garment, I shall be safe. \ef - \fr 9.21 \fq Touch only. \ft Not only Christ's words, but his garment and touch thereof, or any thing to him belonging, might do and did miracles, force proceeding from his holy person to them. Yea this woman returning home ¹ set up an Image of Christ ², for memory of this benefit, and the hem of the same Image did also miracles. This image Iulian the Apostata threw down, and set up his own in stead thereof, which was immediately destroyed by fire from heaven. But the image of Christ broken in pieces by the heathen, the Christians afterward gathering the pieces together, placed it in the Church: where it was (as Sozomenus writeth ³ ) unto his time. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Euseb. li. 7. c. 14. hist.\+it* \fq ² \ft Relics and Images. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 5. c. 20.\+it*\ef*
\v 22 But \sc Jesus\sc* turning and seeing her, said: Have a good heart daughter, \f 1 \fr 9.22 \ft Lo, her devotion to the hem of his garment, was not superstition, but a token of greater faith: so is the devout touching of holy relics.\f* thy faith hath made thee safe. And the woman became whole from that hour.
\v 23 And when \sc Jesus\sc* was come into the house of the Governor, & saw minstrels and the multitude keeping a stir,
\v 24 he said: Depart, for the wench is not dead, but sleepeth. And they laughed him to scorn.
\v 25 And when the multitude was put forth, he entered in, and held her hand. And the maid arose.
\v 26 And this bruit went forth into all that country.
\v 27 And as \sc Jesus\sc* passed forth from thence, there followed him two blind men crying and saying: Have mercy on us, O Son of David.
\v 28 And when he was come to the house, the blind came to him. And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Do you believe, that I can do this unto you? They say to him: Yea Lord. \ef - \fr 9.28 \fq Do you believe that I can? \ft We see here that to the corporal healing of these men he requireth only this faith, that he is able. Which faith is not sufficient to justify them. How then do the Heretics by this and the like places plead for their only justifying faith? \+it See the Annot. Mar. 5, 36.\+it*\ef*
\v 29 Then he touched their eyes, saying: According to your faith, be it done to you.
\v 30 And their eyes were opened, and \sc Jesus\sc* threatened them, saying: See that no man know it.
\v 31 But they went forth, and bruited him in all that country.
\v 32 And when they were gone forth, \x - \xt Mat. 12, 22.\x* behold they brought him a dumb man, possessed with a devil.
\v 33 And after the devil was cast out, the dumb man spake, and the multitudes marveled saying: Never was the like seen in Israel.
\v 34 But \x - \xt Mat. 12, 24.\x* the Pharisees said: \f 2 \fr 9.34 \ft In like manner say the Heretics, calling all miracles done in the Catholic Church, the lying signs of Antichrist.\f* In the prince of devils he casteth out devils.
\v 35 And \sc Jesus\sc* went about all the cities, and towns, teaching in their Synagogues, and preaching the Gospel of the Kingdom, and curing every disease, and every infirmity.
\v 36 And seeing the multitudes, he pitied them; because they were vexed, and lay like sheep that have not a shepherd.
\v 37 Then he saith to his Disciples: The harvest surely is great, but the workmen are few.
\v 38 Pray therefore the Lord of the harvest, that he send forth workmen into his harvest. \ef - \fr 9.38 \fq Pray therefore. \ft Therefore doth the Church pray and fast in the Imber days, when Holy Orders are given, that is, when workmen are prepared to be sent into the harvest. \+it See Act. 13, 2.\+it*\ef*
\c 10
\cd He giveth to the Twelve the power of Miracles, and so sendeth them to the lost sheep of the Jews. 5. with instructions accordingly: 10. and by occasion of the sending foretelleth of the persecutions after his Ascension, arming them and all other against the same, 40. and also exhorting the people to harbour his servants in such times of persecution.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* having called his twelve disciples together, \x - \xt Mr. 3, 13. 6, 7. Lu. 6, 13. 9, 1.\x* he gave them power over unclean spirits, that they should cast them out, & should cure all manner of disease, & all manner of infirmity. \ef - \fr 10.1 \fq Power. \ft Miracles were so necessary to the confirmation of their doctrine beginning then to be preached, that not only Christ himself did miracles, but also, he gave to his Apostles power to do them.\ef*
\v 2 And the names of the twelve Apostles be these: The first, Simon who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother, \ef - \fr 10.2 \fq First Simon. \ft ¹ Peter the first, not in calling, but in preeminence, for (as S. Ambrose saith in 2. Cor. 12.) \+it Andrew first followed our Saviour before Peter: and yet the Primacy Andrew received not, but Peter.\+it* Which preeminence of S. Peter above the other Apostles is so plainly signified in this word, \+it First\+it*, by the judgement even of Heretics, that Beza ², notwithstanding he confesseth the consent of all copies both Latin and Greek, yet is not ashamed to say that he suspecteth that this word was thrust into the text by some favourer of Peter's Primacy. Whereby we have also that they care no more for the Greek than for the Latin, when it maketh against them: but at their pleasure say that all is corrupted. \fq ¹ \ft Peter's Primacy. \fq ² \ft \+it Beza in Annot. novi Test. 1556.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 James of Zebedee, and John his brother, Philip and Barthlemew, Thomas and Matthew the publican, and James of Alphæus, and Thaddæus,
\v 4 Simon Cananæus, & Judas Iscariote, who also betrayed him.
\v 5 These twelve did \sc Jesus\sc* send; commanding them, saying: Into the way of the \f 1 \fr 10.5 \ft They have here commission to preach only in Israel: the time being not yet come to call the Gentiles.\f* Gentiles go ye not, and into the cities of the Samaritans enter ye not:
\v 6 but go rather to the sheep that are perished of the house of Israel.
\v 7 And going preach, saying: That the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand.
\v 8 Cure the sick, raise the dead, cleanse the lepers, cast out devils: gratis you have received, gratis give ye.
\v 9 Do not possess gold, nor silver, nor money in your purses: \ef - \fr 10.9 \fq Do not possess. \ft Preachers may not carefully seek after the superfluities of this life, or any thing which may be an impediment to their function. And as for necessaries, they deserve their temporal living at their hands for whom they labour spiritually.\ef*
\v 10 not a scrip for the way, neither two coats, neither shoes, neither rod. For the workman is worthy of his meat.
\v 11 And into whatsoever city or town you shall enter, inquire who in it is worthy: and there tarry till you go forth.
\v 12 And when ye enter into the house, salute it, saying: Peace be to this house. \ef - \fr 10.12 \fq Peace be to this house. \ft ¹ As Christ himself used these words or this blessing often, \+it Peace be to you\+it*, so here he biddeth his Apostles say the like to the house where they come: And so hath it been always a most godly use of Bishops ² to give their blessing where they come. Which blessing must needs be of great grace and profit, when none but worthy persons (as here we read) might take good thereof: and when it is never lost, but returneth to the giver, when the other party is not worthy of it. ³ Among other spiritual benefits it taketh away venial sins. \+it Amb. in 9. Luc.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Bishops' blessing. \fq ² \ft \+it * Aug. civit. li. 22. c. 8. Leo Imp. in vit. S. Chrys. Socrat. l. 6. c. 14.\+it* \fq ³ \ft It remitteth venial sins.\ef*
\v 13 And if so be that house be worthy, your peace shall come upon it. But if it be not worthy, your peace shall return to you.
\v 14 And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words; going forth out of the house or the city shake off the dust from your feet. \ef - \fr 10.14 \fq Shake off the dust. \ft To contemn the true preachers, or not to receive the truth preached, is a very damnable sin.\ef*
\v 15 Amen I say to you, it shall be more tolerable for the land of the Sodomites and Gomorrheans in the day of judgement, than for that city. \ef - \fr 10.15 \fq More tolerable. \ft Hereby it is evident that there be degrees and differences of damnation in Hell fire according to men's deserts. \+it Aug. li. 4. de Bapt. c. 19.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 10.16 \ft The Gospel upon the Commemoration of S. Paul, Iun. 30.\f* Behold I send you as sheep in the midst of wolves. Be ye therefore \f 2 \fr 10.16 \ft Wisdom and simplicity both be necessary in preachers, Bishops and Priests.\f* wise as serpents, and simple as doves.
\v 17 And take heed of men. For they will deliver you up in Councils, and in their Synagogues they will scourge you.
\v 18 And to presidents and to kings shall you be led for my sake, in testimony to them and the Gentiles. \ef - \fr 10.18 \fq Kings. \ft In the beginning Kings and Emperors persecuted the Church, that by the very death and blood of Martyrs it should grow more miraculously. Afterward when the Emperors and kings were themselves become Christians, they used their power for the Church, against Infidels and Heretics. \+it Aug. ep. 48.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 But when they shall deliver you up, \x - \xt Mar. 13, 11. Luc. 12, 11.\x* take no thought how or what to speak: for it shall be given you in that hour what to speak. \ef - \fr 10.19 \fq It shall be given. \ft This is verified even at this present also, when many good Catholics, that have no great learning by their answers confound the Adversaries.\ef*
\v 20 For it is not you that speak, but the Spirit of your Father that speaketh in you.
\v 21 \x - \xt Luc. 21, 16.\x* The brother also shall deliver up the brother to death, and the father the Son: and the children shall rise up against the parents, and shall work their death,
\v 22 and you shall be odious to all men for my name: but he that shall persevere unto the end, he shall be saved.
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 10.23 \ft The Gospel upon S. Athanasius day. Maij 2.\f* And when they shall persecute you in this city, flee into another. Amen I say to you, you shall not finish all the cities of Israel, till the Son of man come.
\v 24 \x - \xt Luc. 6, 40.\x* The disciple is not above the master, nor the servant above his lord.
\v 25 It sufficeth the disciple that he be as his master, and the servant as his lord. If they have called the goodman of the house Beelzebub, how much more them of his household? \ef - \fr 10.25 \fq How much more. \ft No marvel therefore if Heretics call Christ's Vicar Antichrist, when their forefathers the faithless Jews, called Christ himself Beelzebub.\ef*
\v 26 \f 1 \fr 10.26 \ft The Gospel for some Martyrs, not Bishops.\f* Therefore fear ye not them. For nothing is hid, that shall not be revealed: and secret, that shall not be known.
\v 27 That which I speak to you in the dark, speak ye in the light: and that which you hear in the ear, preach ye upon the house tops.
\v 28 And \f 1 \fr 10.28 \ft A goodly comfort for Christians and Catholics and all good men, in the persecutions of Turk, of Heretics, of all wicked men.\f* fear ye not them that kill the body, and are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him that can destroy both soul and body into Hell.
\v 29 Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing: and not one of them shall fall upon the ground without your father?
\v 30 But your very hairs of the head are all numbered.
\v 31 Fear not therefore: better are you than many sparrows.
\v 32 \x - \xt Mar. 8, 38. Luc 9, 26. 12, 8.\x* Every one therefore that shall confess me before men, I also will confess him before my Father which is in Heaven. \ef - \fr 10.32 \fq Confess me. \ft ¹ See how Christ esteemeth the open confessing of him, that is, of his truth in the Catholic Church, for as when Saul persecuted the Church, he said ² himself was persecuted: so to confess him, and his Church, is all one. Contrarywise, see how he abhorreth them that deny him before men. Which is not only to deny any one little article of the Catholic faith commended to us by the Church: but also to allow or consent to heresy by any means, as by subscribing, coming to their service and sermons, furthering them any way against Catholics, and such like. \fq ¹ \ft Confessing of Christ and his truth. \fq ² \ft \+it Act. 9.\+it*\ef*
\v 33 But he that shall deny me before men, I also will deny him before my Father which is in Heaven.
\v 34 \x - \xt Luc. 12, 51.\x* Do not ye think \f 2 \fr 10.34 \ft The Gospel upon S. Augustine's day our Apostle. Maij 26. And for some Martyrs not Bishops.\f* that I came to send peace into the earth: I came not to send peace, but the sword. \ef - \fr 10.34 \fq Not peace but sword. \ft Christ came to break the peace of worldlings and sinners: as when the son believeth in him, and the father doth not: the wife is a Catholic, and the husband is not. For to agree together in infidelity, heresy, or any other sin, is a naughty peace. This being the true meaning of Christ's words, mark that the Heretics interpret this to maintain their rebellions and troubles which their new gospel breedeth. \+it Beza in no. Test. an. 1565.\+it*\ef*
\v 35 For I came to separate \x - \xt Mich. 7, 6.\x* man against his father, and the daughter against her mother, and the daughter in law against her mother in law.
\v 36 And a man's enemies, they of his own household.
\v 37 He that loveth father or mother more than me, is not worthy of me: and he that loveth son or daughter above me, is not worthy of me. \ef - \fr 10.37 \fq More than. \ft No earthly thing, nor duty to Parents, wife, children, country, or to a man's own body and life, can be any just excuse why a man should do, or feign himself to do or believe any thing, against Christ or the unity and faith of his Church.\ef*
\v 38 And he that taketh not his cross, and followeth me, is not worthy of me.
\v 39 He that hath found his life, shall lose it: and he that hath lost his life for me, shall find it.
\v 40 \x - \xt Luc. 10, 16.\x* He that receiveth you, receiveth me: and he that receiveth me, receiveth him that sent me.
\v 41 He that receiveth a Prophet in the name of a Prophet, shall receive the reward of a Prophet, and he that receiveth a \f 1 \fr 10.41 \ft The reward for harbouring & helping any blessed just person suffering for his justice and conscience.\f* just man in the name of a just man, shall receive the reward of a just man. \ef - \fr 10.41 \fq In the name. \ft Reward for hospitality, and specially for receiving an holy person, as Prophet, Apostle, Bishop, or Priest persecuted for Christ's sake, For by receiving of him in that respect as he is such an one, he shall be partaker of his merits, and be rewarded as for such an one, Whereas on the contrary side, he that receiveth an Heretic into his house and a false preacher, doth communicate with his wicked works. \+it Ep. 2. Io.\+it*\ef*
\v 42 And \x - \xt Mar. 9, 41.\x* whosoever shall give drink to one of these little ones a cup of cold water, only in the name of a disciple, amen I say to you, he shall not lose his reward.
\c 11
\cd John the Baptist in prison also doing his diligence, sendeth some of his disciples to Christ: that as they heard, so they might also see his miracles with their eyes. 7 Afterward Christ declareth how worthy of credit John's testimony was: 16 and inveigheth against the Jews, who with neither of their manners of life could be won: 20 no nor with Christ's infinite miracles: 25 praying God's wisdom in this behalf, 27 and calling to himself all \it such as feel their own burdens.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* it came to pass: when \sc Jesus\sc* had done commanding his twelve Disciples, he passed from thence, to teach & preach in their cities.
\v 2 \x - \xt Luc. 7, 18.\x*\f 2 \fr 11.2 \ft The Gospel on the 2. Sunday in Advent.\f* And when John had heard in prison the works of Christ; sending two of his Disciples, he said to him:
\v 3 Art thou he that art to come, or look we for another? \ef - \fr 11.3 \fq Art thou he. \ft John himself doubted not, for he baptized him and gave great testimony of him before: \+it Io. 1.\+it* But because his disciples knew him not, nor esteemed of him so much as of John their own Master, therefore did he send them unto Christ, that by occasion of Christ's answer he might the better instruct them what he was, and so make them Christ's disciples, preferring them to a better Master.\ef*
\v 4 And \sc Jesus\sc* making answer said to them: Go and report to John what you have heard and seen.
\v 5 \x - \xt Esa. 35, 5. 61, 1.\x* The blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are made clean, the deaf hear, the dead rise again, to the poor the Gospel is preached.
\v 6 and blessed is he that shall not be scandalized in me.
\v 7 And when they went their way, \sc Jesus\sc* began to say to the multitudes of John: What went you out into the desert to see? a reed shaken with the wind? \ef - \fr 11.7 \fq What went you out. \ft ¹ High commendation of John's holiness, as well for his fasting, rough attire, solitary life, and constancy, as for the dignity of his function. \fq ¹ \ft Eremitical life.\ef* \ef - \fr 11.7 \fq Into the desert. \ft The faithful people in all ages resorted of devotion into wilderness to see men of special and rare holiness, Prophets, Eremites, Anchorites &c. to have their prayers or ghostly counsel. \+it See S. Hierom de vita Hilarionis.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 But what went you out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? Behold they that are clothed in soft garments, are in kings' houses.
\v 9 But what went you out to see? A Prophet? Yea I tell you and more than a Prophet.
\v 10 For this is he of whom it is written: \x - \xt Mal. 3, 1.\x* \it Behold I send mine Angel before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee.\it*
\v 11 Amen I say to you, there hath not risen among the born of women a greater than John the Baptist: yet he that is the lesser in the Kingdom of Heaven, is greater than he.
\v 12 And \x - \xt Luc. 16, 16.\x* from the days of John the Baptist until now, the Kingdom of Heaven suffereth violence, and the violent bear it away.
\v 13 For all the Prophets and the Law prophesied unto John:
\v 14 and if you will receive it, he is \x - \xt Mal. 4, 5.\x* Elias that is for to come. \ef - \fr 11.14 \fq Elias. \ft As ¹ Elias shall be the messenger of Christ's later coming, so was John his messenger and Precursor at his former coming: and therefore is he called Elias, because of his like office and like spirit. \+it Luc. 1. Grego. ho. 7. in Evang.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Elias.\ef*
\v 15 He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.
\v 16 And \x - \xt Luc. 7, 31.\x* whereunto shall I esteem this Generation to be like? It is like to children sitting in the market place,
\v 17 which crying to their companions, say: we have piped to you, and you have not danced: we have lamented, and you have not mourned.
\v 18 For \x - \xt Mt. 3, 4.\x* John came neither eating nor drinking; and they say: He hath a devil.
\v 19 The Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say: Behold a man that is a glutton and a wine-drinker, a friend of Publicans and sinners. And wisdom is justified of her children. \ef - \fr 11.19 \fq Eating and drinking. \ft ¹ The wicked quarrellers of the world misconstrue easily all the acts and life of good men. If they be great fasters and austere livers, they are blasphemed and counted hypocrites: if they converse with other men in ordinary manner, then they be counted dissolute. \fq ¹ \ft Penance.\ef*
\v 20 Then \x - \xt Luc. 10, 13.\x* began he to upbraid the cities, wherein were done the most of his miracles, for that they had not done penance.
\v 21 Woe be to thee Corozain, woe be to thee Beth-saida: for if in Tyre & Sidon had been wrought the miracles that have been wrought in you, they had done penance in hair-cloth & ashes long ago. \ef - \fr 11.21 \fq Penance in sackcloth. \ft By this sackcloth and ashes added here and in other places, we see evidently that Penance is not only leaving of former sins, and change or amendment of life past, no nor bare sorrowfulness or recounting of our offences already committed, but requireth punishment and chastisement of our persons by these and such other means as the Scriptures do elsewhere set forth. And therefore concerning the word also, it is rather to be called Penance, as in our translation: than (as the Adversaries of purpose avoiding the word) Repentance or Amendment of life: and that according to the very usual signification of the ¹ Greek word in the most ancient Ecclesiastical Greek writers: who for \+it poenitentes\+it* (which in the Primitive Church did public penance) say, ² \+it ὁι ἐν μετανοία ὄντες\+it*, that is, \+it Men that are doing penance.\+it* And concerning that part of penance which is Confession, the Ecclesiastical history calleth it by the same Greek word, and the penitents coming to confession, \+it τοὺς μετανοοῦντας. Sozom. li. 7. c. 16. Socrat. li. 5. c. 19.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it μετάνοια.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Dionys. Eccl. hier. c. 3. in initio.\+it*\ef*
\v 22 But nevertheless, I say to you, it shall be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon in the day of judgement, than for you.
\v 23 And thou Capharnaum, shalt thou be exalted up to Heaven? thou shalt come down even unto Hell. For if in Sodom had been wrought the miracles that have been wrought in thee, perhaps it had remained unto this day.
\v 24 But notwithstanding I say to you, that it shall be more tolerable for the land of Sodom in the day of judgement, than for thee.
\v 25 \x - \xt Luc. 10, 21.\x* At that time \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said: \f 2 \fr 11.25 \ft The Gospel upon S. Matthias' day Feb. 24. and upon S. Francis' day Octob. 4. and for many Martyrs, and upon S. Paul's day the first Eremite Ian. 15.\f* I confess to thee O Father Lord of Heaven and earth, because thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them to little ones. \ef - \fr 11.25 \fq Little ones. \ft These little ones do not signify here only the unlearned, as though Cobblers, and weavers, and women, and girls had this revelation, and therefore do understand all Scriptures and are able to expound them: but here are signified the humble, whether they be learned or unlearned: as when he saith, ¹ \+it Unless you become as little ones, you shall not enter into the Kingdom of heaven.\+it* And so also the greatest Doctors (who as they were most learned, so most humbled themselves to the judgment of the Catholic Church) are these little ones: and Heretics, who although unlearned, yet vaunt their knowledge and their spirit of understanding above all ancient Fathers and the whole Church, cannot be of these little and humble ones. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Mt. 18, 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 26 Yea Father; for so hath it well pleased thee.
\v 27 All things are delivered me of my Father. And no man knoweth the Son but the Father: neither doth any know the Father, but the Son, and to whom it shall please the Son to reveal.
\v 28 Come ye to me all that labour, and are burdened, and I will refresh you.
\v 29 Take up my yoke upon you, and learn of me, because I am meek, and humble of heart: and you shall find rest to your souls.
\v 30 For my yoke is sweet, and my burden light. \ef - \fr 11.30 \fq Yoke sweet. \ft ¹ What is this light burden and sweet yoke, but his commandments, of which S. John saith 1. Ep. 5. \+it His commandments are not heavy\+it*? clean contrary to the Adversaries that say, they are unpossible to be kept. \fq ¹ \ft The commandments possible.\ef*
\c 12
\cd The blindness of the Pharisees about the Sabboth he reproveth by Scriptures, by reason, and by a miracle. 14. and his death being therefore sought by them, he meekly goeth out of the way, according as Esay had prophesied of him. 22. His casting out of devils also he defendeth against them, 31. and setteth forth the danger they stand in for their horrible blasphemy. 38. And because they ask yet for a sign, he sheweth how worthily they shall be damned, as foretelling how the devil shall possess their Nation, 46. and testifying that although he be of their blood, yet not they for this, but such as keep his commandments are dear unto him.
\p
\v 1 AT that time \x - \xt Mr. 2, 23. Lu. 6, 1.\x* \sc Jesus\sc* went through the corn on the Sabboth: and his Disciples being hungry, began to pluck the ears, and to eat.
\v 2 And the Pharisees seeing them, said to him: Lo, thy Disciples do that which is not lawful for them to do on the Sabboth-days.
\v 3 But he said to them: Have you not read what \x - \xt 1. Re. 21, 4.\x* David did when he was an hungered, and they that were with him:
\v 4 how he entered into the house of God, and did eat the loaves of proposition, which it was not lawful for him to eat, nor for them that were with him, \x - \xt Lev. 24, 9.\x* but for Priests only?
\v 5 Or have ye not read in the \x - \xt Nu. 28, 9.\x* Law, that on Sabboth-days the Priests in the temple do break the Sabboth, & are without blame?
\v 6 But I tell you that there is here a greater than the temple.
\v 7 And if you did know what it is, \x - \xt Ose. 6, 6.\x* \it I will mercy, and not \it* \f 2 \fr 12.7 \ft See the annotation chapter. \+it 9, 13.\+it*\f* \it Sacrifice:\it* you would never have condemned the innocents.
\v 8 For the Son of man is Lord of the Sabboth also.
\v 9 And when he had passed from thence, he came into their Synagogue.
\v 10 And \x - \xt Mr. 3, 1. Lu. 6, 6.\x* behold there was a man which had a withered hand, and they asked him saying: Whether is it lawful to cure on the Sabboths? that they might accuse him.
\v 11 But he said to them: What man shall there be of you, that shall have one sheep: and if the same fall into a ditch on the Sabboths, will he not take hold and lift it up?
\v 12 How much better is a man more than a sheep? Therefore it is lawful on the Sabboths to do a good deed.
\v 13 Then he saith to the man: Stretch forth thy hand, and he stretched it forth, and it was restored to health even as the other.
\v 14 And the Pharisees going forth made a consultation against him, how they might destroy him.
\v 15 But \sc Jesus\sc* knowing it, retired from thence: and many followed him, and he cured them all.
\v 16 and he charged them that they should not disclose him.
\v 17 That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esay the Prophet, saying:
\v 18 \x - \xt Esa. 42, 1.\x* \it Behold my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul hath well liked. I will put my spirit upon him, and judgement to the Gentiles shall he shew.\it*
\v 19 \it He shall not contend, nor cry out, neither shall any man hear in the streets his voice.\it*
\v 20 \it The reed bruised he shall not break, & smoking flax he shall not extinguish: till he cast forth judgement unto victory.\it*
\v 21 \it And in his name the Gentiles shall hope.\it*
\v 22 Then \x - \xt Luc. 11, 14. Mar. 3, 22.\x* was offered to him one possessed with a devil, blind and dumb: and he cured him, so that he spake and saw.
\v 23 And all the multitudes were amazed, and said: Whether this be the Son of David?
\v 24 But the Pharisees hearing it, said: This fellow casteth not out devils but in Beelzebub the prince of the devils. \ef - \fr 12.24 \fq In Beelzebub. \ft The like blasphemy against the Holy Ghost is, to attribute the miracles done by Saints either dead or alive, to the Devil.\ef*
\v 25 And \sc Jesus\sc* knowing their cogitations, said to them: Every kingdom \f 1 \fr 12.25 \ft Therefore the kingdom of heretics cannot possibly stand, because it is always full of division and dissension.\f* divided against itself shall be made desolate: and every city or house divided against itself, shall not stand.
\v 26 And if Satan cast out Satan, he is divided against himself: how then shall his kingdom stand?
\v 27 And if I in Beelzebub cast out devils, your children in whom do they cast out? Therefore they shall be your judges.
\v 28 But if I in the Spirit of God do cast out devils, then is the Kingdom of God come upon you.
\v 29 Or how can a man enter into the house of the strong, and rifle his vessels, unless he first bind the strong? and then he will rifle his house.
\v 30 He that is not with me, is against me: and he that gathereth not with me, scattereth. \ef - \fr 12.30 \fq Not with me. \ft ¹ They that are indifferent to all religions, commonly and fitly called Neuters, joining themselves to neither part, let them mark these words well, and they shall see, that Christ accounted all them to be against him and his Church, that are not plainly and flatly with him and it. \fq ¹ \ft Neuters in religion.\ef* \ef - \fr 12.30 \fq Gathereth not with me. \ft He speaketh not only of his own person, but of all to whom he hath committed the government of his Church, and specially of the chief Pastors succeeding Peter in the government of the whole. As S. Hierom writing to Damasus Pope of Rome ¹, applieth these words unto him, saying of all Heretics, \+it He that gathereth not with thee, scattereth: that is to say, He that is not with Christ, is with Antichrist.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Ep. 58.\+it*\ef*
\v 31 Therefore I say to you: Every sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven men, but the blasphemy of the Spirit shall not be forgiven. \ef - \fr 12.31 \fq The blasphemy of the Spirit. \ft He meaneth not that there is any sin so great, which God will not forgive, or whereof a man may not repent in this life, as some Heretics at this day affirm: but that some heinous sins (as namely this blasphemy of the Jews against the evident works of the Holy Ghost, and likewise Arch-heretics who willfully resist the known truth and works of the Holy Ghost in God's Church) are hardly forgiven, and seldom have such men grace to repent. Otherwise among all the sins against the Holy Ghost (which are commonly reckoned six) one only shall never be forgiven, that is, dying without repentance willfully, called Final impenitence ¹. Which sin he committeth that dieth with contempt of the Sacrament of Penance, obstinately refusing absolution, by the Church's ministry: as S. Augustine plainly declareth in these words. ² \+it Whosoever he be that believeth not man's sins to be remitted in God's Church, and therefore despiseth the bountifulness of God in so mighty a work, if he in that obstinate mind continue till his life's end, he is guilty of sin against the Holy Ghost,\+it* in which Holy Ghost Christ remitteth sins. \+it Enchir. 83. Ep. 50. in fine.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Final impenitence. \fq ² \ft Remission of sins in the Church.\ef*
\v 32 And whosoever shall speak a word against the Son of man, it shall be forgiven him: but he that shall speak against the Holy-Ghost, it shall not be forgiven him neither in this world, nor in the world to come. \ef - \fr 12.32 \fq Son of man. \ft The Jews in their words sinned against the son of man, when they reprehended those things which he did as a man, to wit, calling him a glutton, a great drinker of wine, a friend of the Publicans, a Samaritane, and taking offence because he kept company with sinners, brake the Sabboth, and such like: and this sin might more easily be forgiven them, because they judged of him as they would have done of any other man: but they sinned and blasphemed against the Holy Ghost (called here the finger of God whereby he wrought miracles) when of malice they attributed the evident works of God in casting out devils, to the devil himself: and this sin shall not be remitted, because it shall hardly be remitted, as we see by the plague of their posterity until this day.\ef* \ef - \fr 12.32 \fq Nor in the world to come. \ft ¹ S. Augustine and other Holy Doctors gather hereupon, that some sins may be remitted in the next life, and consequently prove purgatory thereby. \+it De Civit. Dei li. 21, c. 13. D. Gregor, Dial. li. 4, c. 39.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Purgatory.\ef*
\v 33 Either \f 1 \fr 12.33 \ft It is a man's own free will and election, to be a good tree or an ill tree: to bring forth good fruits or bad. S. Augustine upon this place. \+it li. 2. c. 4. de actis cum Felic. Manichæo.\+it*\f* make the tree good, and his fruit good: or make the tree evil, and his fruit evil. For of the fruit the tree is known.
\v 34 You vipers' broods, how can you speak good things, whereas you are evil? For of the aboundance of the heart the mouth speaketh.
\v 35 A good man out of a good treasure bringeth forth good things: and an evil man out of an evil treasure bringeth forth evil things.
\v 36 But I say unto you, that every idle word that men shall speak, they shall render an account for it in the day of judgement. \ef - \fr 12.36 \fq Idle word. \ft If of every idle word we must make account before God in judgement, and yet shall not for every such word be damned everlastingly: then there must needs be some temporal punishment in the next life.\ef*
\v 37 For of thy words thou shalt be justified, and of thy words thou shalt be condemned.
\v 38 \f 1 \fr 12.38 \ft The Gospel upon Imber Wednesday, the first week of Lent.\f* Then answered him certain of the Scribes and Pharisees, saying: Master, we would see a sign from thee.
\v 39 Who answered, and said to them: The wicked and adulterous Generation seeketh a sign: and a sign shall not be given it, but the sign of Jonas the Prophet.
\v 40 For as \x - \xt Ion. 2, 2.\x* Jonas was in the whale's belly three days and three nights; so shall the Son of man be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.
\v 41 The men of Ninivee shall rise in the judgement with this Generation, and shall condemn it: because \x - \xt Ion. 3, 5.\x* they did penance at the preaching of Jonas. And behold more than Jonas here.
\v 42 The \x - \xt 3. Reg. 10, 1.\x* queen of the South shall rise in the judgement with this Generation, and shall condemn it: because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Salomon, and behold more than Salomon here.
\v 43 And \x - \xt Luc. 11, 24.\x* when an unclean spirit shall go out of a man, he walketh through dry places, seeking rest, and findeth not.
\v 44 Then he saith: I will return into my house whence I came out. And coming he findeth it vacant, swept with besoms, and trimmed.
\v 45 Then goeth he, and taketh with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter in and dwell there: and \x - \xt 2. Pet. 2, 20.\x* the last of that man be made worse than the first. So shall it be also to this wicked Generation.
\v 46 \x - \xt Mar. 3, 31. Luc. 8, 20.\x* As he was yet speaking to the multitudes, \f 2 \fr 12.46 \ft The Gospel upon the day of the Seven Brethren &c. Iulij 10.\f* behold his mother and his brethren stood without, seeking to speak to him.
\v 47 And one said unto him: Behold thy mother and thy brethren stand without, seeking thee.
\v 48 But he answering him that told him, said: Who is my mother, and who are my brethren? \ef - \fr 12.48 \fq Who is my mother. \ft The dutiful affection toward our parents and kinsfolk is not blamed, but the inordinate love of them to the hindrance of our service and duty toward God. ¹ Upon this place some old Heretics denied Christ to have any mother. \+it Aug. li. de Fid. & Symb. c. 4.\+it* Neither ever was there any heresy so absurd, but it would seem to have Scripture for it. \fq ¹ \ft All Heretics allege Scriptures.\ef*
\v 49 And stretching forth his hand upon his Disciples, he said: Behold my mother and my brethren.
\v 50 For whosoever shall do the will of my Father, that is in Heaven: he is my brother, and sister, and mother.
\c 13
\cd Speaking in parables (as the Scripture foretold of him, and as meet was for the reprobate Jews:) he sheweth by the parable of the Sower, that in the labours of his Church, three parts of four do perish through the fault of the hearers. 24. and yet, by the parable of good seed and cockle (as also of the Net) that his servants must not for all that, never while the world lasteth, make any schism or separation. 31. And by parables of the little mustard seed and leaven, that notwithstanding the three parts perishing, and oversowing of cockles, yet that fourth part of the good seed shall spread over all the world. 44. And withal, what a treasure, and pearl it is. 53. After all which, yet his own country will not honour him.
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* same day \sc Jesus\sc* going out of the house, sat by the sea side.
\v 2 And \x - \xt Mr. 4, 1. Lu. 8, 4.\x* great multitudes were gathered together unto him, in so much that he went up into a boat & sat: and all the multitude stood in the shore;
\v 3 and he spake to them many things in parables, saying: Behold the Sower went forth to sow.
\v 4 And whiles he soweth, some fell by the way side, and the fowls of the air did come and eat it.
\v 5 Other some also fell upon rocky places, where they had not much earth: and they shot up incontinent, because they had not deepness of earth,
\v 6 and after the sun was up, they parched: and because they had not root, they withered.
\v 7 And other fell among thorns: and the thorns grew and choked them.
\v 8 And other some fell upon good ground: and they yielded fruit, the one an hundredfold, the other threescore, and another thirty. \ef - \fr 13.8 \fq One an hundred. \ft ¹ This difference of fruits is the difference of merits in this life, and rewards for them in the next life, according to the diversities of states, or other differences. Of states, as that the hundred-fold agreeth to virgins professed, threescore-fold to religious widows, thirty-fold to the married. \+it Aug. li. de Virginit. c. 44. & seq.\+it* which truth the old Heretic Jovinian denied (as ours do at this day) affirming that there is no difference of merits or rewards. \+it Hiero. li. 2. adv. Iovin. Ambros. ep. 82. Aug. hær. 82.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Difference of merits and rewards.\ef*
\v 9 He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.
\v 10 And his Disciples came and said to him: Why speakest thou to them in parables?
\v 11 Who answered and said unto them: Because to you it is given to know the mysteries of the Kingdom of Heaven: but to them it is not given. \ef - \fr 13.11 \fq To you it is given. \ft To the Apostles and such as have the guiding and teaching of others, deeper knowledge of God's word and mysteries is given, than to the common people. As also to Christians generally, that which was not given to the obstinate Jews.\ef*
\v 12 For he that hath, to him shall be given, and he shall abound: but he that hath not, from him shall be taken away that also which he hath.
\v 13 Therefore in parables I speak to them: because seeing they see not, and \f 1 \fr 13.13 \ft When God's word is preached, they properly have ears to hear, that have hearts to obey: and they hearing do not hear, which hear by sense of their body, and obey not by consent of their hearts. \+it Aug. de dono persev. c. 14.\+it*\f* hearing they hear not, neither do they understand:
\v 14 and the Prophecy of Esay is fulfilled in them, which saith: \it With hearing shall you hear; and you shall not understand: and seeing shall you see, and you shall not see.\it*
\v 15 \it For the heart of this people is waxed gross, and with their ears they have heavily heard, and their eyes they have shut: lest at any time they may see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart and be converted, and I may heal them.\it* \ef - \fr 13.15 \fq They have shut. \ft In saying that they shut their own eyes, which S. Paul also repeateth \+it Act. 28.\+it* he teacheth us the true understanding of all other places, where it might seem by the bare words that God is the very author and worker of this induration, and blindness, and of other sins: ¹ ² which was an old condemned blasphemy, and is now the Heresy of ³ Calvin: whereas our Saviour here teacheth us, that they shut their own eyes, and are the cause of their own sin and damnation, God not doing, but permitting it, and suffering them to fall further because of their former sins, as S. Paul declareth of the reprobate Gentiles. \+it Ro. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft God is not the author of evil. \fq ² \ft \+it Iren. apud Euseb. li. 5. c. 19.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Calv. li. 2. Instit. c. 4.\+it*\ef*
\v 16 But blessed are your eyes because they do see, and your ears because they do hear.
\v 17 For amen I say to you, that \x - \xt Luc. 10, 23.\x* many Prophets and just men have desired to see the things that you see, and have not seen them: and to hear the things that you hear, and have not heard them.
\v 18 Hear you therefore the parable of the sower.
\v 19 Every one that heareth the word of the Kingdom and understandeth not, there cometh the wicked one, and catcheth away that which was sown in his heart: this is he that was sown by the way side.
\v 20 And he that was sown upon rocky places: this is he that heareth the word, and incontinent receiveth it with joy,
\v 21 yet hath he not root in himself, but is for a time: and when there falleth tribulation and persecution for the word, he is by and by scandalized.
\v 22 And he that was sown among thorns, this is he that heareth the word, and the carefulness of this world and the deceitfulness of riches choket up the word, and he becometh fruitless.
\v 23 But he that was sown upon good ground: this is he that heareth the word, and understandeth, and bringeth fruit, and yieldeth some an hundred-fold, and another threescore, and another thirty.
\v 24 \f 1 \fr 13.24 \ft The Gospel upon the 5. Sunday after the Epiphany.\f* Another parable he proposed to them, saying: The Kingdom of Heaven is resembled to a man that sowed good seed in his field.
\v 25 But when men were a sleep, his enemy came and oversowed cockle among the wheat, and went his way. \ef - \fr 13.25 \fq Oversowed. \ft First by Christ and his Apostles was planted the truth, and falsehood came afterward, and was oversown by the enemy the Devil, and not by Christ, who is not the author of evil. \+it Tertul. de præscript.\+it*\ef*
\v 26 And when the blade was shot up, and had brought forth fruit, then appeared also the cockle.
\v 27 And the servants of the goodman of the house coming said to him: Sir, didst thou not sow good seed in thy field? Whence then hath it cockle?
\v 28 And he said to them: The enemy-man hath done this. And the servants said to him: Wilt thou we go and gather it up?
\v 29 And he said: No lest perhaps gathering up the cockle, you may root up the wheat also together with it. \ef - \fr 13.29 \fq Lest perhaps you pluck up also. \ft The good must tolerate the evil, when it is so strong that it cannot be redressed without danger and disturbance of the whole Church, and commit the matter to God's judgement in the later day. Otherwise where ill men (be they Heretics or other malefactors) may be punished or suppressed without disturbance and hazard of the good, they may and ought by public authority either Spiritual or temporal to be chastised or executed.\ef*
\v 30 Suffer both to grow until the harvest, and in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers: Gather up first the cockle, and bind it into bundles to burn, but the wheat gather ye into my barn. \ef - \fr 13.30 \fq Suffer both to grow. \ft ¹ The good and bad (we see here) are mingled together in the Church. Which maketh against certain Heretics and Schismatics, which severed themselves of old from the rest of the whole world, under pretence that themselves only were pure, and all others both Priests and people sinners: and against some Heretics of this time also, which say that evil men are not of, or in the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Good and evil in the Church.\ef*
\v 31 \x - \xt Mar. 4, 30. Luc. 13, 18.\x* Another parable he proposed unto them, saying: \f 2 \fr 13.31 \ft The Gospel upon the 6 Sunday after the Epiphany.\f* The Kingdom of Heaven is like to a mustard-seed, which a man took and sowed in his field.
\v 32 Which is the least surely of all seeds: but when it is grown, it is greater than all herbs, and is made a tree, so that the fowls of the air come, and dwell in the branches thereof. \ef - \fr 13.32 \fq The least of all seeds. \ft The Church of Christ had a small beginning, but afterward became the most glorious and known common-wealth in earth: the greatest powers and the most wise of the world putting themselves into the same.\ef*
\v 33 Another parable he spake to them: The Kingdom of Heaven is like to leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, until the whole was leavened.
\v 34 All these things \sc Jesus\sc* spake in parables to the multitudes, and without parables he did not speak to them:
\v 35 that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the Prophet saying: I will open my mouth in parables, \x - \xt Psa. 77, 2.\x* \it I will utter things hidden from the foundation of the world.\it*
\v 36 Then having dismissed the multitudes, he came into the house, and his Disciples came unto him, saying: Expound us the parable of the cockle of the field.
\v 37 Who made answer & said to them: He that soweth the good seed, is the Son of man.
\v 38 And the field, is the world. And the good seed: these are the children of the kingdom. And the cockle: are the children of the wicked one.
\v 39 And the enemy that sowed them, is the devil. But the harvest, is \f 1 \fr 13.39 \ft Not God then, but the Devil is the author of all evil.\f* the end of the world. And the reapers, are the Angels.
\v 40 Even as cockle therefore is gathered up, and burnt with fire: so shall it be in the end of the world.
\v 41 The Son of man shall send his Angels, and they shall gather out of his Kingdom all scandals, and them that work iniquity:
\v 42 and shall cast them into the furnace of fire: There shall be weeping & gnashing of teeth.
\v 43 Then shall the just shine as the sun, in the Kingdom of their Father. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear.
\v 44 \f 1 \fr 13.44 \ft The Gospel upon S. Lucy's day Decemb. 13. And S. Anne Iulij, 26. And for some other Virgins & other holy women.\f* The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. Which a man having found, did hide it, and for joy thereof goeth, and selleth all that he hath, and buyeth that field.
\v 45 Again the Kingdom of Heaven is like to a merchant man, seeking good pearls.
\v 46 And having found one precious pearl, he went his way, and sold all that he had, and bought it.
\v 47 Again the Kingdom of Heaven is like to a net cast into the sea, and gathering together of all kind of fishes.
\v 48 Which, when it was filled, drawing it forth, and sitting by the shore, they chose out the \f 1 \fr 13.48 \ft Here also are signified good and bad in the Church.\f* good into vessels, but the bad they did cast out.
\v 49 So shall it be in the consummation of the world. The Angels shall go forth, and shall separate the evil from among the just.
\v 50 And shall cast them into the furnace of fire; there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
\v 51 Have ye understood all these things? They say to him, Yea.
\v 52 He said unto them: Therefore every Scribe instructed in the Kingdom of Heaven, is like to a man that is an householder, which bringeth forth out of his treasure new things and old.
\v 53 And it came to pass: when \sc Jesus\sc* had ended these parables, he passed from thence.
\v 54 And \x - \xt Mr. 6, 1. Luc. 4, 16.\x* coming into his own country, he taught them in their Synagogues, so that they marveled, and said: How came this fellow by this wisdom and virtues?
\v 55 Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called \sc Mary\sc*, and his brethren James, and Joseph, and Simon, and Jude: \ef - \fr 13.55 \fq Carpenter's son. \ft Hereupon Iulian the Apostata and his flatterer Libanius took their scoff against our Saviour, saying (at his going against the Persians) to the Christians, what doeth the Carpenter's son now? And threatening that after his return, the Carpenter's son should not be able to save them from his fury. Whereunto a godly man answered by the Spirit of Prophecy, \+it He whom Iulian calleth the Carpenter's son, is making a wooden coffin for him against his death.\+it* And indeed not long after, there came news that in that battail he died miserably. \+it Sozo. li. 6. c. 2. Theodo. li. 3. c. 18.\+it* The very like scoff use Heretics that call the body of Christ in the B. Sacrament, baker's bread. It seemeth indeed to the senses to be so, as Christ seemed to be Joseph's natural son, but faith telleth us the contrary as well in the one as in the other.\ef*
\v 56 and his sisters, are they not all with us? Whence therefore hath he all these things?
\v 57 And they were scandalized in him. But \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: There is not a Prophet without honour but in his own country, and in his own house.
\v 58 And he wrought not many miracles there because of their incredulity.
\c 14
\cd Hearing the unworthy decollation of John Baptist by Herod, 13. he betaketh him to his usual solitariness in the desert, and there feedeth 5000 with five loaves. 23. And then after the night spent in the mountain in prayer, he walketh upon the sea (signifying the wide world) 28. yea and Peter also: whereupon they adore him as the son of God. 35. And with the very touch of his garment's hem he healeth innumerable.
\p
\v 1 AT that time \x - \xt Mar. 6, 14. Lu. 9, 7. 3, 19.\x* Herod the Tetrarch heard the fame of \sc Jesus\sc*:
\v 2 and said to his servants: This is John the Baptist: he is risen from the dead, & therefore virtues work in him.
\v 3 For Herod apprehended John and bound him, & put him into prison because of Herodias, his \f 1 \fr 14.3 \fq brother's \ft brother Philip's\f* brother's wife. \ef - \fr 14.3 \fq Because of Herodias. \ft It is too ordinary in Princes to put them to death that freely tell them such faults: women, whom they fancy, specially inciting them to such mischief.\ef*
\v 4 For John said unto him: It is not lawful for thee to have her.
\v 5 And willing to put him to death, he feared the People: because they esteemed him as a Prophet.
\v 6 But on Herod's birth-day, the daughter of Herodias danced before them: and pleased Herod.
\v 7 Whereupon he promised with an oath, to give her whatsoever she would ask of him.
\v 8 But she being instructed before of her mother saith: Give me here in a dish the head of John the Baptist.
\v 9 And the king was stroken sad: yet because of his \f 1 \fr 14.9 \ft A wicked and rash oath, and more wickedly fulfilled: because an unlawful oath bindeth no man.\f* oath, and for them that sat with him at table, he commanded it to be given.
\v 10 And he sent, and beheaded John in the prison.
\v 11 And his head was brought in a dish: and it was given to the damsel, and she brought it to her mother.
\v 12 And his Disciples came and took the body, and buried it: \f 1 \fr 14.12 \ft S. John's disciples at this time had well learned their duty toward Christ.\f* and came and told \sc Jesus\sc*. \ef - \fr 14.12 \fq Buried it. \ft An example of duty toward the dead bodies of the faithful. Wherein see the difference of Catholic Christian men and of all infidels, be they Pagans, Apostates, or Heretics. For whereas the Christians had laid the body of this blessed Prophet and Martyr ¹ in Samaria with the Relics of Elias and Abdias, by virtue whereof wonderful miracles were wrought in that place: in Iulian the Apostate's time, when men might do all mischief freely against Christian religion, ² the Pagans opened the tomb of S. John Baptist, burnt his bones, scattered the ashes about the fields: but certain religious Monks coming thither a pilgrimage at the same time, adventured their life and saved as much of the holy Relics as they could, and brought them to their Abbot Philip, a man of God: who esteeming them too great a treasure for him and his, to keep for their private devotion, sent them to Athanasius the B. of Alexandria, and he with all reverence laid them in such a place (as it were by the Spirit of Prophecy) where afterward by occasion of them was built a goodly chapel. \+it Theod. li. 3. c. 6. Ruff. li. 2. c. 27. 28.\+it* Mark here that the Heretics of our time do as those Pagans, to the bodies and Relics of all blessed Saints that they can destroy: and Catholics contrarywise have the religious devotion of those old Christians, as appeareth by the honour done now to his head at Amiens in France. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hiero. in Epitaph. Paulæ. c. 6.\+it* \fq ² \ft Sacrilege against holy Relics.\ef*
\v 13 Which when \sc Jesus\sc* had heard, \x - \xt Mr. 6, 31. Lu. 9, 10. Io. 6, 2.\x* he retired from thence by boat, into a desert place apart, and the multitudes having heard of it, followed him on foot out of the cities. \ef - \fr 14.13 \fq Retired. \ft Christ much esteemed John, and withdrew himself aside, to give example of moderate mourning for the departed, and to shew the horror of that execrable murder, as in the Primitive Church many good men seeing the miserable state of the world in the time of persecution, and the sins that abounded withal: took an occasion to forsake those tumults, and to give themselves to contemplation: and for that purpose retired into the deserts of Ægypt and elsewhere, to do penance for their own sins and the sins of the world. Whereupon partly rose that infinite number of Monks and Eremites ¹, of whom the Fathers and Ecclesiastical histories make mention. \+it Hiero. to. 2. in vit. Pauli Eremitæ. Sozo. li. 1. c. 12. 13.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Eremites.\ef*
\v 14 And he coming forth saw a great multitude, and pitied them, and cured their diseased.
\v 15 And when it was evening, his Disciples came unto him, saying: It is a desert place, and the hour is now past: dismiss the multitudes, that going into the towns, they may buy themselves victuals.
\v 16 But \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: They have no need to go: give ye them to eat.
\v 17 They answered him: We have not here, but five loaves, and two fishes.
\v 18 Who said to them: Bring them hither to me.
\v 19 And when he had commanded the multitude to sit down upon the grass, he took the five loaves and the two fishes, and looking up unto Heaven he blessed, and brake, and gave the loaves to his Disciples, and the Disciples to the multitudes. \ef - \fr 14.19 \fq The Disciples to the multitudes. \ft A figure of the ministry of the Apostles, who as they here had the distribution and ordering of these miraculous loaves, so had they also to bestow and dispense all the food of our souls in ministering of the word and Sacraments, neither may laymen challenge the same.\ef*
\v 20 And they did all eat, and had their fill. And they took the leavings, twelve full baskets of the fragments.
\v 21 And the number of them that did eat was, five thousand men, beside women and children.
\v 22 \f 1 \fr 14.22 \ft The Gospel upon the Octave of S. Peter and S. Paul. Iulij 6.\f* And forthwith \sc Jesus\sc* commanded his Disciples to go up into the boat, and to go before him over the water, till he dismissed the multitudes.
\v 23 And having dismissed the multitude, he \x - \xt Mr. 6, 46. Io. 6, 16.\x* ascended into a mountain alone to pray. And when it was evening, he was there alone.
\v 24 But the boat in the midst of the sea was tossed with waves: for the wind was contrary.
\v 25 And in the fourth watch of the night, he came unto them walking upon the sea.
\v 26 And seeing him upon the sea walking, they were troubled saying: That it is a ghost: & for fear they cried out. \ef - \fr 14.26 \fq Walking. \ft when not only Christ, but by his power Peter also walketh upon the waters, it is evident that he can dispose of his own body above nature and contrary to the natural conditions thereof, as to go through a door. \+it Io. 20.\+it* to be in the compass of a little bread. \+it Epiphan. in Anchorato.\+it*\ef*
\v 27 And immediately \sc Jesus\sc* spake unto them, saying: Have confidence: it is I, fear ye not.
\v 28 And Peter making answer said: Lord if it be thou, bid me come to thee upon the waters.
\v 29 And he said, Come. And Peter descending out of the boat, walked upon the water to come to \sc Jesus\sc*. \ef - \fr 14.29 \fq Walked. \ft ¹ Peter (saith S. Bernard) walking upon the waters, as Christ did, declared himself the only Vicar of Christ, which should be ruler not over one people, but over all. For many waters, are many peoples. \+it Bernard. li. 2. de consid. c. 8.\+it* See the place, how he deduceth from Peter the like authority and jurisdiction to his successor the Bishop of Rome. \fq ¹ \ft Peter's Primacy.\ef*
\v 30 But seeing the wind rough, he was afraid: and when he began to be drowned, he cried out saying: Lord, save me.
\v 31 And incontinent \f 1 \fr 14.31 \ft Not withstanding the infirmities of them that govern the Church, yet Christ sustaineth them, and holdeth them up, yea and by them, whatsoever they are, he upholdeth and preserveth his Church.\f* \sc Jesus\sc* stretching forth his hand took hold of him, and said unto him: O thou of little faith, why didst thou doubt?
\v 32 And when they were gone up into the boat, the wind ceased.
\v 33 And they that were in the boat, came and adored him, saying: Indeed thou art the Son of God.
\v 34 And having passed the water, they came into the country of Genesar.
\v 35 And when the men of that place understood of him, they sent into all that country, and brought unto him all that were ill at ease:
\v 36 and they besought him that they might touch but the \f 1 \fr 14.36 \ft See before, chap. \+it 9, 20.\+it*\f* hem of his garment, and whosoever did touch, were made whole.
\c 15
\cd The Pharisees of Hierusalem coming so far to carp him, he chargeth with a tradition contrary to God's commandment. 10. And to the people he yieldeth the reason of that which they reprove: 15. and again to his Disciples, shewing the ground of the Pharisaical washing (to wit, that meats otherwise defile the soul) to be false. 21. then he goeth aside to hide himself among the Gentiles: where, in a woman he findeth such faith, that he is fain, lest the Gentiles should before the time extort the whole bread, as she had a crumb, to return to the Jews. 34. where (all contrary to those Pharisees) the common people seek wonderfully unto him, and he after he hath cured their diseased, feedeth 4000 of them with seven loaves.
\p
\v 1 \sc Then\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 7, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 15.1 \ft The Gospel upon Wednesday the 3. week in Lent.\f* came to him from Jerusalem Scribes and Pharisees, saying:
\v 2 Why do thy Disciples transgress the tradition of the Ancients? For they wash not their hands when they eat bread.
\v 3 But he answering said to them: Why do you also transgress the commandment of God for your tradition? For God said:
\v 4 \x - \xt Exo. 20, 12.\x* \it Honour father and mother.\it* And: \x - \xt Lev. 20, 9.\x* \it He that shall curse father or mother, dying let him die.\it*
\v 5 But you say: whosoever shall say to father or mother, the gift whatsoever proceedeth from me, shall profit thee:
\v 6 And shall not honour his father or his mother: & you have made frustrate the commandment of God for your own tradition.
\v 7 Hypocrites, well hath Esay Prophesied of you, saying:
\v 8 \x - \xt Esa. 29, 13.\x* \it This People honoureth me with their lips: but their heart is far from me.\it* \ef - \fr 15.8 \fq With their lips. \ft This is to be understood properly of such as have ever God in their mouth, the word of our Lord, the Scriptures, the Gospel, but in their heart and all their life be indeed Godless. It may be applied also to such as say their prayers without attention or elevation of mind to God, whether he understand the prayers or no, that saith them. For many a poor Christian man that understandeth not the words he speaketh, hath his heart nearer heaven, more fervour and devotion, more edification to himself, more profit in spirit (as the Apostle speaketh ¹ ) and less distractions, than not only all Heretics which have no true feeling of such things, but than many learned Catholics. And therefore it is not to be understood of praying in unknown tongues, as Heretics sometime expound it, far wide from the circumstance of the place and Christ's intention, speaking of the hypocritical Jews. \fq ¹ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 14.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 \it And in vain do they worship me, teaching doctrines and commandments of men.\it* \ef - \fr 15.9 \fq Commandments of men. \ft Such only are here called traditions, doctrines, or commandments of men, which be either repugnant to God's laws, as this of defrauding their parents under pretence of religion: or which at the least be frivolous, unprofitable, and impertinent to piety or true worship, as that other sort of so often washing hands and vessels without regard of inward purity of heart and mind. ¹ Let no man therefore be abused with the Protestants' perverse application of this place against the holy laws, canons, and precepts of the Church and our spiritual Governors, concerning fasts, festivities, and other rules of discipline and due order in life and in the service of God. For such are not repugnant but consonant to God's word and all piety, and our Lord is truly honoured, worshipped, and served both by the making and also by the observing of them. ² S. Paul gave commandments both by his epistles and by word of mouth, even in such matters wherein Christ had prescribed nothing at all, and he chargeth the faithful to observe the same. ³ The Apostles and Priests at Hierusalem made laws, and the Christians were bound to obey them. ⁴ The Keeping of Sunday in stead of the Sabboth is the tradition of the Apostles, and dare the Heretics deny the due observation thereof to be an acceptable worship of God? ⁵ They prescribed the Feasts of Easter, and Whitsuntide and other Solemnities of Christ and his Saints, which the Protestants themselves observe. ⁶ They appointed the Lent and Imber fasts and other, as well to chastise the concupiscence of man, as to serve and please God thereby, as is plain in the fasting of Anna, Tobie, Judith, Esther, who served and pleased God thereby. Therefore neither these nor other such Apostolic Ordinances, nor any precepts of the holy Church or of our lawful Pastors are implied in these Pharisaical traditions here reprehended, nor to be counted or called the doctrines and commandments of men, because they are not made by mere human power, but by Christ's warrant and authority, and by such as he hath placed to rule his Church, of whom he saith, ⁷ \+it He that heareth you, heareth me: he that despiseth you, despiseth me.\+it* They are made by the Holy Ghost, joining with our Pastors in the regiment of the faithful, they are made by our Mother the Church, which whosoever obeyeth not, ⁸ we are warned to take him as an Heathen. But on the other side, all laws, doctrines, services and injunctions of Heretics, how soever pretended to be consonant to the Scriptures, be commandments of men: because both the things by them prescribed are impious, and the Authors have neither sending nor commission from God. \fq ¹ \ft The difference between the Jewish traditions here reprehended, and the Church's Apostolical traditions. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Thes. 2, 15. 1. Cor. 11.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 15.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Aug. Ser. de temp. 251. See 1. Cor. 16, 2.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Epiph. hær. 75.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Hiero. ep. 54. ad Marcel. cont. Mont.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft \+it Luc. 2, 37. Tob. 12. Judith c. 8. Esth. 4. Luc. 10, 16\+it* \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Mat. 18, 17.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 And having called together the multitudes unto him, he said to them: Hear ye and understand.
\v 11 Not that which entereth into the mouth, defileth a man: but that which proceedeth out of the mouth, that defileth a man. \ef - \fr 15.11 \fq Not that which entereth. \ft ¹ The Catholics do not abstain from certain meats, for that they esteem any meat unclean either by creation or by Judaical observation: but they abstain for chastisement of their concupiscences. \+it Aug. li. de mor. Ec. Cath. c. 33.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Difference of meats.\ef*
\v 12 Then came his Disciples, and said to him: Dost thou know that the Pharisees, when they heard this word, were scandalized?
\v 13 But he answering said: All planting which my Heavenly Father hath not planted, shall be rooted up.
\v 14 Let them alone: blind they are, guides of the blind. And if the blind be guide to the blind, both fall into the ditch.
\v 15 And Peter answering said to him: Expound us this parable.
\v 16 But he said: Are you also as yet without understanding?
\v 17 Do you not understand, that all that entereth into the mouth, goeth into the belly, and is cast forth into the privy?
\v 18 But the things that proceed out of the mouth, come forth from the heart, and those things defile a man. \ef - \fr 15.18 \fq Defile a man. \ft It is sin only which properly defileth man, and meats of themselves or of their own nature do not defile: but so far as by accident they make a man to sin, as the disobedience of God's commandment or of our Superiors who forbid some meats for certain times and causes, is a sin. ¹ As the apple which our first parents did eat of ², though of itself it did not defile them, yet being eaten against the precept, it did defile. So neither flesh nor fish of itself doth defile, but the breach of the Church's precept defileth. \fq ¹ \ft Catholic abstinence. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 For from the heart come forth evil cogitations, murders, adulteries, fornications, thefts, false testimonies, blasphemies.
\v 20 These are the things that defile a man. But to eat with unwashen hands, doth not defile a man.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 15.21 \ft The Gospel upon Thursday the first week in Lent.\f* And \sc Jesus\sc* went forth from thence and retired into the quarters of Tyre and Sidon.
\v 22 And behold \x - \xt Mr. 7, 25.\x* a woman of Chanaan came forth out of those coasts, & crying out, said to him: Have mercy upon me, O Lord the Son of David: my daughter is sore vexed of a devil.
\v 23 Who answered her not a word. And his Disciples came and besought him saying: Dismiss her, because she cryeth out after us.
\v 24 And he answering said: I was not sent but to the sheep that are lost of the house of Israel.
\v 25 But she came and adored him, saying: Lord, help me.
\v 26 Who answering, said: It is not good to take the bread of the Children, and to cast it to the dogs.
\v 27 But she said: Yea Lord; for the whelps also eat of the crumbs that fall from the table of their masters.
\v 28 Then \sc Jesus\sc* answering said to her: O woman, \f 1 \fr 15.28 \ft It were a strange case that Christ should commend in this woman a sole faith without good works, that is to say, a dead faith such as could not work by love, and which S. James doubted not to call the faith not of Christians but of Devils. \+it Aug. de Fid. & Op. c. 16.\+it*\f* great is thy faith: be it done to thee as thou wilt: And her daughter was made whole from that hour.
\v 29 And when \sc Jesus\sc* was passed from thence, he came beside the sea of Galilee: & ascending into the mountain, sat there.
\v 30 And there came to him great multitudes, having with them dumb persons, blind, lame, feeble, and many others: and they cast them down at his feet, and he cured them:
\v 31 So that the multitudes marveled seeing the dumb speak, the lame walk, the blind see: and they magnified the God of Israel.
\v 32 And \x - \xt Mr. 8, 1.\x* \sc Jesus\sc* called together his Disciples, and said: I pity the multitude because three days now they continue with me, & have not what to eat: and dismiss them fasting I will not, lest they faint in the way.
\v 33 And the Disciples say unto him: whence then may we get so many loaves in the desert as to fill so great a multitude?
\v 34 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: How many loaves have you? But they said: Seven, & a few little fishes.
\v 35 And he commanded the multitude to sit down upon the ground.
\v 36 And taking the Seven loaves & the fishes, and giving thanks, he brake, & gave it to his Disciples, and \f 1 \fr 15.36 \ft Here we see again that the people must not be their own carvers, nor receive the Sacraments or other spiritual sustenance immediately of Christ, or at their own hand, but of their spiritual governers.\f* the Disciples gave to the people.
\v 37 And they did all eat, and had their fill. And that which was left of the fragments they took up, seven baskets full.
\v 38 And there were that did eat, four thousand men, beside children & women.
\v 39 And having dismissed the multitude, he went up into a boat, and came into the coasts of Magedan.
\c 16
\cd The obstinate Pharisees and Sadducees, as though his foresaid miracles were not sufficient to prove him to be Christ, require to see some one from heaven. 5. Whereupon forsaking them, he warneth his disciples to beware of the leaven of their doctrine: 13. and Peter (the time now approaching for him to go into Jewry to his Passion) for confessing him to be Christ, he maketh the Rock of his Church, giving fullness of Ecclesiastical power accordingly. 21. And after, he so rebuketh him for dissuading his Cross and Passion. That he also affirmeth the like suffering in every one to be necessary to salvation.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mar. 8, 12.\x* there came to him the Pharisees and Sadducees tempting: and they demanded him to shew them a sign from Heaven.
\v 2 \x - \xt Luc. 12, 54.\x* But he answered & said to them: When it is evening, you say: It will be fair-weather, for the element is red.
\v 3 And in the morning: This day there will be a tempest, for the element doth glow and lower. The face therefore of the element you have skill to discern: & the signs of times can you not?
\v 4 The \x - \xt Mat. 12, 39.\x* naughty and adulterous Generation seeketh for a sign: and there shall not a sign be given it, but the sign of Jonas the Prophet. And he left them and went away.
\v 5 And \x - \xt Mar. 8, 14.\x* when his Disciples were come over the water, they forgot to take bread.
\v 6 \x - \xt Lu. 12, 1.\x* Who said to them: Look well and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees & Sadducees.
\v 7 But they thought within themselves saying: Because we took not bread.
\v 8 And \sc Jesus\sc* knowing it, said: Why do you think within yourselves, O ye of little faith, for that you have not bread?
\v 9 Do you not yet understand, neither do you remember \x - \xt Mat. 14, 17. 15, 34.\x* the five loaves among five thousand men, and how many baskets you took up?
\v 10 neither the seven loaves, among four thousand men, and how many maunds you took up?
\v 11 Why do you not understand that I said not of bread to you: Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees, & Sadducees?
\v 12 Then they understood that he said not they should beware of the leaven of bread, but of the doctrine of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
\v 13 \x - \xt Mar. 8, 27. Luc. 9, 18.\x* And \f 2 \fr 16.13 \ft The Gospel upon SS. Peter and Paul's day Iun. 29. \+it And in Cathedra Petri Romæ, Ian. 18. & Antiochiæ Febr. 22. And Petri ad vincula Aug. 1.\+it* And on the day of the creation and coronation of the Pope, and on the Anniversary thereof. And upon S. Leo his day April 11.\f* \sc Jesus\sc* came into the quarters of Cæsarea Philippi: and he asked his Disciples, saying: whom say men that the Son of man is? \ef - \fr 16.13 \fq Whom say men. \ft ¹ Christ intending here to take order for the founding, regiment, and stability of his Church after his decease, and to name the person to whom he meant to give the general charge thereof, would before by interrogatories draw out (and namely out of that one whom he thought to make the chief) the profession of that high and principal Article, That he was the son of the living God. Which being the ground of the Church's faith, was a necessary quality and condition in him that was to be made Head of the same Church, and the perpetual keeper of the said faith and all other points thereon depending. \fq ¹ \ft OF PETER'S PRIMACY.\ef*
\v 14 But they said: Some John the Baptist, & othersome Elias, and others Jeremie, or one of the Prophets. \ef - \fr 16.14 \fq But they said. \ft When Christ asked the people's opinion of him, the Apostles all indifferently made answer: but when he demanded what themselves thought of him, then lo Peter the mouth and head of the whole fellowship answered for all. \+it Chrys. ho. 55. in Mat.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: But whom do you say that I am?
\v 16 Simon Peter answered & said: Thou art Christ the Son of the living God.
\v 17 And \sc Jesus\sc* answering, said to him: Blessed art thou Simon Bar-Jona: because flesh & blood hath not revealed it to thee, but my Father which is in Heaven. \ef - \fr 16.17 \fq Blessed art thou. \ft Though some other (as Nathanael \+it Io. 1, 49.\+it* ) seem to have before believed and professed the same thing for which Peter is here counted blessed, yet it may be plainly gathered by this place, and so S. Hilary and others think ¹, that none before this did further utter of him, than that he was the son of God by adoption as other Saints be, though more excellent than other be. For it was of congruity and Christ's special appointment, that he upon whom he intended to found his new Church, and whose faith he would make infallible, should have the preeminence of this first profession of Christ's natural divinity, or, that he was by nature the very son of God; a thing so far above the capacity of nature, reason, flesh and blood, and so repugnant to Peter's sense and sight of Christ's humanity, flesh, and infirmities, that for the belief and public profession thereof he is counted blessed, as Abraham was for his faith: and hath great promises for himself and his posterity, as the said Patriarch had for him and his seed. According as S. Basil saith ², Because he excelled in faith, he received the building of the Church committed to him. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hilar. can. 6. in Mat. & li. 6. de Trinit. Chrys. ho. 55. in Mat.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Basil. li. 2. adv. Eunom.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 And I say to thee: \it That thou art\it* \x - \xt Io. 1, 42. Io. 21, 15.\x*\f 2 \fr 16.18 \ft That is, a Rock.\f* Peter; \it and upon this\it* Rock \it will I build my Church, and the gates of Hell shall not prevail\it* against it. \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq And I say to thee. \ft Our Lord recompenseth Peter for his confession, giving him a great reward, in that upon him he builded his Church. \+it Theophilactus\+it* upon this place.\ef* \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq Thou art Peter. \ft Christ (in the first of John v. 42.) foretold and appointed that this man then named Simon, should afterward be called \+it Cephas\+it*, or \+it Petrus\+it*, that is to say, \+it a Rock\+it*, not then uttering the cause, but now expressing the same, \+it videlicet\+it* (as S. Cyril writeth ¹ ) \+it For that upon him as upon a firm rock his Church should be builded.\+it* Whereunto S. Hilary agreeing saith ², \+it O happy foundation of the Church in the imposing of thy new name. &c.\+it* And yet Christ here doth not so much call him by the name Peter or Rock, as he doth affirm him to be a rock: signifying by that Metaphor, both that he was designed for the foundation and ground work of his house, which is the Church: and also that he should be of invincible force, firmity, durableness, and stability, to sustain all the winds, waves, and storms that might fall or beat against the same. And the Adversaries objecting against this, that Christ only is the Rock or foundation, wrangle against the very express Scriptures and Christ's own words, giving both the name and the thing to this Apostle. And the simple may learn by S. Basil's words ³, how the case standeth. \+it Though\+it* (saith he) \+it Peter be a rock, yet he is not a rock as Christ is. For Christ is the true unmovable rock of himself, Peter is unmovable by Christ the rock. For Jesus doth communicate and impart his dignities, not voiding himself of them, but holding them to himself, bestoweth them also upon others. He is the light, and yet,\+it* ⁴ \+it You are the light: he is the Priest, and yet he\+it* ⁵ \+it maketh Priests: he is the rock, and he made a rock.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Cyr. l. 8. c. 12. Com. in Io.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Hilar. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Basil. li. de poenit.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it (2) Mt. 5, 14.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft (3) Luc. 22, 19.\ef* \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq And upon this rock. \ft ¹ Upon that which he said Peter was, will he build his Church: and therefore by most evident sequel he foundeth his Church upon Peter. And the Adversaries wrangling against this, do against their own conscience and knowledge: specially seeing they know and confess that in Christ's words speaking in the Syriac tongue, there was no difference at all between \+it Petrus\+it* and \+it Petra\+it*: yea and that the Greek ² words also though differing in termination, yet signify one thing, to wit, \+it a rock\+it*, or \+it stone\+it*, as themselves also translate it. \+it Io. 1, 42.\+it* So that they which profess to follow the Hebrew or Syriac and the Greek, and to translate immediately out of them into Latin or English, should if they had dealt sincerely, have thus turned Christ's words, \+it Thou art a rock, and upon this rock:\+it* or, \+it Thou art Peter, and upon this peter will I build my Church:\+it* For so Christ spake by their own confession without any difference. Which doth expressly stop them of all their vain evasions, that \+it Petrus\+it* the former word is referred to the Apostle: and \+it petra\+it* the later word, either to Christ only, or to Peter's faith only: neither the said original tongues bearing it, nor the sequel of the words, \+it upon this\+it*, suffering any relation in the world but to that which was spoken of in the same sentence next before: neither the words following which are directly addressed to Peter's person, nor Christ's intention by any means admitting it, which was not to make himself or to promise himself to be the head or foundation of the Church. For his Father gave him that dignity, and he took not that honour to himself, nor sent himself, nor took the keys of heaven of himself, but all of his Father. He had his commission the very hour of his incarnation. And though S. Augustine ³ sometimes refer the word \+it (Petra)\+it* to Christ in this sentence (which no doubt he did because the terminations in Latin are diverse, and because he examined not the nature of the original words which Christ spake, nor of the Greek, and therefore the Adversaries which otherwise flee to the tongues, should not in this case allege him) yet he never denieth but Peter also is the Rock and head of the Church, saying that himself expoundeth it of Peter ⁴ in many places, and allegeth also S. Ambrose for the same in his hymn which the Church singeth. And so do we allege the holy Councel of Chalcedon, \+it Act. 3. pag. 118. Tertullian, de præscript.\+it* Origen, \+it Ho. 5. in Exo.\+it* S. Cyprian, \+it De unit. Ec.\+it* S. Hilary, \+it Can. 16. in Mat.\+it* S. Ambrose, \+it Ser. 47. 68. li. 6. in c. 9. Lucæ.\+it* S. Hierom, \+it Li. 1. in Iovin. & in c. 2. Esa. & in c. 16. Hier.\+it* S. Epiphanius, \+it In Anchor.\+it* S. Chrysostom, \+it Ho. 55. in Mat.\+it* S. Cyril, \+it Li. 2. c. 12. com. in Io.\+it* S. Leo, \+it Ep. 89.\+it* S. Gregory, \+it Li. 4. ep. 32. ind. 13.\+it* ⁵ and others: every one of them saying expressly that the Church was founded and builded upon Peter. For though sometimes they say the Church to be builded on Peter's faith, yet they mean not (as our Adversaries do unlearnedly take them) that it should be builded upon faith either separated from the man, or in any other man: but upon faith as in him who here confessed that faith. \fq ¹ \ft Thou art \+it Cephas\+it*, and upon this \+it Cephas\+it*. \fq ² \ft πέτρος. │rock. πέτρα. ┘ \fq ³ \ft \+it Aug. li. 1. retr. c. 21.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it In Psal. 66. De verb. Do. sec. Io. ser. 49. ser. 15. 16. 26. 29. de Sanctis. Annot. in Iob. c. 30.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Theodor. li. 5. hær. Fabul. c. de poenit.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq Rock. \ft The Adversaries hearing also the Fathers sometimes say, that Peter had these promises and prerogatives, as bearing the person of all the Apostles or of the whole Church, deny absurdly that himself in person had these prerogatives. As though Peter had been the proctor only of the Church or of the Apostles, confessing the faith and receiving these things in other men's names. Where the holy Doctors mean only, that these prerogatives were not given to him for his own use, but for the good of the whole Church, and to be imparted to every vocation according to the measure of their callings: and that these great privileges given to Peter should not decay or die with his person, but be perpetual in the Church in his successors. Therefore S. Hierom ¹ to Damasus taketh this Rock not to be Peter's person only, but his successors and his Chair. \+it I\+it* (saith he) \+it following no chief or principal but Christ, join myself to the communion of Peter's chair, upon that rock I know the Church was built.\+it* And of that same Apostolic Chair S. August. saith ², \+it That same is the Rock which the proud gates of Hell do not overcome.\+it* And S. Leo, \+it Our Lord would the Sacrament or mystery of this gift so to pertain unto the office of all the Apostles, that he placed it principally in blessed S. Peter the chief of all the Apostles, that from him as from a certain head he might pour out his gifts, as it were through the whole body: that he might understand himself to be an alien from the divine mystery that should presume to revolt from the solidity or steadfastness of Peter.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hiero. ep. 7. to. 2.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Psal. cont. part. Donat. to. 7. Leo ep. 89.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq Build my Church. \ft The Church or house of Christ was only promised here to be builded upon him (which was fulfilled. \+it Io. 21, 15.\+it* ) the foundation stone and other pillars or matter being yet in preparing, and Christ himself being not only the supereminent founder but also the founder of the same: which is another more excellent quality than was in Peter, for which he called it \+it my Church\+it*: meaning specially the Church of the new Testament. Which was not perfectly formed and finished, and distincted from the Synagogue till Whitsunday, though Christ gave Peter and the rest their commissions actually before his Ascension.\ef* \ef - \fr 16.18 \fq Gates of hell. \ft Because the Church is resembled to a house or a city, the adversary powers also be likened to a contrary house or town, the gates whereof, that is to say, the fortitude or impugnations shall never prevail against the city of Christ. And so by this promise we are assured that no heresies nor other wicked attempts can prevail against the Church builded upon Peter, which the Fathers call Peter's see and the Roman Church. \+it Count\+it* (saith S. Augustine ¹ ) \+it the Priests from the very See of Peter, and in that order of Fathers consider who to whom hath succeeded, that same is the rock which the proud gates of Hell do not overcome.\+it* And in another place ², \+it that is it which hath obtained the top of authority, Heretics in vain barking round about it.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Psal. cont. part. Donati.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it De util. cred. c. 17.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 \it And I will give to thee the keys of the Kingdom of Heaven. And whatsoever thou shalt bind upon earth, it shall be bound also in the Heavens: and whatsoever thou shalt loose in earth, it shall be loosed also in the Heavens.\it* \ef - \fr 16.19 \fq To thee. \ft In saying, \+it to thee will I give\+it*, it is plain that as he gave the keys to him, so he builded the Church upon him. So saith S. Cyprian ¹, \+it To Peter first of all, upon whom our Lord built the Church, and from whom he instituted and shewed the beginning of unity, did he give this power, that that should be loosed in the heavens, which he had loosed in earth.\+it* Whereby appeareth the vain cavil of our Adversaries, which say the Church was built upon Peter's Confession only, common to him and the rest, and not upon his person, more than upon the rest. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Cyp. Epist. 73. Greg. l. 4. ep. 32. ind. 12.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 16.19 \fq The Keys. \ft ¹ That is, The authority or Chair of doctrine, knowledge, judgement and discretion between true and false doctrine: the height of government, the power of making laws, of calling Councels, of the principal voice in them, of confirming them, of making Canons and wholesome decrees, of abrogating the contrary, of ordaining Bishops and Pastors or deposing and suspending them, finally the power to dispense the goods of the Church both spiritual and temporal. Which signification of preeminent power and authority by the word \+it keys\+it* the Scripture expresseth in many places: namely speaking of Christ, ² \+it I have the keys of death and Hell, that is, the rule\+it*. And again, ³ \+it I will give the key of the house of David upon his shoulder.\+it* Moreover it signifieth that men cannot come into heaven but by him, the keys signifying also authority to open and shut, as it it said \+it Apoc. 3.\+it* of Christ, \+it Who hath the key of David, he shutteth and no man openeth.\+it* By which words we gather that Peter's authority is marvelous, to whom the keys, that is, the power to open and shut heaven, is given. And therefore by the name of keys is given that supereminent power which is called in comparison of the power granted to other Apostles, Bishops and Pastors, \+it plenitudo potestatis,\+it* fullness of power. \+it Bernard. lib. 2. de considerat. c. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The dignities of the keys. \fq ² \ft \+it Apoc. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Esa. 22, 22.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 16.19 \fq Whatsoever thou shall bind. \ft All kind of discipline and punishment of offenders, either spiritual (which directly is here meant) or corporal so far as it tendeth to the execution of the spiritual charge, is comprised under the word, bind. Of which sort be Excommunications, Anathematisms, Suspensions, degradations, and other censures and penalties or penances enjoined either in the Sacrament of Confession or in the exterior Courts of the Church, for punishment both of other crimes, and specially of heresy and rebellion against the Church and the chief pastors thereof.\ef* \ef - \fr 16.19 \fq Loose. \ft To loose, is as the cause and the offenders' case requireth, to loose them of any the former bands, and to restore them to the Church's Sacraments and Communion of the faithful and execution of their function, to pardon also either all, or part of the penances enjoined, or what debts so ever man oweth to God or the Church for the satisfaction of his sins forgiven. Which kind of releasing or loosing is called \+it Indulgence\+it*: finally this \+it whatsoever\+it*, excepteth nothing that is punishable or pardonable by Christ in earth, for he hath committed his power to Peter. And so the validity of Peter's sentence in binding or loosing whatsoever, shall by Christ's promise be ratified in heaven. \+it Leo Ser. de Transfig. & Ser. 2. in annivers. assumpt. ad Pontif. Hilar. can. 16. in Matth. Epiph. in Anchorato prope initium\+it*. If now any temporal power can shew their warrant out of Scripture for such sovereign power, as is here given to Peter and consequently to his successors, by these words, \+it whatsoever thou shall bind,\+it* and by the very keys, whereby greatest sovereignty is signified in God's Church as in his family and household, and therefore principally attributed and given to Christ ¹ who in the scripture is said to have the key of David, but here communicated also unto Peter, as the name of Rock: if I say any temporal potestate can shew authority for the like sovereignty, let them challenge hardly to be head not only of one particular, but of the whole universal Church. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Esa. 22, Apoc. 3.\+it*\ef*
\v 20 Then he commanded his Disciples, that they should tell nobody that he was \sc Jesus Christ\sc*.
\v 21 From that time \sc Jesus\sc* began to shew his Disciples, that he must go to Jerusalem, & suffer many things of the Ancients & Scribes and Chief-Priests, and be killed, and the third day rise again.
\v 22 And Peter taking him unto him, began to rebuke him, saying: Lord, be it far from thee, this shall not be unto thee.
\v 23 Who turning said to Peter: Go after me \f 1 \fr 16.23 \ft This word in Hebrew signifieth an adversary, as \+it 3. Reg.\+it*\f* Satan, thou art a scandal unto me: because thou savourest not the things that are of God, but the things that are of men.
\v 24 \f 1 \fr 16.24 \ft THE Gospel for a Martyr that is a bishop; And upon S. Laurence Eve.\f* Then \sc Jesus\sc* said to his Disciples: If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.
\v 25 For he that will save his life, shall lose it, and he that shall lose his life for me, shall find it.
\v 26 For what doth it profit a man, if he gain the whole world, and sustain the damage of his soul? Or what permutation shall a man give for his soul?
\v 27 For the Son of man shall come in the glory of his Father with his Angels: and then will he render to every man according to his works. \ef - \fr 16.27 \fq Works. \ft ¹ He saith not, to give every man according to his mercy (or their faith) but according to their works. \+it August. de verb. Apost. Ser. 35.\+it* And Again, How should our Saviour reward every one according to their works, if there were no free will? ² \+it August. lib. 2. cap. 4. 5. 8. de act. cum Foelic. Manich.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Good works. \fq ² \ft Freewill.\ef*
\v 28 Amen I say to you, \x - \xt Mar. 9, 1. Luc. 9, 27.\x* there be some of them that stand here, that shall not taste death, till they see the Son of man coming in his Kingdom.
\c 17
\cd As he promised, he giveth them a sight of the glory, unto which suffering doth bring: 9 and then again doth inculcate his Passion. 14 A devil also he casteth out which his Disciples could not for their incredulity and lack of praying and fasting, 22 being yet in Galilee, he revealeth more about his Passion. 24 and the tribute that the Collectors exacted for all, he payeth for himself and Peter: declaring yet withal his freedom both by word and miracle.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 9, 2. Luc. 9, 28.\x*\f 2 \fr 17.1 \ft The Transfiguration of our Lord, celebrated in the Church the 6. of Aug. The Gospel of the said feast, & of the 2. Sunday in Lent: and on Imber Saturday before.\f* after six days \sc Jesus\sc* taketh unto him Peter, and James, & John his brother, & bringeth them into a high mountain apart:
\v 2 \x - \xt 2. Pet. 1, 17.\x* And he was transfigured before them. And his face did shine as the sun: & his garments became white as snow. \ef - \fr 17.2 \fq Transfigured. \ft ¹ Mark in this Transfiguration many marvelous points, as, that he made not only his own body, which then was mortal, but also the bodies of Moyses and Elias, the one dead, the other to die, for the time as it were immortal: thereby to represent the state and glory of his body and his Saints in heaven. By which marvelous transfiguring of his body, you may the less marvel that he can exhibit his body under the form of bread and wine or otherwise as he list. \fq ¹ \ft Christ can exhibit his body under what form he list.\ef*
\v 3 And behold there appeared to them Moyses and Elias talking with him. \ef - \fr 17.3 \fq Appeared Moyses. \ft ¹ By this that Moyses personally appeared and was present with Christ, it is plain that the Saints departed may in person be present at the affairs of the living. \+it August. de cura pro mort. c. 15. 16.\+it* For even as Angels elsewhere, so here the Saints also served our Saviour: and therefore as Angels both in the old Testament and the new, were present often at the affairs of men, so many Saints. \fq ¹ \ft Saints after their death deal with and for the living.\ef*
\v 4 And Peter answering, said to \sc Jesus\sc*: Lord, it is good for us to be here: if thou wilt, let us make here three tabernacles, one for thee, and one for Moyses, and one for Elias.
\v 5 And as he was yet speaking, behold a bright cloud overshadowed them. And lo a voice out of the cloud, saying: This is my wellbeloved Son, in whom I am well pleased: hear ye him.
\v 6 And the Disciples hearing it, fell upon their face, and were sore afraid.
\v 7 And \sc Jesus\sc* came and touched them: and he said to them: Arise, and fear not.
\v 8 And they lifting up their eyes, saw nobody, but only \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 9 And as they descended from the mount, \sc Jesus\sc* commanded them, saying: Tell the vision to nobody, till the Son of man be risen from the dead. \ef - \fr 17.9 \fq Mount. \ft ¹ This mount (commonly esteemed and named of the ancient Fathers Thabor) S. Peter ² calleth \+it the holy Mount\+it* because of this wonderful vision, like as in the old Testament where God appeared to Moyses in the bush, and elsewhere to others, he calleth the place of such Apparitions, \+it holy ground\+it* ³. Whereby it is evident that by such Apparitions, places are sanctified, and thereupon groweth a religion and devotion ⁴ in the faithful toward such places, and namely to this Mount Thabor (called in S. Hierom \+it Itabirium Ep. 17.\+it* ) there was great Pilgrimage in the Primitive Church, as unto all those places which our Saviour had sanctified with his presence and miracles, and therefore to the whole land of promise, for that cause called the holy land ⁵. \+it See. S. Hiero. in Epitap. Paulæ. & ep. 17. & 18. ad Marcellam.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Holy places. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Pet. 1, 18.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Exo. 3, 5.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Devotion and Pilgrimage to the same. \fq ⁵ \ft The holy land.\ef*
\v 10 And his Disciples asked him, saying: what say the Scribes then, that \x - \xt Mal. 4, 5.\x* Elias must come first?
\v 11 But he answering, said to them: Elias indeed shall come, and restore all things. \ef - \fr 17.11 \fq Elias shall come. \ft He distinguisheth here plainly between ¹ Elias in person, who is yet to come before the judgement: and between Elias in name, to wit, John the Baptist, who is come already in the spirit and virtue of Elias ², So that it is not John Baptist only nor principally of whom Malachie prophesieth (as our Adversaries say) but Elias also himself in person. \fq ¹ \ft Elias. \fq ² \ft \+it Luc. 1, 17. Mal. 4, 5.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 And I say to you, that Elias is already come, and they did not know him, but wrought on him whatsoever they would. So also the Son of man shall suffer of them.
\v 13 Then the Disciples understood, that of John the Baptist he had spoken to them.
\v 14 And \x - \xt Mar. 9, 14. Luc. 9, 37.\x* when he was come unto the multitude, there came to him a man falling down upon his knees before him, saying: Lord have mercy upon my Son, for he is lunatic, and sore vexed: for he falleth often into the fire, and often into the water.
\v 15 And I offered him to thy Disciples, and they could not cure him.
\v 16 \sc Jesus\sc* answered and said: O faithless and perverse generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring him hither to me.
\v 17 And \sc Jesus\sc* rebuked him, and the devil went out of him, and the lad was cured from that hour.
\v 18 Then came the Disciples to \sc Jesus\sc* secretly, and said: Why could we not cast him out? \ef - \fr 17.18 \fq Why could we not. \ft ¹ No marvel if the Exorcists of the Catholic Church which have power to cast out devils, yet do it not always when they will, and many times with much a do: whereas the Apostles having received this power ² before over unclean spirits, yet here cannot cast them out. But as for heretics, they can never do it, nor any other true miracle, to confirm their false faith. \fq ¹ \ft True miracles only in the Cath. Church. \fq ² \ft \+it Mt. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 \sc Jesus\sc* said to them, because of your incredulity: For, Amen I say to you, if you have faith as a mustard seed, you shall say to this mountain, Remove from thence thither, and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible to you. \ef - \fr 17.19 \fq Faith as a mustard seed. \ft This is the Catholic faith, by which only all miracles are wrought: yet not of every one that hath the Catholic faith, but of such as have a great and forcible faith, and withal the gift of miracles. These are able as here we see by Christ's warrant not only to do other wonderful miracles here signified by this one, but also this very same, that is, to move mountains indeed, as S. Paul ¹ also presupposeth, and S. Hierom ² affirmeth, and Ecclesiastical histories namely telleth of ³ Gregorius Neocæsariensis, that he moved a mountain to make room for the foundation of a Church, called therefore and for other his wonderful miracles, Thaumaturgus. And yet faithless Heretics laugh at all such things and believe them not. \fq ¹ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 13.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Hiero in vita S. Hilarionis. Niceph. li. 6. c. 17. Greg. Niss. de vit. Gregory.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Gregorius Thaumaturgus.\ef*
\v 20 But this kind is not cast out but by prayer and fasting. \ef - \fr 17.20 \fq Prayer and fasting. \ft ¹ The force of fasting and praying: whereby also we may see that the holy Church in Exorcisms doth according to the Scriptures, when she useth beside the name of \+sc Jesus\+sc*, many prayers and much fasting to drive out Devils, because these also are here required beside faith. \fq ¹ \ft Prayer and Fasting.\ef*
\v 21 And \x - \xt Mr. 9, 31. Luc. 9, 44.\x* when they conversed in Galilee, \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: The Son of man is to be betrayed into the hands of men:
\v 22 and they shall kill him, and the third day he shall rise again. And they were stroken sad exceedingly.
\v 23 And when they were come to Capharnaum, there came they that received the didrachmes, unto Peter, and said to him: Your master doth he not pay the \f 1 \fr 17.23 \ft These didrachmes were pieces of money which they payed for tribute.\f* didrachmes?
\v 24 He saith, Yes. And when he was entered into the house, \sc Jesus\sc* prevented him, saying: What is thy opinion Simon? The kings of the earth, of whom receive they tribute or cense? Of their children, or of strangers?
\v 25 And he said: Of strangers. \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Then the children are free. \ef - \fr 17.25 \fq The Children free. \ft Though Christ to avoid scandal, payed tribute, yet indeed he sheweth that both himself ought to be free from such payments (as being the king's son, as well by his eternal birth of God the Father, as temporal of David) and also his Apostles, as being of his family, and in them their successors the whole Clergy, who are called in Scripture the lot and portion of our Lord. ¹ Which exemption and privilege being grounded upon the very law of nature itself, and therefore practised even among the Heathen \+it (Gen. 42, 27.)\+it* good Christian Princes have confirmed and ratified by their laws, in the honour of Christ, whose ministers they are, and as it were the king's sons, as S. Hierom declareth plainly in these words, \+it We for his honour pay not tributes, and as the King's sons, are free from such payments. Hiero.\+it* upon this place. \fq ¹ \ft The privileges and exemptions of the Clergy.\ef*
\v 26 But that we may not scandalize them, go thy ways to the sea, and cast a hook: and that fish which shall first come up, take: and when thou hast opened his mouth, thou shalt find a \f 1 \fr 17.26 \ft This stater was a double didrachme, and therefore was payed for two.\f* stater: take that, and give it them for me and thee. \ef - \fr 17.26 \fq Me and thee. \ft ¹ A great mystery in that he payed not only for himself, but for Peter bearing the Person of the Church, and in whom as the chief, the rest were contained. \+it Aug. q. ex no. Test. q. 75. to. 4.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Peter's preeminence.\ef*
\c 18
\cd To his Disciples he preacheth against ambition the mother of Schism: 7. foretelling both the author whosoever he be, and also his followers, of their woe to come. 10. and shewing on the contrary side, how precious Christian souls are to their Angels, to the son of man, and to his Father. 15. charging us therefore to forgive our brethren, when also we have just cause against them, be it never so often, and to labour their salvation by all means possible.
\p
\v 1 AT \x - \xt Mr. 9, 34. Luc. 9, 46.\x*\f 2 \fr 18.1 \ft The Gospel on Michaelmas day Septemb. 29. And upon his Apparition. Maij 8.\f* that hour the Disciples came to \sc Jesus\sc*, saying: Who, thinkest thou, is the greater in the Kingdom of Heaven? \ef - \fr 18.1 \fq Who is the greater. \ft The occasion of this question and of their contention for Superiority among the rest of their infirmities which they had before the coming of the Holy Ghost, was (as certain holy Doctors write) upon emulation toward Peter, whom only they saw preferred before the rest in the payment of the tribute ¹, by these words of our Saviour, Give it them for me and thee. \+it Chrys. ho. 59. Hiero. in Mat.\+it* Upon this place. \fq ¹ \ft \+it C. 17, v. 27.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 And \sc Jesus\sc* calling unto him a little child, set him in the midst of them,
\v 3 and said: Amen I say to you, unless you be converted, and become as little children, you shall not enter into the Kingdom of Heaven.
\v 4 Whosoever therefore shall humble himself as this \f 1 \fr 18.4 \ft Humility, innocency, simplicity, commended to us in the state and person of a child.\f* little child, he is the greater in the Kingdom of Heaven.
\v 5 And he that shall receive one such little child in my name, receiveth me.
\v 6 And \x - \xt Mr. 9, 42. Lu. 17, 2.\x* he that shall scandalize one of these little ones that believe in me, it is expedient for him that a millstone be hanged about his neck, and that he be drowned in the depth of the sea.
\v 7 Woe be to the world for scandals. For it is necessary that scandals do come: but nevertheless woe to that man by whom the scandal cometh. \ef - \fr 18.7 \fq Scandals. \ft The simple be most annoyed by taking scandal of their preachers, Priests, and elders ill life: and great damnation is to the guides of the people, whether they be temporal or spiritual, but specially to the spiritual, if by their ill example and scandalous life, the people be scandalized.\ef*
\v 8 And \x - \xt Mt. 5, 30. Mar. 9, 43.\x* if thy hand, or thy foot scandalize thee, cut it off, and cast it from thee. It is good for thee to go into life maimed or lame, rather than having two hands or two feet, to be cast into everlasting fire. \ef - \fr 18.8 \fq Hand, foot, eye. \ft By these parts of the body so necessary and profitable for a man, is signified, that whatsoever is nearest and dearest to us, wife, children, friends, riches, all are to be contemned and forsaken for to save our soul.\ef*
\v 9 And if thine eye scandalize thee, pluck him out, and cast him from thee: It is good for thee having one eye to enter into life, rather than having two eyes to be cast into the Hell of fire.
\v 10 See that you despise not one of these little ones: for I say to you, that their Angels in Heaven always do see the face of my Father which is in Heaven. \ef - \fr 18.10 \fq Their Angels. \ft ¹ A great dignity and a marvelous benefit that every one hath from his Nativity an Angel for his custody and Patronage against the Wicked, before the face of God. \+it Hiero. upon this place.\+it* And the thing is so plain, that Calvin dare not deny it, and yet he will needs doubt of it, \+it lib. 1. Inst. c. 14. sect. 7.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Protection of Angels.\ef*
\v 11 For \x - \xt Luc. 19, 10.\x* the Son of man is come to save that which was perished.
\v 12 \x - \xt Lu. 15, 4.\x* How think you? If a man have an hundred sheep, and one of them shall go astray; doth he not leave ninety nine in the mountains, and goeth to seek that which is strayed?
\v 13 And if it chance that he find it: Amen I say to you, that he rejoiceth more for that, than for the ninety nine that went not astray.
\v 14 Even so it is not the will of your Father, which is in Heaven, that one perish of these little ones.
\v 15 \x - \xt Luc. 17, 3.\x* But \f 2 \fr 18.15 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday the 3 week in Lent.\f* if thy brother shall offend against thee, go, and rebuke him between thee and him alone. If he shall hear thee, thou shalt gain thy brother.
\v 16 And if he will not hear thee, join with thee besides, one or two: that in the mouth of \x - \xt Deu. 19, 15.\x* two or three witnesses every word may stand.
\v 17 And if he will not hear them, \f 1 \fr 18.17 \ft That is (as S. Chrysostom here expoundeth it) tell the Prelates and chief Pastors of the Church: for they have jurisdiction to bind and loose such offenders, by the words following v. 18.\f* tell the Church. \it And if he will not hear the Church, let him be to thee as the Heathen and the Publican.\it* \ef - \fr 18.17 \fq Not hear the Church. \ft ¹ Not only Heretics, but any other obstinate offender that will not be judged nor ruled by the Church, may be excommunicated, and so made as an Heathen or publican was to the Jews, by the discipline of the same, casting him out of the fellowship of Catholics. Which ² Excommunication is a greater punishment than if he were executed by sword, fire, and wild beasts. \+it Aug. cont. Adv. leg. li. 1. c. 17.\+it* And again he saith, Man is more sharply and pitifully bound by the Church's Keys, than with any iron or adamantine manacles or fetters in the world. \+it August. ibidem.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Disobedience to the Church. \fq ² \ft Excommunication.\ef* \ef - \fr 18.17 \fq Heathen. \ft Heretics therefore because they will not hear the Church, be no better nor no otherwise to be esteemed of Catholics, than heathen men and Publicans were esteemed among the Jews.\ef*
\v 18 Amen I say to you, whatsoever you shall bind upon earth, shall be bound also in Heaven: and whatsoever you shall loose upon earth, shall be loosed also in Heaven. \ef - \fr 18.18 \fq You shall bind. \ft ¹ As before he gave this power of binding and loosing over the whole, first of all and principally to Peter, upon whom he builded his Church: so here not only to Peter ², and in him to his successors, but also to the other Apostles, and in them to their successors, every one in their charge. \+it Hierom. lib. 1. c. 14. advers. Iovin. and Epist. ad Heliod. Cyprian. de unit. Eccl. nu. 3.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Power to bind and loose. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. c. 16, 19.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 18.18 \fq Shall loose. \ft Our Lord giveth no less right and authority to the Church to loose, than to bind, as S. Ambrose ¹ writeth against the Novatians, who confessed that the Priests had power to bind, but not to loose. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Li. 1. de poenit. c. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 Again I say to you, that if two of you shall \f 1 \fr 18.19 \ft All joining together in the unity of Christ's Church in Councels and Synods, or public prayers, is of more force than of any particular man.\f* consent upon earth, concerning every thing whatsoever they shall ask, it shall be done to them of my Father which is in Heaven.
\v 20 For where there be two or three gathered in my name, there am I in the midst of them. \ef - \fr 18.20 \fq In the middes of them. \ft ¹ Not all assemblies may challenge the presence of Christ, but only such as be gathered together in the unity of the Church. And therefore no conventicles of Heretics directly gathering against the Church, are warranted by this place. \+it Cypr. de unit. Eccles. nu. 7. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Catholic Assemblies.\ef*
\v 21 Then came Peter unto him and said: \x - \xt Lu. 17, 4.\x* Lord, how often shall my brother offend against me, & I forgive him? Until seven times?
\v 22 \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: I say not to thee \x - \xt Luc. 17, 4.\x* until seven times but until seventy times seven times. \ef - \fr 18.22 \fq Seventy times seven. \ft There must be no end of forgiving them that be penitent, either in the Sacrament by absolution, or one man another their offences.\ef*
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 18.23 \ft The Gospel upon the 21. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Therefore is the Kingdom of Heaven likened to a man being a king, that would make an account with his servants.
\v 24 And when he began to make the account, there was one presented unto him that owed him ten thousand talents.
\v 25 And having not whence to repay it, his Lord commanded that he should be sold, and his wife and children, and all that he had, and it to be repayed.
\v 26 But that servant falling down, besought him, saying: Have patience toward me, and I will repay thee all,
\v 27 And the Lord of that servant moved with pity, dismissed him, and the debt he forgave him.
\v 28 And when that servant was gone forth, he found one of his fellow-servants that did owe him an hundred pence: and laying hands upon him throttled him, saying: Repay that thou owest.
\v 29 And his fellow-servant falling down, besought him, saying: Have patience toward me, and I will repay thee all.
\v 30 And he would not: but went his way, and cast him into prison, till he repayed the debt.
\v 31 And his fellow-servants seeing what was done, were very sorry, and they came, and told their Lord all that was done.
\v 32 Then his Lord called him; and he said unto him: Thou ungracious servant, I forgave thee all the debt, because thou besoughtest me:
\v 33 oughtest not thou therefore also to have mercy upon thy fellow-servant, even as I had mercy upon thee?
\v 34 And his Lord being angry delivered him to the tormenters, until he repayed all the debt.
\v 35 So also shall my Heavenly Father do to you, if you forgive not every one his brother from your hearts.
\c 19
\cd He answereth the tempting Pharisees, that the ease of a man with his wife shall be in the first institution it was) utterly indissoluble, though for one cause he may be divorced. 10. And thereupon to his Disciples he highly commendeth Single life for heaven. 13. He will have children come unto him. 16 He sheweth what is to be done to enter into life everlasting: 20. What also, for a rich man to be perfect: 27. As also what passing reward they shall have which follow that his counsel of perfection: 29. yea though it be but in some one piece. \f - \ft The fourth part of this Gospel, Christ's coming into Jury toward his Passion.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 10, 1.\x* it came to pass, when \sc Jesus\sc* had ended these words, he departed from Galilee, and came into the coasts of Jury beyond Jordan,
\v 2 and great multitudes followed him; and he cured them there.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 19.3 \ft The Gospel for Marriage, and upon S. Agatha's day. Feb. 5.\f* And there came to him the Pharisees tempting him, and saying: Is it lawful for a man to dismiss his wife for every cause?
\v 4 Who answering, said to them: Have ye not read, that he which did \x - \xq make \xt Gen. 1, 27.\x* make from the beginning, \f 2 \fr 19.4 \ft \+it make man\+it*\f* \it made them male & female?\it* And he said:
\v 5 \x - \xt Gen. 2, 24.\x* \it For this cause, man shall leave father & mother, & shall cleave to his wife, and they two shall be in one flesh.\it*
\v 6 Therefore now they are not two, but one flesh. That therefore which God hath joined together, let not man separate. \ef - \fr 19.6 \fq Not man separate. \ft This inseparability betwixt man and wife riseth of that, that wedlock is a Sacrament. \+it Aug. li. 2. de pec. origine c. 34. to. 7. De nupt. & concupis. li. 1. c. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 7 They say to him: Why then \x - \xt Deut. 24, 1.\x* did Moyses command to give a bill of divorce, and to dismiss her?
\v 8 He saith to them: Because Moyses for the hardness of your heart permitted you to dismiss your wives: but from the beginning it was not so.
\v 9 And I say to you, that \x - \xt Mt. 5, 32. Mr. 10, 11. Luc. 16, 18. 1. Cor. 7, 11.\x* whosoever shall dismiss his wife, but for fornication, and shall marry another, doth commit adultery: and he that shall marry her that is dismissed, committeth adultery. \ef - \fr 19.9 \fq But for fornication. \ft For adultery one may dismiss another, \+it Matt. 5.\+it* ¹ But neither party can marry again for any cause during life. \+it Aug. li. 11. de adult. coniug. c. 21. 22. 24.\+it* For the which unlawful act of marrying again, Fabiola that noble matron of Rome albeit she was the innocent part, did public penance, as S. Hierom ² writeth in her high commendation therefore. And in S. Paul \+it Ro. 7.\+it* it is plain that she which is with another man, her husband yet living, shall be called an adultress: contrary to the doctrine of our Adversaries. \fq ¹ \ft Marriage after divorce unlawful. \fq ² \ft \+it In Epitaph. Fabiola.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 His Disciples say unto him: If the case of a man with his wife be so, it is not expedient to marry.
\v 11 Who said to them: Not all \f 1 \fr 19.11 \ft \+it χωροῦσι.\+it* capiunt.\f* take this word, but they to whom it is given. \ef - \fr 19.11 \fq Not all take. \ft Whosoever have not this gift given them, it is either for that they will not have it, or for that they fulfil not that which they will: and they that have this gift or attain to this word, have it of God and their own free will. \+it Aug. li. de grat. & lib. arbit. c. 4.\+it* So that it is evident no man is excluded from this gift, but (as Origen ¹ here saith) it is given to all that ask for it: contrary to our Adversaries that say it is impossible, and that for excuse of breaking their vows, wickedly say, they have not the gift. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Orig. tract. 7. in Mat.\+it*\ef*
\v 12 For there are Eunuchs which were born so from their mother's womb: and there are Eunuchs, which were made by men: and there are Eunuchs, which have gelded themselves for the Kingdom of Heaven. He that can take, let him take. \ef - \fr 19.12 \fq Gelded themselves. \ft ¹ They geld themselves for the kingdom of heaven which vow chastity. \+it Aug. de virginitate c. 24.\+it* Which proveth those kind of vows to be both lawful, and also more meritorious, and more sure to obtain life everlasting, than the state of wedlock, contrary to our Adv. in all respects. \fq ¹ \ft Vow of chastity.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.12 \fq He that can. \ft ¹ ² It is not said of the Precepts, keep them who can, for they be necessary under pain of damnation to be kept: but of Counsels only (as of virginity, abstaining from flesh and wine, and of giving all a man's goods away to the poor) it is said, He that can attain to it, let him do it: which is counsel only, not a commandment. Contrary to our Adv. that say, there are no Counsels, but only precepts. \fq ¹ \ft Counsels not Precepts. \fq ² \ft \+it Aug. ser. 6. de temp.\+it*\ef*
\v 13 Then \x - \xt Mr. 10, 13. Luc. 18, 15.\x* were little children presented to him, that he should impose hands upon them & pray. And the Disciples rebuked them. \ef - \fr 19.13 \fq Impose. \ft ¹ They knew the value of Christ's blessing, and therefore brought their children to him: as good Christian people have at all times brought their children to Bishops to have their blessing. See Annotation before \+it Chap. 10. 12.\+it* And of Religious men's blessing see Ruffin. \+it li. 2. c. 8. hist.\+it* S. Hierom \+it in Epitaph. Paulæ c. 7. & in vit. Hilarionis. Theodoret. in historia sanctorum Patrum num. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Bishops and Religious men's blessing.\ef*
\v 14 But \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Suffer the little children, and stay them not from coming unto me: for the Kingdom of Heaven is for such.
\v 15 And when he had imposed hands upon them, he departed from thence.
\v 16 And \x - \xt Mr. 10, 17. Luc. 18, 18.\x* behold one came and said to him: Good Master, what good shall I do that I may have life everlasting?
\v 17 Who said to him: What asketh thou me of good? One is good, God. But \f 1 \fr 19.17 \ft Isee not (saith S. Augustine) why Christ should say, if thou wilt have life everlasting, keep the commandments: if without observing of them, by only faith one might be saved. \+it Aug. de Fid. & op. c. 15.\+it*\f* if thou wilt enter into life, keep the commandments.
\v 18 He saith to him, which? And \sc Jesus\sc* said: \x - \xt Exo. 20, 13.\x* \it Thou shalt not murder, Thou shalt not commit adultery, Thou shalt not steal, Thou shalt not bear false witness.\it*
\v 19 \it Honour thy father & thy mother, \it* \x - \xt * Lev. 19, 18.\x* \it Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.\it*
\v 20 The young man saith to him: All these have I kept from my youth; what is yet wanting unto me?
\v 21 \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: If thou wilt be perfect, go, sell the things that thou hast, & give to the poor, and thou shalt have treasure in Heaven: and come, follow me. \ef - \fr 19.21 \fq If thou wilt be perfect. \ft ¹ Lo, he maketh a plain difference between keeping the commandments, which is necessary for every man: and being perfect, which he counseleth only to them that will. And this is the state of great perfection which Religious men do profess, according to Christ's counsel here, leaving all things and following him. \fq ¹ \ft The Religious state of perfection.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.21 \fq Follow me. \ft Thus to follow Christ is to be without wife and care of children, to lack propriety, and to live in common, and this hath great reward in heaven above other states of life: which, S. Augustine saith, the Apostles followed, and himself, and that he exhorted others to it as much as lay in him. \+it Aug. ep. 89. in fine, & in Ps. 103. Conc. 3. post med.\+it*\ef*
\v 22 And when the young man had heard this word, he went away sad: for he had many possessions.
\v 23 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to his Disciples: Amen I say to you, that a rich man shall hardly enter into the Kingdom of Heaven.
\v 24 And again I say to you, it is easier for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle, \f 1 \fr 19.24 \ft S. Mark expoundeth it thus, rich men trusting in their riches. \+it ca. 10, 24.\+it*\f* than for a rich man to enter into the Kingdom of Heaven.
\v 25 And when they had heard this, the Disciples marveled very much, saying: Who then can be saved?
\v 26 And \sc Jesus\sc* beholding, said to them: With men this is impossible: but with God all things are possible. \ef - \fr 19.26 \fq All things possible. \ft This of the camel through a needle's eye, being possible to God, although he neither hath done it, nor by like will do it: maketh against the blasphemous infidelity of our Adversaries that say, God can do no more than he hath done or will do. We see also that God can bring a camel through a needle's eye, and therefore his body through a door, and out of the sepulchre shut, and out of his mother a virgin, and generally above nature and contrary to nature do with his body as he list.\ef*
\v 27 \f 1 \fr 19.27 \ft The Gospel upon the Conversion of S. Paul. Ian. 25. Also within the Octave, and in a votive Mass of SS. Peter & Paul, and for holy abbots.\f* Then Peter answering, said to him: Behold we have left all things, & have followed thee: what therefore shall we have? \ef - \fr 19.27 \fq Left all. \ft ¹ This perfection of leaving all things the Apostles vowed. \+it Aug. li. 17. de Civ. Dei c. 4.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Vow of poverty in respect of reward.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.27 \fq What shall we have. \ft They leave all things in respect of reward, and Christ doeth well allow it in them by his answer.\ef*
\v 28 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Amen I say to you, that you which have followed me, in the regeneration, when the Son of man shall sit in the seat of his majesty, you also shall sit upon twelve seats, judging the twelve Tribes of Israel. \ef - \fr 19.28 \fq You also shall sit. \ft ¹ Note that not only Christ, who is the principal and proper judge of the living and the dead, but with him the Apostles and all perfect Saints shall judge: and yet that doeth nothing derogate to his prerogative, by whom and under whom they hold this and all other dignities in this life and the next. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Aug. in Ps. 121.\+it*\ef*
\v 29 And every one that hath left house, or brethren, or sisters, or father, or mother, or \f 1 \fr 19.29 \ft Hereof is gathered that the Apostles among other things left their wives also to follow Christ. Hiero. li. 1. adv. Iovin.\f* wife, or children, or lands for my name's sake: shall receive an hundred-fold, & shall possess life everlasting.
\v 30 And \x - \xt Mr. 10, 31. Lu. 13, 30.\x* many shall be first, that are last; and last, that are first.
\c 20
\cd To shew how through God's grace the Jews shall be overrun of the Gentiles, although they begin after, he bringeth a parable of men working sooner and later in the vineyard, but the later rewarded in the end even as the first. 17. He revealeth more to his Disciples touching his passion: 20. Bidding the ambitious two suitors to think rather of suffering with him: 24. And teaching us (in the rest of his Disciples) not to be grieved at our Ecclesiastical Superiors, considering they are (as he was himself) to toil for our Salvation. 29. Then going out of Jericho, he giveth sight unto two blind.
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* \f 1 \fr 20.1 \ft The Gospel upon the Sunday of Septuagesima.\f* Kingdom of Heaven is like to a man that is an householder which went forth early in the morning to hire workmen into his vineyard. \ef - \fr 20.1 \fq In the morning. \ft God called some in the morning, that is, in the beginning of the world, as Abel, Enoch, Noe, and other the just and faithful of the first age: at the third hour, Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, and the rest of their age: at the 6. hour of the day, Moyses, Aaron, and the rest: at the 9. hour, the Prophets: at the eleventh, that is, at the later end of the world, the Christian Nations. \+it Aug. de verb. Domini ser. 59.\+it* Briefly, this calling at diverse hours signifieth the calling of the Jews from time to time in the first ages of the world, and of the Gentiles in the later age thereof. It signifieth also that God calleth countries to the faith, some sooner, some later: and particular men to be his servants, some younger, some elder, of diverse ages.\ef*
\v 2 And having made covenant with the workmen for a penny a day, he sent them into his vineyard.
\v 3 And going forth about the third hour, he saw other standing in the market place idle,
\v 4 and he said to them: Go you also into the vineyard, and that which shall be just, I will give you.
\v 5 And they went their way. And again he went forth about the sixth & the ninth hour: and did likewise.
\v 6 But about the eleventh hour he went forth and found other standing, & he saith to them: What stand you here all the day idle?
\v 7 They say to him: Because no man hath hired us. He saith to them: Go you also into the vineyard.
\v 8 And when evening was come, the Lord of the vineyard saith to his bailiff, Call the workmen, and pay them their hire, beginning from the last even to the first.
\v 9 Therefore when they were come that came about the eleventh hour, they received every one a penny. \ef - \fr 20.9 \fq Penny. \ft ¹ The penny promised to all, was life everlasting, which is common to all that shall be saved: but in the same life there be degrees of glory, as ² betwixt star and star in the element. \+it Aug. li. de virginit. c. 26.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Diversity of glory in Heaven. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 15.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 But when the first also came, they thought that they should receive more: and they also received every one a penny.
\v 11 And receiving it they \f 1 \fr 20.11 \ft The Jews are noted for envying the vocation of the Gentiles, and their reward equal with themselves.\f* murmured against the good-man of the house.
\v 12 Saying: These last have continued one hour, and thou hast made them equal to us that have borne the burden of the day & the heats.
\v 13 But he answering said to one of them, Friend, I do thee no wrong: didst thou not covenant with me for a penny?
\v 14 Take that is thine, and go: I will also give to this last even as to thee also.
\v 15 Or, is it not lawful for me to do that I will? Is thine eye naught, because I am good?
\v 16 So shall the last be first, and the first, last. For many be called, but few elect. \ef - \fr 20.16 \fq Few elect. \ft Those are elect which despised not their caller, but followed and believed him: for men believe not but of their own free will. \+it Aug. li. 1. ad Simplic. q. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 17 \x - \xt Mr. 10, 32. Luc. 18, 31.\x*\f 2 \fr 20.17 \ft The Gospel upon Wednesday the 2. week in Lent. And in a votive Mass of the holy Cross.\f* And \sc Jesus\sc* going up to Jerusalem, took the twelve Disciples secretly, and said to them:
\v 18 Behold we go up to Jerusalem, and the Son of man shall be delivered to the chief Priests and to the Scribes, and they shall condemn him to death,
\v 19 and shall deliver him to the Gentiles to be mocked, and scourged, and crucified, and the third day he shall rise again.
\v 20 \x - \xt Mr. 10, 35.\x*\f 2 \fr 20.20 \ft The Gospel upon S. Jame's day. \+it Iul. 25.\+it* And \+it S. John's ante portam Latinam Maij 6.\+it*\f* Then came to him the mother of the sons of Zebedee with her sons, adoring and desiring something of him.
\v 21 Who said to her: What wilt thou? She saith to him: Say that these my two sons may sit, one at thy right hand, & one at thy left hand in thy Kingdom.
\v 22 And \sc Jesus\sc* answering, said: You know not what you desire. Can you drink of the cup that I shall drink of? They say to him: We can.
\v 23 He saith to them: My cup indeed you shall drink of: but to sit at my right hand and left, is not mine to give to you: but to whom it is prepared of my Father. \ef - \fr 20.23 \fq To whom it is prepared. \ft The kingdom of heaven is prepared for them that are worthy of it and deserve it by their well doing, as in holy Scripture it is very often, That ¹ \+it God will repay every man according to his works\+it*, and, ² \+it come ye blessed, possess the kingdom prepared for you.\+it* Why? \+it Because I was hungry, and you gave me meat: thirsty, and you gave me drink\+it*: &c. ³ Therefore doth Christ say here, \+it It is not mine to give\+it*. Because he is just and will not give it to every man without respect of their deserts: yea nor alike to every one, but diversly according to greater or lesser merits, as here S. Chryso. ⁴ maketh it plain, when our Saviour telleth them, that although they suffer martyrdom for his sake, yet he hath not to give them the two chief places. \+it See S. Hiero. upon this place, and li. 2. adv. Iovin. c. 15.\+it* This also is a lesson for them that have to bestow Ecclesiastical benefices, that they have no carnal respect to kindred &c. but to the worthiness of the persons. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Mat. 16, 27. Ro. 2, 6.\+it* \fq ² \ft \+it Mt. 25, 34.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Difference of merits and reward. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Chrys. ho. 66. in Mt.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 And the ten hearing it, were displeased at the two brethren.
\v 25 And \sc Jesus\sc* calleth them unto him, and said: \x - \xt Mr. 10, 41. Luc. 22, 25.\x* You know that the princes of the Gentiles \f 2 \fr 20.25 \ft Superiority is not here forbidden among Christians, neither Ecclesiastical nor temporal; but heathenish tyranny is forbidden, and humility commended.\f* overrule them: and they that are the greater, exercise power against them.
\v 26 It shall not be so among you, but whosoever will be the greater among you, let him be your minister:
\v 27 And he that will be first among you, shall be your servant.
\v 28 Even as the Son of man is not come to be ministered unto, but to minister, and to give his life a redemption for many. \ef - \fr 20.28 \fq As the son of man. \ft Christ himself as he was the Son of man, was their and our Superior, and ¹ Lord and Master, notwithstanding his humility: ² and therefore it is pride and haughtiness which is forbidden, and not Superiority or Lordship, as some Heretics would have it. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Io. 13, 13.\+it* \fq ² \ft Puritanes.\ef*
\v 29 And \x - \xt Mr. 10, 46.\x* when they went out from Jericho, a great multitude followed him.
\v 30 And behold two blind men sitting by the way side, heard that \sc Jesus\sc* passed by, and they cried out saying: Lord, have mercy upon us, Son of David.
\v 31 And the multitude rebuked them that they should hold their peace. But they cried out the more, saying: Lord, have mercy upon us, Son of David.
\v 32 And \sc Jesus\sc* stood, and called them, and said: What will ye that I do to you?
\v 33 They say to him: Lord, that our eyes may be opened.
\v 34 And \sc Jesus\sc* having compassion on them, \f 1 \fr 20.34 \ft Our Saviour who always could, and often did cure the diseased, by his only will, or word, here gave sight to these blind men, by touching their eyes with his holy hand.\f* touched their eyes. And immediately they saw, and followed him.
\c 21
\cd Being now come to the place of his Passion, he entereth with humility and triumph together: 12. Sheweth his zeal for the house of God joined with great marvels. 15. And to the Rulers he boldly defendeth the acclamations of the children. 18. He curseth also that fruitless leafy tree: 23. avoucheth his power by the witness of John: 28. and foretelleth in two parables their reprobation (with the Gentiles' vocation) for their wicked deserts, 42. and consequently their irreparable damnation that shall \it ensue thereof.\it* \f - \ft THE FIFTH part of this Gospel. Of the Holy Week of his Passion in Hierusalem.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 11, 1. Luc. 19, 20. Io. 12, 15.\x*\f 2 \fr 21.1 \ft The Gospel on Palm Sunday before the benediction of the Palms.\f* when they drew nigh to Jerusalem, and were come to Beth-phagee unto Mount-olivet, then \sc Jesus\sc* sent two Disciples,
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 21.2 \ft PALM SUNDAY.\f* saying to them: Go ye into the town that is against you, and immediately you shall find an ass tied and a colt with her: loose them & bring them to me: \ef - \fr 21.2 \fq You shall find. \ft Christ by divine power both knew where these beasts were, being absent, and commanded them for his use, being another man's, and suddenly made the colt fit to be ridden on, never broken before.\ef*
\v 3 And if any man shall say ought unto you, say ye, that our Lord hath need of them: and forthwith he will let them go.
\v 4 And this was done that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the Prophet, saying:
\v 5 \x - \xt Esa. 62, 11. Zach. 9, 9.\x* \it Say ye to the daughter of Sion: Behold thy King cometh to thee, meek, & sitting upon an ass and a colt the foal of her that is used to the yoke.\it*
\v 6 And the Disciples going, did as \sc Jesus\sc* commanded them.
\v 7 And they brought the ass and the colt: and laid their garments upon them, and made him to sit thereon. \ef - \fr 21.7 \fq The ass and the colt. \ft ¹ This ass under yoke signifieth the Jews under the Law and under God their Lord, as it were his old and ancient people: the young colt now first ridden on by Christ, signifieth the Gentiles, wild hitherto and not broken, now to be called to the faith and to receive our Saviour's yoke. And therefore the three last Evangelists writing specially to the Gentiles, make mention of the colt only. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Hiero. in Mat. Aug. li. 12. cont. Faust. c. 42.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 And a very great multitude spread their garments in the way: and others did cut boughs from the trees, and strawed them in the way: \ef - \fr 21.8 \fq Garments in the way. \ft These offices of honour done to our Saviour extraordinarily, were very acceptable: and for a memory here of the holy Church maketh a solemn ¹ Procession every year upon this day, specially in our Country when it was Catholic, with the B. Sacrament reverently carried, as it were Christ upon the ass, and strawing of rushes and flowers, bearing of Palms, setting up boughs, spreading and hanging up the richest clothes, the choir and choristers singing, as here the children and the people. ² All done in a very goodly ceremony to the honour of Christ and the memory of his triumph upon this day. The like service and the like duties done to him in all other solemn Processions of the B. Sacrament, and otherwise, be undoubtedly no less grateful. \fq ¹ \ft Procession on Palm-Sunday with the B. Sacrament. \fq ² \ft All devout offices in that kind, exceeding grateful.\ef*
\v 9 and the multitudes that went before and that followed, cried, saying: \x - \xt Ps. 117, 26.\x* \it Hosanna to the Son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of our Lord. Hosanna in the highest.\it* \ef - \fr 21.9 \fq Hosanna. \ft ¹ These very words of joyful cry and triumphant voice of gratulation to our Saviour, holy Church useth always in the preface of the Mass, as it were the voice of the Priest and all the people (who then specially are attent and devout) immediately before the Consecration and Elevation, as it were expecting, and rejoicing at his coming. \fq ¹ \ft HOSANNA.\ef*
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 21.10 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday in the first week in Lent.\f* And when he was entered Jerusalem, the whole city was moved, saying: Who is this?
\v 11 And the People said: This is \sc Jesus\sc* the Prophet, of Nazareth in Galilee.
\v 12 And \x - \xt Mr. 11, 15. Lu. 19, 45.\x* \sc Jesus\sc* entered into the temple of God, and cast out all that \f 2 \fr 21.12 \ft How much the abuse of Churches by merchandising, walking, or other profane occupying of them, displeaseth God, here we may see.\f* sold and bought in the temple, and the tables of the bankers, & the chairs of them that sold pigeons he overthrew:
\v 13 and he saith to them: It is written, \x - \xt Esa. 56, 7. Ier. 7, 11.\x* \it My house shall be called the house of prayer: but you have made it a den of thieves.\it* \ef - \fr 21.13 \fq House of prayer. \ft Note here that he calleth external sacrifice (out of the Prophet Esay) prayer. For he speaketh of the Temple, which was builded properly and principally for sacrifice.\ef*
\v 14 And there came to him the blind, and the lame in the temple; and he healed them.
\v 15 And the chief Priests & Scribes seeing the marvelous things that he did, and the children crying in the temple, & saying, \it Hosanna to the Son of David;\it* they had indignation,
\v 16 and said to him: Hearest thou what these say? And \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Very well. Have you never read: \x - \xt Ps. 8, 3.\x* \it That out of the mouth of infants and sucklings thou hast perfited praise?\it* \ef - \fr 21.16 \fq Mouth of infants. \ft ¹ Young children's prayers proceeding from the instinct of God's spirit, be acceptable: and so the voices of the like, or of other simple folk now in the Church, though themselves understand not particularly what they say, be marvelous grateful to Christ. \fq ¹ \ft Prayers not understood of the party, are acceptable.\ef*
\v 17 And leaving them, he went forth out of the city into Bethania, and remained there.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 21.18 \ft MONDAY.\f* And in the morning returning into the city, he was an hungered.
\v 19 \x - \xt Mr. 11, 13.\x* And seeing a certain \f 2 \fr 21.19 \ft The Jews having the words of the law, and not the deeds, were the fig tree full of leaves, and void of fruit. \+it Aug. de verb. Do. Serm. 44.\+it*\f* figtree by the way side, he came to it, and found nothing on it but leaves only, and he saith to it: Never grow there fruit of thee for ever. And incontinent the figtree was withered.
\v 20 And the Disciples seeing it, marveled saying: How is it withered incontinent?
\v 21 \x - \xt Mt. 17, 20.\x* And \sc Jesus\sc* answering said to them: Amen I say to you, \f 2 \fr 21.21 \ft b TUESDAY.\f* if you shall have faith, and stagger not, not only that of the figtree shall you do, but and if you shall say to this mountain, Take up and throw thyself into the sea, it shall be done.
\v 22 And all things whatsoever you shall ask in prayer believing, you shall receive. \ef - \fr 21.22 \fq Believing. \ft In respect of our own unworthiness, and of the thing not always expedient for us, we may well doubt when we pray, whether we shall obtain or no: but on God's part we must believe, that is, we must have no diffidence or mistrust either of his power or of his will, if we be worthy, and the thing expedient. And therefore S. Mark hath thus, \+it Have ye faith of God.\+it* ¹ \fq ¹ \ft \+it Mr. 11. 22.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 And when he was come into the temple, there came to him as he was teaching, the chief Priests and ancients of the people, saying: \x - \xt Mr. 11, 28. Lu. 20, 2.\x* In what power doest thou these things? and who hath given thee this power? \ef - \fr 21.23 \fq In what power? \ft ¹ The Heretics presumptuously think themselves in this point like to Christ, because they are asked, in what power they come, and who sent them: but when they have answered this question as fully as Christ did here by that which he insinuateth of John's testimony for his authority, they shall be heard, and till then they shall be still taken for those of whom God speaketh by the Prophet, ² \+it They ran, and I sent them not.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heretics run, not sent. \fq ² \ft \+it Iere. 23.\+it*\ef*
\v 24 \sc Jesus\sc* answering said to them: I also will ask you one word, which if you shall tell me, I also will tell you in what power I do these things.
\v 25 The Baptism of John whence was it? from Heaven, or from men? But they thought within themselves, saying:
\v 26 If we shall say from Heaven, he will say to us, why then did you not believe him? But if we shall say from men, we fear the multitude, for all hold John as a Prophet.
\v 27 And answering to \sc Jesus\sc* they said: We know not. He also said to them: Neither do I tell you in what power I do these things.
\v 28 But what is your opinion? A certain man had two sons, and coming to the first, he said: Son, go work today in my vineyard. \ef - \fr 21.28 \fq The first. \ft The first son here is the people of the Gentiles, because Gentility was before there was a peculiar and chosen people of the Jews. And therefore the Jews here as the later, are signified by the other son.\ef*
\v 29 And he answering, said: I will not. But afterward moved with repentance he went.
\v 30 And coming to the other, he said likewise. And he answering, said: I go Lord, and he went not:
\v 31 Which of the two did the father's will? They say to him: The first. \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Amen I say to you, that the Publicans and whores go before you into the Kingdom of God.
\v 32 For John came to you in the way of justice, and you did not believe him. But the Publicans and whores did believe him: but you seeing it, neither have ye had repentance afterward, to believe him.
\v 33 \x - \xt Es. 5, 1. Mr. 12, 1. Lu. 20. 9.\x* Another parable hear ye: A man there was an householder who \f 2 \fr 21.33 \ft The Gospel upon Friday the 2. week in Lent.\f* planted a vineyard, and made a hedge round about it, and digged in it a press, and builded a tower, and let it out to husbandmen: and went forth into a strange country.
\v 34 And when the time of fruits drew nigh, he sent his servants to the husbandmen, to receive the fruits thereof.
\v 35 And the husbandmen apprehending his servants, one they beat, another they killed, and another they stoned.
\v 36 Again he sent other servants more than the former: and they did to them likewise.
\v 37 And last of all he sent to them his son, saying: They will reverence my son.
\v 38 But the husbandmen seeing the son, said within themselves: This is the heir, come, let us kill him, and we shall have his inheritance.
\v 39 And apprehending him they cast him forth out of the vineyard, & killed him.
\v 40 When therefore the Lord of the vineyard shall come, what will he do to those husbandmen?
\v 41 They say to him: The naughty men he will bring to naught: and his vineyard he will let out to other husbandmen, that shall render him the fruit in their seasons.
\v 42 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Have you never read in the Scriptures: \x - \xt Psa. 117. 21.\x* \it The stone which the builders rejected, the same is made into the head of the corner? By our Lord was this done, and it is marvelous in our eyes.\it*
\v 43 Therefore I say to you, that the Kingdom of God shall be taken away from you, and shall be given to a Nation yielding the fruits thereof.
\v 44 And \x - \xt Es. 8, 14.\x* he that falleth upon this stone, shall be broken: and on whom it falleth, it shall all to bruise him.
\v 45 And when the chief Priests and Pharisees had heard his parables, they knew that he spake of them.
\v 46 And seeking to lay hands upon him, they feared the multitudes: because they held him as a Prophet.
\c 22
\cd Yet by one other parable he foresheweth the most deserved reprobation of the earthly and persecuting Jews, and the gracious vocation of the Gentiles in their place. 15. Then he defeateth the snare of the Pharisees and Herodians about paying tribute to Cæsar. 23. He answereth also the invention of the Sadducees against the Resurrection: 34. and a question that the Pharisees ask to pose him: turning and posing them again, because they imagined that Christ should be no more than a man: 46. and so he putteth all the busy Sects to silence.
\p
\v 1 \sc And \f 1 \fr 22.1 \ft The Gospel upon the 19. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Jesus\sc* answering, spake again in parables to them, saying:
\v 2 The Kingdom of Heaven is likened to a man being a king, which made a marriage to his son. \ef - \fr 22.2 \fq Marriage. \ft Then did God the Father make this marriage, when by the mystery of the Incarnation he joined to his son our Lord, the holy Church for his spouse. \+it Greg. hom. 38.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 And he sent his servants to call them that were invited to the marriage: and they would not come. \ef - \fr 22.3 \fq Servants. \ft The first servants here sent to invite, were the Prophets: the second, were the Apostles: and all that afterward converted countries, or that have and do reconcile men to the Church.\ef*
\v 4 Again he sent other servants, saying: Tell them that were invited, Behold I have prepared my dinner; my beeves & fatlings are killed, and all things are ready: come ye to the marriage.
\v 5 But they neglected and went their ways, one to his farm, and another to his merchandise: \ef - \fr 22.5 \fq One to his farm. \ft ¹ Such as refuse to be reconciled to Christ's Church, allege often vain impediments and worldly excuses, which at the day of judgement will not serve them. \fq ¹ \ft Worldly excuses against reconciliation.\ef*
\v 6 and the rest laid hands upon his servants, and spitefully entreating them, murdered them.
\v 7 But when the king had heard of it, he was wroth, and sending his hosts, destroyed those murderers, and burnt their city.
\v 8 Then he saith to his servants: The marriage indeed is ready: but they that were invited, were not worthy.
\v 9 Go ye therefore into the high ways; and whosoever you shall find, call to the marriage.
\v 10 And his servants going forth into the ways, gathered together all that they found, \f 1 \fr 22.10 \ft Not only good men be within the Church, but also evil men; against the Heretics of these days.\f* bad and good: and the marriage was filled with guests.
\v 11 And the king went in to see the guests: and he saw there a man not attired in a wedding garment. \ef - \fr 22.11 \fq A man not attired. \ft It profiteth not much to be within the Church and to be a Catholic, except a man be of good life, for such an one shall be damned, because with faith he hath not good works: as is evident by the example of this man, who was within, and at the feast as the rest, but lacked the garment of charity and good works. ¹ And by this man are represented all the bad that are called. And therefore they also are in the Church, as this man was at the feast: but because he was called, and yet none of the elect, it is evident that the Church doth not consist of the elect only, contrary to our Adversaries. \fq ¹ \ft The Church consisteth of good and bad.\ef*
\v 12 And he saith to him: Friend, how camest thou in hither not having a wedding garment? But he was dumb.
\v 13 Then the king said to the waiters: Bind his hands and feet, and cast him into the utter darkness: there shall be weeping & gnashing of teeth.
\v 14 For many be called, but few elect.
\v 15 \x - \xt Mr. 12, 13. Lu. 20, 20.\x*\f 2 \fr 22.15 \ft The Gospel upon the 22. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Then the Pharisees departing, consulted among themselves for to entrap him in his talk.
\v 16 And they send to him their disciples with the Herodians, saying: Master, we know that thou art a true speaker, and teachest the way of God in truth, neither carest thou for any man. For thou dost not respect the person of men:
\v 17 Tell us therefore what is thy opinion, is it lawful to give tribute to Cæsar, or not?
\v 18 But \sc Jesus\sc* knowing their naughtiness, said: What do you tempt me Hypocrites?
\v 19 Shew me the tribute coin. And they offered him a penny.
\v 20 And \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: Whose is this image and superscription?
\v 21 They say to him, Cæsar's. Then he saith to them: Render therefore the things that are Cæsar's, to Cæsar: and the things that are God's, to God. \ef - \fr 22.21 \fq To Cæsar. \ft Temporal duties and payments exacted by worldly Princes must be payed, so that God be not defrauded of his more sovereign duty. ¹ And therefore Princes have to take heed, how they exact: and others, how they give to Cæsar, that is, to their Prince, the things that are due to God, that is, to his Ecclesiastical ministers. Whereupon S. Athanasius reciteth these goodly words out of an epistle of the ancient and famous Confessor Hosius Cordubensis to Constantius the Arian Emperor: Cease I beseech thee, and remember that thou art mortal, fear the day of judgement, intermeddle not with Ecclesiastical matters, neither do thou command us in this kind, but rather learn them of us. To thee God hath committed the Empire, to us he hath committed the things that belong to the Church: and as he that with malicious eyes carpeth thine Empire, gainsayeth the ordinance of God: so do thou also beware, lest in drawing unto thee Ecclesiastical matters, thou be made guilty of a great crime. It is written, Give ye the things that are Cæsar's, to Cæsar: and the things that are God's, to God. Therefore neither is it lawful for us in earth to hold the Empire, neither has thou (O Emperor) power over incense and sacred things. \+it Athan. Ep. ad Solit. vitam agentes.\+it* And S. Ambrose to Valentinian the Emperor (who by the ill counsel of his mother Justina an Arian, required of S. Ambrose to have one Church in Milan deputed to the Arian Heretics) saith: we pay that which is Cæsar's, to Cæsar: and that which is God's, to God. Tribute is Cæsar's, it is not denied: the Church is God's, it may not verily be yielded to Cæsar: because the Temple of God cannot be Cæsar's right. Which no man can deny but it is spoken with the honour of the Emperor. For what is more honourable than that the Emperor be said to be the son of the Church? For a good Emperor is within the Church, not above the Church. \+it Ambr. lib. 5. Epist. Orat. de Basil. trad.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Neither must temporal Princes exact, nor their Subjects give unto them, Ecclesiastical jurisdiction.\ef*
\v 22 And hearing it they marveled, and leaving him went their ways.
\v 23 \x - \xt Mr. 12, 18. Lu. 20, 27. Act. 23, 6.\x* That day there came to him the Sadducees, that say there is no resurrection, and asked him,
\v 24 saying: Master, Moyses said, \x - \xt Deu. 25, 5.\x* \it If a man die not having a child, that his brother marry his wife, and raise up seed to his brother.\it*
\v 25 And there were with us seven brethren: and the first having married a wife, died; and not having issue, left his wife to his brother.
\v 26 In like manner the second and the third even to the seventh.
\v 27 And last of all the woman died also.
\v 28 In the Resurrection therefore whose wife of the seven shall she be? For they all had her.
\v 29 And \sc Jesus\sc* answering, said to them: You do err, not knowing the Scriptures, nor the power of God.
\v 30 For in the resurrection neither shall they marry nor be married: but are as the Angels of God in Heaven. \ef - \fr 22.30 \fq As Angels. \ft ¹ As Christ proveth here, that in heaven they neither marry nor are married, because there they shall be as Angels: by the very same reason, is proved, that Saints may hear our prayers and help us, be they near or far off, because the Angels do so, and in every moment are present where they list, and need not to be near us, when they hear or help us. \fq ¹ \ft The saints hear our prayers.\ef* \ef - \fr 22.30 \fq As Angels. \ft ¹ Not to marry nor be married, is to be like to Angels: therefore is the state of Religious men and women and Priests, for not marrying, worthily called of the Fathers, an Angelical life. \+it Cyp. lib. 2. de discipl. & hab. virg. sub finem.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Religious single life, Angelical.\ef*
\v 31 And concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read that which was spoken of God saying to you.
\v 32 \x - \xt Exo. 3, 6.\x* \it I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob?\it* He is not God of the dead, but of the living. \ef - \fr 22.32 \fq Of the dead. \ft S. Hierom by this place disproveth the Heretic Vigilantius, and in him these of our time, which to diminish the honour of Saints, call them of purpose, dead men.\ef*
\v 33 And the multitudes hearing it, marveled at his doctrine.
\v 34 \x - \xt Mar. 12, 28.\x*\f 2 \fr 22.34 \ft The Gospel upon the 17. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* But the Pharisees hearing that he had put the Sadducees to silence, came together:
\v 35 and one of them a doctor of law asked of him, tempting him:
\v 36 Master, which is the great commandment in the law?
\v 37 \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: \x - \xt Deut. 6, 5.\x* \it Thou shalt love the Lord thy God from thy whole heart, and with thy whole soul, and with thy whole mind.\it*
\v 38 This is the greatest & the first commandment.
\v 39 And the second is like to this: \x - \xt Lev. 19, 18.\x* \it Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.\it*
\v 40 On these two commandments dependeth the whole Law and the Prophets. \ef - \fr 22.40 \fq On these two. \ft ¹ Hereby it is evident that all dependeth not upon faith only, but much more upon charity (though faith be the first) which is the love of God and of our neighbour, which is the sum of all the law and the Prophets: because he that hath this double charity expressed here by these two principal commandments, fulfilleth and accomplisheth all that is commanded in the Law and the Prophets. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith.\ef*
\v 41 And \x - \xt Mr. 12, 35. Lu. 20, 41.\x* the Pharisees being assembled, \sc Jesus\sc* asked them
\v 42 saying: What is your opinion of Christ? whose son is he? They say to him, David's.
\v 43 He saith to them: How then doth David in spirit call him Lord, saying:
\v 44 \x - \xt Ps. 109. 1.\x* \it The Lord said to my Lord, sit on my right hand, until I put thine enemies the foot-stool of thy feet?\it*
\v 45 If David therefore call him Lord, how is he his son?
\v 46 And no man could answer him a word: neither durst any man from that day ask him any more.
\c 23
\cd The Scribes and Pharisees after all this, continuing still incorrigible, although he will have the doctrine of their Chair obeyed, yet against their works (and namely their ambition) he openly inveigheth, crying to them eight woes for their eightfold hypocrisy and blindness: 34. and so concluding with the most worthy reprobation of that persecuting generation and their mother-city Jerusalem with her Temple.
\p
\v 1 \sc Then \f 1 \fr 23.1 \ft The Gospel upon Tuesday the second week in Lent.\f* Jesus\sc* spake to the multitudes and to his Disciples,
\v 2 saying: Upon the chair of Moyses have sitten the Scribes and the Pharisees. \ef - \fr 23.2 \fq Chair of Moyses. \ft ¹ God preserveth the truth of Christian religion in the Apostolic See of Rome, which is in the new law answerable to the chair of Moyses, notwithstanding the Bishops of the same were never so wicked of life: yea though some traitor as ill as Judas were Bishop thereof, it should not be prejudicial to the Church and innocent Christians, for whom our Lord providing said, Do that which they say, but do not as they do. \+it August. Epist. 165.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The See of Rome preserved in truth.\ef*
\v 3 All things therefore whatsoever they shall say to you, observe ye and do ye: but according to their works do ye not, for they say and do not. \ef - \fr 23.3 \fq Whatsoever they shall say. \ft ¹ \+it Why\+it* (saith S. Augustin ² ) \+it doest thou call the Apostolic Chair the chair of pestilence? If for the men, why? Did our Lord Jesus Christ for the Pharisees, any wrong to the Chair wherein they sat? Did he not commend that chair of Moyses, and preserving the honour of the chair, reprove them? For he saith: They sit upon the Chair of Moyses, that which they say, do ye. These things if you did well consider, you would not for the men whom you defame, blaspheme the See Apostolic, wherewith you do not communicate.\+it* And again he saith ³: \+it Neither for the Pharisees (to whom you compare us not of wisdom but of malice) did our Lord command the Chair of Moyses to be forsaken, in which chair verily he figured his own. For he warneth the people to do that which they say, and not to do that which they do, and that the holiness of the Chair be in no case forsaken, nor the unity of the flock divided, for the naughty Pastors.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The dignity of the See of Rome, notwithstanding some evil Bishops thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Contra lit. Petil. l. 2. c. 51.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Contra lit. Petil. l. 2. c. 61.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 For \x - \xt Luc. 11, 46. Act. 15, 10.\x* they bind heavy burdens & importable: & put them upon men's shoulders: but with a finger of their own they will not move them.
\v 5 But they do all their works for to be seen of men. For they make broad their \x - \xt Deu. 22, 12. Nu. 15, 38.\x* phylacteries, and enlarge their \f 2 \fr 23.5 \ft These phylacteries were pieces of parchment, wherein they wrote the ten commandments, and folded it, and carried it on their forehead before their eyes, imagining grossly and superstitiously, that so they fulfilled that which is said Deu. 6, \+it They shall be immovable before thine eyes.\+it* Hiero. in 23. Mat.\f* fringes.
\v 6 And they love the first places at suppers, and \x - \xt Mr. 12, 38.\x* the first chairs in the Synagogues, \ef - \fr 23.6 \fq Love the first places. \ft He condemneth not due places of Superiority given or taken of men according to their degrees, but ambitious seeking for the same, and their proud heart and wicked intention, which he saw within them, and therefore might boldly reprehend them.\ef*
\v 7 and salutations in the market-place, and to be called of men, Rabbi.
\v 8 But be not you called Rabbi. For one is your Master, and all you are brethren. \ef - \fr 23.8 \fq One is your master. \ft ¹ In the Catholic Church there is one Master, Christ our Lord, and under him one Vicar, with whom all Catholic Doctors and teachers are one, because they teach all one thing. But in Arch-heretics it is not so, where every one of them is a diverse master, and teacheth contrary to the other, and will be called Rabbi and Master, every one of their own Disciples: Arius a Rabbi among the Arians, Luther among the Lutherans, and among the Calvinists Calvin. \fq ¹ \ft Many masters are many Arch-heretics.\ef*
\v 9 And call none Father to yourself upon earth: for one is your Father, he that is in Heaven.
\v 10 Neither \x - \xt Ia. 3, 1.\x* be ye called Masters: for one is your Master, Christ. \ef - \fr 23.10 \fq Masters. \ft ¹ Wicleffe and the like Heretics of this time do hereupon condemn degrees of School and titles of Doctors and Masters: where they might as well reprove S. Paul for calling himself \+it Doctor and Master of the Gentiles\+it* ² \+it :\+it* and for saying ³ that there should be always \+it Doctors\+it* in the Church. And whereas they bring the other words following, against Religious men who are called Fathers: as well might they by this place take away the name of carnal fathers, and blame S. Paul for calling himself the only spiritual Father of the Corinthians ⁴. But indeed nothing is here forbidden but the contentious division and partiality of such as make themselves Ringleaders of Schisms and Sects, as Donatus, Arius, Luther, Calvin. \fq ¹ \ft Doctors, Masters, and spiritual fathers. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Tim. 2. 2. Tim. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Eph. 4.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 4, 15.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 He that is the greater of you, shall be your servitor.
\v 12 And he that exalteth himself, shall be humbled: and he that humbleth himself, shall be exalted.
\v 13 But woe to you Scribes & Pharisees, hypocrites: because you shut the Kingdom of Heaven before men. For yourselves do not enter in: & those that are going in, you suffer not to enter. \ef - \fr 23.13 \fq Scribes and Pharisees. \ft ¹ In all these reprehensions it is much to be noted, that our Saviour for the honour of Priesthood never reprehendeth Priests by that name. \+it Cypr. ep. 65.\+it* whereas our Heretics use this name of purpose in reproach and despite. \fq ¹ \ft The honour of Priesthood.\ef*
\v 14 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites: because you \x - \xt Lu. 20, 47.\x* devour widows' houses, praying long prayers. For this you shall receive the greater judgement. \ef - \fr 23.14 \fq Praying long prayers. \ft ¹ They are not reprehended here for the things themselves, which for the most part are good, as, long prayer, making Proselytes, garnishing the Prophets' sepulchres, &c. but for their wicked purpose and intention, as before is said of fasting, prayers, alms, \+it Mat. 6.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The intention.\ef*
\v 15 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites: because you go round about the sea and the land, to make one proselyte: and when he is made, you make him the child of hell double more than yourselves. \ef - \fr 23.15 \fq Double more. \ft ¹ They that teach that it is enough to have only faith, do make such Christians, as the Jews did Proselytes, children of Hell far more than before. \+it August. lib. de fide & oper. cap. 26.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith.\ef*
\v 16 Woe to you blind guides, that say, whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing: but he that shall swear by the gold of the temple, is bound.
\v 17 Ye foolish and blind; for whether is greater, the gold, or the temple that sanctifieth the gold?
\v 18 And whosoever shall swear by the Altar, it is nothing: but whosoever shall swear by the gift that is upon it, is bound.
\v 19 Ye blind; for whether is greater, the gift, or the Altar that sanctifieth the gift? \ef - \fr 23.19 \fq Sanctifieth. \ft Note that donaries and gifts bestowed upon Churches and altars, be sanctified by dedication to God, and by touching the altar and other holy things: as now specially the vessels of the sacrifice and Sacrament of Christ's body and blood, by touching the same, ¹ and the altar itself whereupon it is consecrated. Whereof Theophylacte ² writeth thus upon this place: \+it In the old law Christ permitteth not the gift to be greater than the altar, but with us, the altar is sanctified by the gift: for the hosts by the divine grace are turned into our Lord's body, and therefore is the altar also sanctified by them.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The altar is sanctified by our Lord's body thereupon. \fq ² \ft \+it Theophyl. 23. in Mat.\+it*\ef*
\v 20 He therefore that sweareth by the Altar, sweareth by it, and by all things that are upon it:
\v 21 And whosoever shall swear by the temple, sweareth by it and by him that dwelleth in it: \ef - \fr 23.21 \fq By him that dwelleth therein. \ft By this we see that swearing by creatures, as by the Gospel, by Saints, is all referred to the honour of God, whose Gospel it is, whose Saints they are.\ef*
\v 22 And he that sweareth by Heaven, sweareth by the throne of God, and by him that sitteth thereon.
\v 23 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites: because you tithe mint, and anise, and cummin, and have left the weightier things of the law, judgement, and mercy, and faith. These things you ought to have done, & not to have omitted those.
\v 24 Blind guides, that strain a gnat, and swallow a camel.
\v 25 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, Hypocrites: because you make clean that on the outside of the cup and dish, but within \f 1 \fr 23.25 \fq you are full \ft \+it they are full.\+it*\f* you are full of rapine and uncleanness.
\v 26 Thou blind Pharisee, first make clean the inside of the cup and the dish that the outside may become clean.
\v 27 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, Hypocrites: because you are like to whited sepulchres, which outwardly appear unto men beautiful, but within are full of dead men's bones, and all filthiness.
\v 28 So you also outwardly indeed appear to men just; but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and iniquity. \ef - \fr 23.28 \fq Appear to men. \ft Christ might boldly reprehend them so often and so vehemently for hypocrisy, because he knew their hearts and intentions: but we that cannot see within men, may not presume to call men's external good doings, hypocrisy: but judge of men as we see and know.\ef*
\v 29 Woe to you Scribes and Pharisees, Hypocrites: because you build the Prophets' sepulchres, and garnish the monuments of just men, \ef - \fr 23.29 \fq Garnish. \ft Christ blameth not the Jews for adorning the sepulchres of the Prophets, but entwiteth them of their malice toward him, and of that which by his divine knowledge he foresaw, that they would accomplish the wickedness of their fathers in shedding his blood, as their fathers did the blood of the Prophets. \+it Hilar.\+it*\ef*
\v 30 and say: If we had been in our fathers' days, we had not been their fellows in the blood of the Prophets.
\v 31 Therefore you are a testimony to your own selves, that you are the sons of them that killed the Prophets.
\v 32 And fill you up the measure of your fathers.
\v 33 You serpents, viper's broods, how will you flee from the judgement of Hell?
\v 34 \f 1 \fr 23.34 \ft The Gospel upon S. Steven's day Decemb. 26. And his Invention. Aug. 3.\f* Therefore behold I send unto you Prophets, and wise men, and Scribes, and of them you shall kill & crucify, and of them you shall scourge in your Synagogues, and persecute from city into city:
\v 35 that upon you may come all the just blood that was shed upon the earth, from the blood of \x - \xt Gen. 4, 8.\x* Abel the just, even unto the blood of \x - \xt 2. Par. 24, 22.\x* Zacharias the son of Barachias, whom you murdered between the temple & the altar.
\v 36 Amen I say to you, all these things shall come upon this generation.
\v 37 \x - \xt Luc. 13, 34.\x* Jerusalem, Jerusalem, which killest the Prophets, and stonest them that were sent to thee, how often would I gather together thy children as the hen doth gather together her chickens under her wings, and thou \f 2 \fr 23.37 \ft Free will.\f* wouldest not?
\v 38 Behold, your house shall be left desert to you.
\v 39 For I say to you, you shall not see me from hence forth till you say: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of our Lord.
\c 24
\cd To his Disciples (by occasion of Hierusalem and the Temple's destruction) he foretelleth 4. what things shall be before the consummation of the world, as specially, 14. the Church's full preaching unto all nations: 15. then, what shall be in the very consummation, to wit, Antichrist with his passing great persecution and seduction, but for a short time: 29. then incontinent, the Day of judgement to our great comfort in those miseries under Antichrist. 35 As for the moment, to us it pertaineth not to know it, 37. but rather every man to watch, that we be not unprovided when he cometh to each one particularly by death.
\p
\v 1 \sc And \x - \xt Mr. 13, 1. Lu. 21, 5.\x* Jesus\sc* being gone out of the temple, went. And his Disciples came to shew him the buildings of the temple.
\v 2 And he answering said to them: Do you see all these things? Amen I say to you, there shall not be left here a stone upon a stone that shall not be destroyed. \ef - \fr 24.2 \fq Not left. \ft This was fulfilled 40 years after Christ's Ascension by Vespasian the Emperor and his son Titus. \+it Euseb. li. 3. c. 6. & seq. ex Josepho.\+it* Upon which words, \+it There shall not be left &c.\+it* which threaten the destruction of the Jews' Temple: and those words, \+it Upon this Rock I will build my Church\+it* ¹, which promise the building of the Catholic Church of all nations ²: S. Chrysostome ³ making a long comparison of these two prophecies of Christ, saith thus: Thou seest in both, his great and unspeakable power, in that that he increased and built up them that worshipped him, and those that stumbled at him, he abased, destroyed, and plucked them up by the root. Doest thou see how whatsoever he hath built, no man shall destroy: and whatsoever he hath destroyed, no man shall build? He builded the Church, and no man shall be able to destroy it: he destroyed the Temple, and no man is able to build it, and that in so long time. For they have endeavoured both to destroy that, and could not: and they have attempted to build up this, and they could not do that neither. &c. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Mat. 16.\+it* \fq ² \ft The Church can never fail. \fq ³ \ft \+it Li. adv. Gentes quod Christus sit Deus, prope finem.\+it*\ef*
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 24.3 \ft The Gospel for a votive Mass in time of war, & for many Martyrs.\f* And when he was sitting upon Mount-olivet, the Disciples came to him secretly, saying: Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign of thy coming, and of the consummation of the world? \ef - \fr 24.3 \fq The sign. \ft Our Master knowing that it was not profitable nor seemly for them to know these secrets, gave them by way of Prophecy, warning of diverse miseries, signs, and tokens, that should fall, some further off, and some nearer the later day: by which the faithful might always prepare themselves; but never be certain of the hour, day, month, nor year, when it should fall. \+it Aug. ep. 80.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 24.4 \ft TUESDAY night.\f* And \sc Jesus\sc* answering, said to them: Beware that no man seduce you: \ef - \fr 24.4 \fq Seduce. \ft ¹ The first and principal warning, needful for the faithful from Christ's Ascension to the very end of the world, is, that they be not deceived by Heretics, which under the titles of true teachers and the name of Christ and his Gospel, will seduce many. \fq ¹ \ft Heretics seduce under fair titles.\ef*
\v 5 for many shall come in my name saying, I am Christ; and they shall seduce many. \ef - \fr 24.5 \fq I am Christ. \ft Not only such as have named themselves Christ, as Simon, Menander, and such like: but all Arch-heretics be Christs to their followers, Luther to the Lutherans, Calvin to the Calvinists: because they believe them, rather than Christ speaking in his Church.\ef*
\v 6 For you shall hear of wars, & bruits of wars. See that ye be not troubled. For these things must be done, but the end is not yet.
\v 7 For Nation shall rise against Nation, and Kingdom against Kingdom; and there shall be pestilences, and famines, and earth-quakes in places;
\v 8 and all these things are the beginnings of sorrows.
\v 9 Then \x - \xt Mat. 10, 17.\x* shall they deliver you into tribulation, and shall kill you: and you shall be odious to all Nations for my name's sake.
\v 10 And then many shall be scandalized: and they shall deliver up one another: and they shall hate one another.
\v 11 And many \f 1 \fr 24.11 \ft There were in the people false Prophets, as among you also shall be lying Masters, which shall bring in Sects of perdition. \+it 2. Pet. 2.\+it*\f* false-prophets shall rise, and shall seduce many.
\v 12 And because iniquity shall abound, the charity of many shall wax cold. \ef - \fr 24.12 \fq Iniquity abound. \ft When Heresy and false teachers reign in the world, namely toward the later day, wicked life aboundeth, and charity decayeth.\ef*
\v 13 But he that shall persevere to the end, he shall be saved.
\v 14 And this Gospel of the Kingdom shall be preached in the whole world, for a testimony to all nations, and then shall come the consummation. \ef - \fr 24.14 \fq Shall be preached. \ft ¹ The Gospel hath been preached of late years, and now is, by holy Religious men of diverse Orders, in sundry great Countries which never heard the Gospel before, as it is thought. \fq ¹ \ft The Gospel now preached to Infidels.\ef*
\v 15 \x - \xt Dan. 9, 27.\x* Therefore when you shall see \f 2 \fr 24.15 \ft The Gospel upon the last Sunday after Pentecost.\f* \it the abomination of desolation,\it* which was spoken of by Daniel the Prophet, standing in the holy place (he that readeth, let him understand) \ef - \fr 24.15 \fq Abomination of desolation. \ft ¹ This abomination of desolation foretold, was first partly fulfilled in diverse profanations of the Temple of Hierusalem, when the sacrifice and service of God was taken away, but specially it shall be fulfilled by Antichrist and his Precursors, when they shall abolish the holy Mass ², which is the Sacrifice of Christ's body and blood, and the only sovereign worship due to God in his Church: as S. Hyppolytus writeth in these words: The Churches shall lament with great lamentations, because there shall neither oblation be made, nor incense, nor worship grateful to God. But the sacred houses of Churches shall be like to cottages, and the precious body and blood of Christ shall not be extant (openly in Churches) in those days, the Liturgy (or Mass) shall be extinguished, the Psalmody shall cease, the reciting of the Scriptures shall not be heard. \+it Hippol. de Antichristo.\+it* By which it is plain that the Heretics of these days be the special fore-runners of Antichrist. \fq ¹ \ft The abomination of desolation. \fq ² \ft The abolishing of the holy sacrifice of the Mass by Antichrist and his ministers.\ef*
\v 16 then they that are in Jewry, let them flee to the mountains:
\v 17 and he that is on the house-top let him not come down to take any thing out of his house:
\v 18 and he that is in the field, let him not go back to take his coat.
\v 19 And woe to them that are with child, and that give suck in those days.
\v 20 But pray that your flight be not in the winter, or on the Sabboth,
\v 21 For there shall be then great tribulation, such as hath not been from the beginning of the world until now, neither shall be.
\v 22 And unless those days had been shortened, no flesh should be saved: but for the Elect the days shall be shortened. \ef - \fr 24.22 \fq Shall be shortened. \ft ¹ The reign of Antichrist shall be short, that is, three years and a half. \+it Dan. 7. Apoc. 11.\+it* Therefore the Heretics are blasphemous and ridiculous, that say, Christ's Vicar is Antichrist, who hath sitten these 1500 years. \fq ¹ \ft Antichrist.\ef*
\v 23 Then if any man shall say unto you: Lo \f 1 \fr 24.23 \ft Whosoever draweth Christ or his Church from the Communion and fellowship of all Nations Christened, to one corner, town, or Country, believe him not. \+it Aug. de unit. Ec. c. 3.\+it*\f* here is Christ, or there; do not believe him.
\v 24 For there shall rise false Christs and false-Prophets, and shall shew great signs and wonders, so that the Elect also (if it be possible) may be induced into error. \ef - \fr 24.24 \fq Great signs. \ft These signs and miracles shall be to the outward appearance only, for S. Paul calleth them ¹ lying signs, to seduce them only that shall perish. Whereby we see that if Heretics could work feigned and forged miracles, yet we ought not to believe them, much less when they cannot so much as seem to do any. \fq ¹ \ft \+it 2. Thes. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 25 Lo I have foretold you.
\v 26 If therefore they shall say unto you: Behold he is in the desert; go ye not out: Behold in the closets, believe it not. \ef - \fr 24.26 \fq In closets. \ft ¹ Christ having made the Church's authority bright and clear to the whole world, warneth the faithful to take heed of Heretics and Schismatics, which have their conventicles aside in certain odd places and obscure corners, alluring curious persons unto them. \+it Aug. li. 1. q. Evang. q. 38.\+it* ² For as for the coming together of Catholics to serve God in secret places, that is a necessary thing in time of persecution, and was used of Christians for three hundred years together after Christ, ³ and the Apostles also and disciples came so together in Hierusalem for fear of the Jews. And Catholics do the same at this day in our country, not drawing religion into corners from the society of the Catholic Church, but practising secretly the same faith, that in all Christendom shineth and appeareth most gloriously. \fq ¹ \ft The secret conventicles of Heretics. \fq ² \ft Catholic Christians secretly assembling in time of persecution. \fq ³ \ft \+it Act. 1, 12.\+it*\ef*
\v 27 For as lightning cometh out of the east, and appeareth even into the west, so shall also the advent of the Son of man be.
\v 28 Wheresoever the body is, thither shall the Eagles also be gathered together.
\v 29 And immediately after the tribulation of those days \x - \xt Ezech. 32, 7. Ioel 3, 15. Dan. 7, 15.\x* the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light, and the stars shall fall from Heaven, and the powers of Heaven shall be moved: \ef - \fr 24.29 \fq Immediately. \ft ¹ If the later day shall immediately follow the persecution of Antichrist, which is to endure but three years and a half, as is aforesaid: then is it mere blasphemy to say, God's Vicar is Antichrist, and that (by their own limitation) these thousand years almost. \fq ¹ \ft Antichrist.\ef*
\v 30 and then shall appear \f 1 \fr 24.30 \ft This sign of the son of man, is the holy Cross, which then shall appear to the Jews to their confusion. \+it Chrys. in Mat. ho. 77.\+it* It shall be no less confusion to Heretics that cannot abide the sign thereof.\f* the sign of the Son of man in Heaven: and then shall all tribes of the earth bewail: and they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds of Heaven with much power and majesty.
\v 31 And he shall send his Angels with a trumpet, and a great voice: and they shall gather together his Elect from the four winds, from the furthest parts of Heaven even to the ends thereof.
\v 32 And of the figtree learn a parable: When now the bough thereof is tender, and the leaves come forth, you know that summer is nigh.
\v 33 So you also, when you shall see these things, know ye that it is nigh even at the doors.
\v 34 Amen I say to you, that this generation shall not pass, till all these things be done.
\v 35 Heaven and earth shall pass, but my words shall not pass.
\v 36 But of that day and hour nobody knoweth, neither the Angels of Heaven, but the Father alone.
\v 37 And as \x - \xt Gen. 7, 5.\x* in the days of Noe, so shall also the coming of the Son of man be.
\v 38 For as they were in the days before the flood, eating and drinking, marrying and giving to marriage, even unto that day in which Noe entered into the ark,
\v 39 and knew not till the flood came, & took them all: so also shall the coming of the Son of man be.
\v 40 Then two shall be in the field: one shall be taken, & one shall be left.
\v 41 Two women grinding in the mill: one shall be taken, & one shall be left.
\v 42 Watch therefore because you know not what hour your Lord will come.
\v 43 \x - \xt 1. Thes. 5, 1.\x* But this know ye, that \f 2 \fr 24.43 \ft The Gospel upon S. Clement martyr his day Novemb. 23. And for some Confessors Bishops.\f* if the good-man of the house did know what hour the thief would come, he would surely watch, and would not suffer his house to be broken up.
\v 44 Therefore be you also ready, because at what hour you know not, the Son of man will come.
\v 45 Who, thinkest thou, is a faithful and wise servant, whom his Lord hath appointed over his family, to give them meat in season?
\v 46 Blessed is that servant, whom when his Lord cometh, he shall find so doing.
\v 47 Amen I say to you, that over all his goods shall he appoint him.
\v 48 But if that naughty servant shall say in his heart: My Lord is long a coming:
\v 49 and shall begin to strike his fellow-servants, and eateth, & drinketh with drunkards:
\v 50 the Lord of that servant shall come in a day that he hopeth not, & an hour that he knoweth not,
\v 51 and shall divide him, & appoint his portion with the hypocrites: there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
\c 25
\cd Continuing his Sermon, he bringeth two parables, of ten Virgins, and of Talents, to shew how it shall be in Domesday with the Faithful that prepare, and that prepare not themselves. 31. Then also without parables he sheweth that such Faithful as do works of mercy, shall have for them life everlasting: and such as do not, everlasting damnation.
\p
\v 1 \sc Then\sc* \f 1 \fr 25.1 \ft The Gospel upon S. Catharine's day. Novemb. 25. And for some other holy Virgins.\f* shall the Kingdom of Heaven be like to ten virgins, which taking their lamps went forth to meet the bridegroom and the bride. \ef - \fr 25.1 \fq Virgins. \ft These virgins five wise, and five foolish, signify that in the Church militant there be good and bad: which bad shall be shut out at the later day, although they have lamps (that is faith) as the other, ¹ because their lamps are out, that is, their faith is dead without charity and good works to lighten them \+it Greg. ho. 12.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Good works necessary.\ef* \ef - \fr 25.1 \fq Lamps. \ft These lamps lighted, be good works, namely of mercy, and the laudable conversation which shineth before men. \+it Aug. ep. 120. c. 33.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 And five of them were foolish, and five wise.
\v 3 But the five foolish, having taken their lamps, did not take oil with them: \ef - \fr 25.3 \fq Oil. \ft ¹ This oil is the right inward intention directing our works to God's glory, and not to the praise of ourselves in the sight of men. \+it Aug. ep. 120. c. 33.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Right intention.\ef*
\v 4 but the wise did take oil in their vessels with the lamps.
\v 5 And the bridegroom tarrying long they slumbered all and slept.
\v 6 And at midnight there was a clamour made: Behold the bridegroom cometh, go ye forth to meet him.
\v 7 Then arose all those virgins, and they trimmed their lamps.
\v 8 And the foolish said to the wise: Give us of \f 1 \fr 25.8 \ft If we be not in the favour of God, and have not our own merits, we shall not be holpen by other men's deserts at the day of judgement.\f* your oil, because our lamps are going out.
\v 9 The wise answered, saying: Lest peradventure there suffice not for us and you, go rather to them that sell, and buy for yourselves.
\v 10 And whiles they went to buy, the bridegroom was come: and they that were ready, entered with him to the marriage, and the gate was shut.
\v 11 But last of all come also the other virgins saying: Lord, Lord, open to us.
\v 12 But he answering said: Amen I say to you, I know you not.
\v 13 Watch ye therefore, because you know not the day nor the hour.
\v 14 \x - \xt Lu. 19, 12.\x* For \f 2 \fr 25.14 \ft The Gospel upon S. Nicolas' day Decemb. 6. and for some other Confessors Bishops.\f* even as a man going into a strange country, called his servants, and delivered them his goods.
\v 15 And to one he gave five talents, and to another two, and to another one, to every one according to his proper faculty: and immediately he took his journey.
\v 16 And he that had received the five talents, went his way, and occupied with the same, and gained other five.
\v 17 Likewise also he that had received the two, gained other two.
\v 18 But he that had received the one, going his way digged into the earth, and hid his lord's money.
\v 19 But after much time the lord of those servants cometh, & made account with them.
\v 20 And there came he that had received the five talents, & offered other five talents, saying: Lord five talents thou didst deliver me, behold \f 1 \fr 25.20 \ft Free will with God's grace doth merit.\f* I have gained other five besides.
\v 21 His lord said unto him: Well-fare thee good and faithful servant, because thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will place thee over many things: enter into the joy of thy lord.
\v 22 And there came also he that had received the two talents, & said: Lord two talents thou didst deliver me: behold I have gained other two.
\v 23 His lord said to him: Well-fare thee good & faithful servant: because thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will place thee over many things, enter into the joy of thy lord.
\v 24 And he also that had received the one talent, came forth, and said: Lord, I know that thou art a hard man; thou reapest where thou didst not sow, and gatherest where thou strawedst not:
\v 25 and being afraid I went, and hid thy talent in the earth: behold lo here thou hast that which thine is.
\v 26 And his lord answering, said to him: \f 1 \fr 25.26 \ft A terrible example for all such as do not employ the very least gift of God, to his glory.\f* Naughty and slothful servant, thou didst know that I reap where I sow not, & gather where I strawed not:
\v 27 thou oughtest therefore to have committed my money to the bankers, and coming I might have received mine own with usury. \ef - \fr 25.27 \fq With usury. \ft Usury is here taken for the lawful gain that a man getteth by well employing his goods. When God giveth us any talent or talents, he looketh for usury, that is, for spiritual increase of the same by our diligence and industry.\ef*
\v 28 Take ye away therefore the talent from him, and give it him that hath ten talents.
\v 29 For to \x - \xt Mr. 13, 12. Lu. 8, 18.\x* every one that hath shall be given, and he shall abound: but from him that hath not, that also which he seemeth to have, shall be taken away from him. \ef - \fr 25.29 \fq That which he seemeth to have. \ft ¹ He is said to have God's gifts, that useth them, and to such an one God will increase his gifts. He that useth them not, seemeth to have, rather than hath them. and from him God will withdraw that which before he gave. \fq ¹ \ft We must use God's gifts.\ef*
\v 30 And the unprofitable servant cast ye out into the utter darkness. There shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
\v 31 \f 1 \fr 25.31 \ft The Gospel upon Monday the first week of Lent.\f* And when the Son of man shall come in his majesty, and all the Angels with him, then shall he sit upon the seat of his majesty:
\v 32 and all nations shall be gathered together before him, and he shall separate them one from another, as the pastor separateth the sheep from the goats: \ef - \fr 25.32 \fq Separate. \ft ¹ Lo here is the separation, for in the Church militant they lived both together. As for Heretics, they went out of the church before, and separated themselves, and therefore are not to be separated here, as being judged already. \fq ¹ \ft Good and bad in the Church.\ef*
\v 33 and shall set the sheep at his right hand, but the goats at his left.
\v 34 Then shall the King say to them that shall be at his right hand: Come ye blessed of my Father, possess you the Kingdom \f 1 \fr 25.34 \ft This Kingdom then is prepared for those only that do good works: as Christ also signifieth elsewhere, saying that it is not in his power to give it otherwise. \+it See the annot. c. 20. 23.\+it*\f* prepared for you from the foundation of the world. \ef - \fr 25.34 \fq Come ye. 41. Get ye away. \ft ¹ It is no incongruity that God should say, Go into everlasting fire, to them that by their free will have repelled his mercy: and to the other, Come ye blessed of my Father, take the kingdom prepared for you, that by their free will have received faith, and confessed their sins and done penance. \+it Aug. li. 2. act. cum Fel. Manich. c. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heaven is the reward of good works, and Hell of the contrary.\ef*
\v 35 For I was an hungered, and you gave me to eat: I was a thirst, and you gave me to drink. \ef - \fr 25.35 \fq You gave me. \ft Hereby we see how much alms-deeds and all works of mercy prevail towards life everlasting, and to blot out former sins. \+it Aug. in Ps. 49.\+it*\ef*
\v 36 I was a stranger, and you took me in: naked, and you covered me: sick, and you visited me. I was in prison, and you came to me.
\v 37 Then shall the just answer him, saying: Lord, when did we see thee an hungered, and fed thee, a thirst, and gave thee drink?
\v 38 and when did we see thee a stranger, and took thee in? Or naked, and covered thee?
\v 39 Or when did we see thee sick or in prison, and came to thee?
\v 40 And the King answering, shall say to them: Amen I say to you, as long as you did it to one of these my least brethren, you did it to me.
\v 41 Then he shall say to them also that shall be at his left hand: Get ye away from me you cursed into fire everlasting, which was prepared for the devil and his angels.
\v 42 For I was an hungered, & you gave me not to eat, I was a thirst, & you gave me not to drink. \ef - \fr 25.42 \fq Gave me not. \ft He chargeth them not here that they believed not, but that they did not good works. For such did believe, but they cared not for good works, as though by dead faith they might have come to heaven. \+it Aug. de fid. & op. c. 15. & ad Dulcit. q. 2. to. 4.\+it*\ef*
\v 43 I was a stranger, and you took me not in: naked, and you covered me not: sick, & in prison, and you did not visit me.
\v 44 Then they also shall answer him, saying: Lord, when did we see thee an hungered, or a thirst, or a stranger, or naked, or sick, or in prison, & did not minister to thee?
\v 45 Then he shall answer them, saying, Amen I say to you, as long as you did it not to one of these lesser, neither did you it to me.
\v 46 And these shall go into punishment everlasting: but the just, into life everlasting.
\c 26
\cd To the Councel of the Jews, Judas by occasion of Mary Magdalen's ointment, doth sell him for little. 17 After the Paschal lamb, 26. he giveth them that bread of life (promised Io. 6.) in a mystical Sacrifice or Separation of his Body and Blood. 31. And that night he is after his prayer 47. taken of the Jews' men, Judas being their captain: and forsaken of the other eleven for fear: 57. is falsely accused, and impiously condemned of the Jews' Councel, 67. and shamefully abused of them: 69. and thrice denied of Peter: All, even as the Scriptures and himself had often foretold.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 14, 1. Luc. 22, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 26.1 \ft The Passion according to S. Matthew in these two Chapters, is the Gospel at Mass upon Palm Sunday.\f* it came to pass, when \sc Jesus\sc* had ended all these words, he said to his Disciples:
\v 2 You know that after two days shall be Pasch, and the Son of man shall be delivered to be crucified.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 26.3 \ft TENEBRÆ-Wednesday.\f* Then were gathered together the chief Priests and Ancients of the people into the court of the high Priest, who was called Caiphas:
\v 4 and they consulted how they might by some wile apprehend \sc Jesus\sc*, and kill him.
\v 5 But they said: Not on the festival day, lest perhaps there might be a tumult among the people.
\v 6 And \x - \xt Mr. 14, 3. Io. 12, 3.\x* when \sc Jesus\sc* was in Bethania in the house of Simon the Leper
\v 7 there came to him a woman having an alabaster-box of precious ointment, and poured it out upon his head as he sat at the table.
\v 8 And the Disciples seeing it, had indignation saying: Whereto is this waste? \ef - \fr 26.8 \fq This waste. \ft ¹ Cost bestowed upon Christ's body then alive, being to the same not necessary, seemed to the disciples lost and fruitless: so the like bestowed upon the same body in the Sacrament, upon altars, or Churches, seemeth to the simple lost, or less meritorious, than if the same were bestowed upon the poor. \fq ¹ \ft Cost upon Churches, altars, &c.\ef*
\v 9 For this might have been sold for much, and given to the poor.
\v 10 And \sc Jesus\sc* knowing it, said to them: Why do you molest this woman? For she hath wrought a good work upon me. \ef - \fr 26.10 \fq Good work. \ft ¹ Cost bestowed for religion, devotion, and signification, is a meritorious work, and often more meritorious than to give to the poor, though both be very good, and in some case the poor are to be preferred: yea ² in certain cases of necessity, the Church will break the very consecrated vessels and jewels of silver and gold, and bestow them in works of mercy. But we may remember very well, and our fathers knew it much better, that the poor were then best relieved, when most was bestowed upon the Church. \fq ¹ \ft Relief of the poor. \fq ² \ft \+it Ambros. li. 2. Off. c. 28.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 For the poor you have always with you: but me you have not always. \ef - \fr 26.11 \fq Have not. \ft ¹ We have him not in visible manner as he conversed on the earth with his disciples, needing relief like other poor men: but we have him after another sort in the B. Sacrament, and yet have him truly and really the self same body. Therefore he saith, they should not have him, because they should not so have him, but after another manner. As when he said \+it Luc. 24\+it* as though he were not then with them, \+it When I was with you.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Christ always with us in the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 12 For she in pouring this ointment upon my body hath done it to bury me.
\v 13 Amen I say to you, wheresoever this Gospel shall be preached in the whole world, that also which she hath done, \f 1 \fr 26.13 \ft Hereby we learn that the good works of Saints are to be recorded and set forth to their honour in the Church after their death. Whereof rise their holy days and Commemorations.\f* shall be reported for a memory of her.
\v 14 \x - \xt Mr. 14, 10. Lu. 22, 3.\x* Then went one of the Twelve, which was called Judas Iscarioth, to the chief Priests,
\v 15 & said to them: What will you give me, and I will deliver him unto you? But they appointed unto him thirty pieces of silver.
\v 16 And from thenceforth he sought opportunity to betray him.
\v 17 \x - \xt Mr. 14, 12. Lu. 22, 7.\x* And \f 2 \fr 26.17 \ft MAUNDY Thursday.\f* the first day of the Azymes the Disciples came to \sc Jesus\sc* saying: Where wilt thou that we prepare for thee to eat the Pasch?
\v 18 But \sc Jesus\sc* said: Go ye into the city to a certain man, and say to him: The Master saith, my time is at hand, with thee do I make the Pasch with my Disciples.
\v 19 And the Disciples did as \sc Jesus\sc* appointed them, and they prepared the Pasch.
\v 20 But when it was even, he sat down with his twelve Disciples. \ef - \fr 26.20 \fq Twelve. \ft ¹ It must needs be a great mystery that he was to work in the institution of the new Sacrifice by the marvelous transmutation of bread and wine into his body and blood. Whereas he admitted none (although many present in the city) but the twelve Apostles, which were already taught to believe it without contradiction \+it Io. 6\+it*, and were to have the administration and consecration thereof by the Order of Priesthood, which also was there given them to that purpose. Whereas at the eating of the Paschal lamb all the family was wont to be present. \fq ¹ \ft A wonderful mystery in the institution of the B. Sacrament.\ef*
\v 21 And while they were eating, he said: Amen I say to you, that one of you shall betray me.
\v 22 And they being very sad, began every one to say: Is it I Lord?
\v 23 But he answering said: \x - \xt Ps. 40, 10.\x* He that dippeth his hand with me in the dish, he shall betray me.
\v 24 The Son of man indeed goeth as it is written of him: but woe be to that man, by whom the Son of man shall be betrayed. It were good for him, if that man had not been born.
\v 25 And Judas that betrayed him, answering said: Is it I Rabbi? He saith to him: Thou hast said.
\v 26 And \x - \xt 1. Cor. 11, 24.\x* whiles they were at supper, \sc Jesus\sc* took bread, and blessed, and brake: and he gave to his Disciples, and said: Take ye, and eat: \sc This is my Body\sc*. \ef - \fr 26.26 \fq He took bread. \ft Here at once is instituted, for the continuance of the external office of Christ's eternal Priesthood according to the order of Melchisedec, ¹ both a Sacrifice and a Sacrament, though the Scriptures give neither of these names to this action: and our Adversaries without all reason or religion accept in a sort the one, and utterly deny the other. A Sacrifice, in that it is ordained to continue the memory of Christ's death and oblation upon the Cross, and the application of the general virtue thereof to our particular necessities, by consecrating the several elements, not into Christ's whole person as it was born of the virgin or now is in heaven, but the bread into his body apart, as betrayed, broken, and given for us: the Wine into his blood apart, as shed out of his body for remission of sins and dedication of the new Testament, which be conditions of his person as he was in sacrifice and oblation. In which mystical and unspeakable manner, he would have the Church to offer and sacrifice him daily, and he in mystery and Sacrament dieth, though now not only in heaven, but also in the Sacrament, he be indeed \+it per Concomitantiam\+it* (as the Church calleth it, that is by sequel of all his parts to each other) whole, alive, and immortal. Which point because our Adversaries understand not, \+it not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God\+it* ², they blaspheme, and abuse the people to their damnation. It is also a Sacrament, in that it is ordained to be received into our bodies and to feed the same to resurrection and immortality, and to give grace and salvation to our souls, if we worthily receive it. \fq ¹ \ft The holy Eucharist is both a Sacrifice and a Sacrament. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 22, 29.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 26.26 \fq Blessed. \ft ¹ Our Adversaries for the two words that are in Greek and Latin, \+it benedixit\+it*, and, \+it gratias egit, he blessed, he gave thank\+it*, use only the later, of purpose to signify that Christ blessed not nor consecrated the bread and the wine, and so by that blessing wrought any effect upon them, but gave thanks only to his Father, as we do in saying grace. But the truth is that the word \+it εὐλογεῖν\+it*, signifieth properly to bless, and is referred to the thing that is blessed, as \+it Luc. 9\+it* of the fishes, \+it εὐλόγησεν αὐτοὺς\+it*, \+it benedixit eis, he blessed them\+it*, and thereby wrought in them that wonderful multiplication. So the blessing of God is always effectual: and therefore here also he blessed the bread, and by that blessing, with the words following, made it his body ². \+it Ambros. li. de his qui initi. myst. c. 9. Aug. ep. 59. ad Paulinum\+it*. Now whereas taking the cup it is said, \+it he gave thanks\+it*. We say that it is all one with blessing, and that he blessed the cup, as before the bread: as it is evident by these words of S. Paul ³, \+it Calix cui benedicimus\+it*, the cup which we bless: and therefore he calleth it, \+it Calicem benedictionis\+it*, the cup of blessing, using the same Greek word that is spoken of the bread. But why is it then said here, he gave thanks? Because we translate the words faithfully as in the Greek and the Latin, and because the sense is all one, as we are taught by S. Paul before alleged, and by the Fathers, which call this giving of thanks over the cup or over the bread, the blessing thereof. S. Justin. \+it in fin. 2. Apol. Panem Eucharistisatum:\+it* ⁴ S. Irenee \+it li. 4. c. 34. Panem in quo gratiæ actæ sunt.\+it* S. Cyprian \+it de coen. Do. Calix solemni benedictione sacratus.\+it* that is, \+it The bread blessed by giving thanks upon it, The cup consecrated by solemn blessing.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The blessing of Christ referred to the creatures and working an effect in them. \fq ² \ft Consecration. \fq ³ \ft \+it 1. Cor. 10, 16.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it τὸν ἄρτον εὐχαριστηθέντα.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 26.26 \fq This is. \ft ¹ \+it The bread and the Wine be turned into the body and blood of Christ by the same omnipotent power by which the world was made, and the word was incarnate in the womb of the virgin. Damasc. li. 4. c. 14. Cypr. de Coen. Domini. Amb. li. de myst init. c. 9.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Transubstantiation.\ef* \ef - \fr 26.26 \fq My body. \ft ¹ \+it He said not, This bread is a figure of my body: or, This Wine is a figure of my blood: but, This is my body, and, This is my blood. Damasc. li. 4. c. 14. Theophyl. in hunc locum. Conc. 2. Nic. act. 6, to. 4. eiusdem actionis in fine\+it*. When some Fathers call it a figure or sign, they mean the outward forms of bread and wine. \fq ¹ \ft No figurative but a real presence.\ef*
\v 27 And taking the chalice, he gave thanks: and gave to them, saying: Drink \f 1 \fr 26.27 \ft See the margent note \+it Mar. 14, 23.\+it*\f* ye all of this.
\v 28 \sc For This is my Blood of The New Testament, Which Shall be Shed For Many Unto Remission of Sins\sc*. \ef - \fr 26.28 \fq Blood of the new Testament. \ft As the old Testament was dedicated with blood in these words, \+it This is the blood of the Testament &c. Heb. 9.\+it* So here is the institution of the new Testament in Christ's blood, by these words, \+it This is the blood of the new Testament &c.\+it* ¹ which is here mystically shed, and not only afterward upon the Cross: for the Greek is the present tense in all the Evangelist's, and S. Paul: and likewise speaking of the body \+it 1 Cor. 11.\+it* it is in the Greek the present tense, and \+it Luc. 22.\+it* and there also in the Latin. And the Heretics themselves so put it in their translations. \fq ¹ \ft \+it ἐκχυνόμενον. κλώμενον. διδόμενον.\+it*\ef*
\v 29 And I say to you, I will not drink from henceforth of this fruit of the vine, until that day when I shall drink it with you new in the Kingdom of my Father. \ef - \fr 26.29 \fq Fruit of the vine. \ft ¹ S. Luke putteth these words before he come to the consecration, whereby it seemeth that he speaketh of the wine of the Paschal lamb, and therefore nameth it, the fruit of the vine, but if he speak of the wine which was now his blood, he nameth it notwithstanding wine, as S. Paul nameth the other bread, for three causes: first because it was so before: as Eve is called Adam's bone ², and, \+it Aaron's rod devoured their rods\+it* ³. Whereas they were not now rods, but serpents. And, \+it He tasted the water turned into wine\+it* ⁴. Whereas it was now wine and not water: and such like. Secondly, because it keepeth the forms of bread and wine, and things are called as they appear: as when Raphael is called a young man \+it Tob. 5.\+it* and, \+it Three men appeared to Abraham Gen. 18.\+it* Whereas they were three Angels, thirdly, because Christ in this Sacrament is very true and principal bread and wine, feeding and refreshing us in body and soul to everlasting life. \fq ¹ \ft The elements after consecration called bread & wine. \fq ² \ft \+it Gen. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Exo. 7.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Io. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 30 \f 1 \fr 26.30 \ft THURSDAY night.\f* And an hymn being said, they went forth unto Mount-olivet.
\v 31 \x - \xt Zach. 13, 7.\x* Then \sc Jesus\sc* saith to them: All you shall be scandalized in me in this night. For it is written: \f 2 \fr 26.31 \ft The Nocturne of Matins in the Church's service, answereth to this night part of our Saviour's Passion, and so consequently the other Canonical hours to the rest.\f* \it I will strike the Pastor, and the sheep of the flock shall be dispersed.\it*
\v 32 But after I shall be risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.
\v 33 And Peter answering, said to him: Although all shall be scandalized in thee, I will never be scandalized.
\v 34 \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Amen I say to thee, that in this night before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice.
\v 35 \x - \xt Io. 13, 38.\x* Peter saith to him: Yea though I should die with thee, I will not deny thee. Likewise also said all the Disciples.
\v 36 Then \sc Jesus\sc* cometh with them into a village called Gethsemani: and he said to his Disciples: Sit you here till I go yonder, and pray.
\v 37 And taking to him Peter and the two sons of Zebedee, he began to wax sorrowful and to be sad.
\v 38 Then he saith to them: My soul is sorrowful even unto death: stay here, and watch with me.
\v 39 And being gone forward a little, he fell upon his face, praying, and saying: My Father, if it be possible, let this chalice pass from me. Nevertheless not as I will, but as thou. \ef - \fr 26.39 \fq Not as I will. \ft A perfect example of obedience and submitting ourself and our wills to God's will and ordinance in all adversity: and that we should desire nothing temporal, but under the condition of his holy pleasure and appointment.\ef*
\v 40 And he cometh to his Disciples, and findeth them sleeping, and he saith to Peter: Even so? Could you not watch one hour with me?
\v 41 Watch ye, & pray that ye enter not into tentation. The Spirit indeed is prompt, but the flesh weak. \ef - \fr 26.41 \fq Watch and pray. \ft ¹ Hereof came Vigils and Nocturnes, that is, watching and praying in the night, commonly used in the Primitive Church of all Christians, as is plain by S. Cyprian ² and ³ S. Hierom: but afterward and until this day, specially of Religious persons. \fq ¹ \ft Vigils and Nocturnes. \fq ² \ft \+it De orat. Do. nu. 15.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * Adv. Vigilant. ep. 53.\+it*\ef*
\v 42 Again the second time he went, and prayed, saying: My Father, if this chalice may not pass, but I must drink it, thy will be done.
\v 43 And he cometh again, and findeth them sleeping, for their eyes were become heavy.
\v 44 And leaving them, he went again: and he prayed the third time, saying the selfsame word.
\v 45 Then he cometh to his Disciples, and saith to them: Sleep ye now and take rest. Behold the hour approacheth, and the Son of man shall be betrayed into the hands of sinners.
\v 46 Rise, let us go: behold he approacheth that shall betray me.
\v 47 \x - \xt Io. 18, 3.\x* As he yet spake, behold Judas one of the Twelve came, and with him a great multitude with swords and clubs, sent from the chief Priests and the ancients of the people.
\v 48 And he that betrayed him, gave them a sign, saying: Whomsoever I shall kiss, that is he, hold him.
\v 49 And forthwith coming to \sc Jesus\sc*, he said: Hail Rabbi. And he kissed him.
\v 50 And \sc Jesus\sc* said to him: Friend, whereto art thou come? Then they drew near, and laid hands on \sc Jesus\sc*, and held him.
\v 51 And behold one of them that were with \sc Jesus\sc*, stretching forth his hand, drew out his sword; and striking the servant of the high Priest, cut off his ear.
\v 52 Then \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Return thy sword into his place: for all that take the sword shall perish with the sword.
\v 53 Thinkest thou that I cannot ask my Father, and he will give me presently more than twelve legions of Angels?
\v 54 How then shall the scriptures be fulfilled, that so it must be done?
\v 55 In that hour \sc Jesus\sc* said to the multitudes: You are come out as it were to a thief with swords and clubs to apprehend me. I sat daily with you teaching in the temple, and you laid no hands on me.
\v 56 And all this was done, that the scriptures of the Prophets might be fulfilled. Then the Disciples all leaving him, fled.
\v 57 But they taking hold of \sc Jesus\sc*, led him to Caiphas the high Priest, where the Scribes and ancients were assembled.
\v 58 And Peter followed him a far off, even to the court of the high Priest. And going in he sat with the servants, that he might see the end.
\v 59 And the chief Priests and the whole Council sought false witness against \sc Jesus\sc*, that they might put him to death:
\v 60 and they found not, whereas many false witnesses had come in. And last of all there came two false witnesses;
\v 61 and they said: \x - \xt Io. 2, 19.\x* This man said, I am able to destroy the temple of God, and after three days to re-edify it.
\v 62 And the high Priest rising up, said to him: Answerest thou nothing to the things which these do testify against thee?
\v 63 But \sc Jesus\sc* held his peace. And the high Priest said to him: I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us if thou be Christ the Son of God.
\v 64 \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Thou hast said. Nevertheless I say to you, hereafter you shall see \x - \xt Dan. 7, 13.\x* the Son of man sitting on the right hand of the power of God, and coming in the clouds of Heaven.
\v 65 Then the high Priest rent his garments, saying: He hath blasphemed, what need we witnesses any further? Behold, now you have heard the blasphemy;
\v 66 how think you? But they answering said: He is guilty of death.
\v 67 Then did they spit on his face, and buffeted him, & other smote his face with the palms of their hands,
\v 68 saying: Prophesy unto us O Christ; who is he that strook thee?
\v 69 But Peter sat without in the court; and there came to him one wench, saying: Thou also wast with \sc Jesus\sc* the Galilean. \ef - \fr 26.69 \fq Wench. \ft ¹ S. Gregory declaring the difference of the Apostles before the receiving of the Holy Ghost and after, saith thus: \+it Even this very Pastor of the Church himself, at whose most sacred body we sit, how weak he was, the wench can tell you: but how strong he was after, his answer to the high Priest declareth, Act. 5, 29: We must obey God rather than men. Greg. ho. 20. Io. Evang.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The virtue of the Holy Ghost.\ef*
\v 70 But he denied before them all, saying: I wot not what thou sayest.
\v 71 And as he went out of the gate, another wench saw him, and she saith to them that were there: And this fellow also was with \sc Jesus\sc* the Nazarite.
\v 72 And again he denied with an oath: That I know not the man.
\v 73 And after a little they came that stood by, and said to Peter: Surely thou also art of them: for even thy speech doth bewray thee.
\v 74 \f 1 \fr 26.74 \ft To this time the LAUDS do answer in the Church's service.\f* Then he began to curse and to swear that he knew not the man. And incontinent the cock crew. \ef - \fr 26.74 \fq To curse. \ft ¹ A goodly example and warning to man's infirmity, and to take heed of presumption, and to hang only upon God in tentations. \fq ¹ \ft Man's infirmity.\ef*
\v 75 And Peter remembered the word of \sc Jesus\sc* which he had said: Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice. And going forth, he wept bitterly. \ef - \fr 26.75 \fq Wept bitterly. \ft ¹ S. Ambrose in his Hymn that the Church useth at Lauds, speaking of this, saith, \+it Hoc ipsa Petra ecclesiæ canente, culpam diluit.\+it* When the Cock crew, the Rock of the Church himself washed away his fault. \+it S. August. 1. Retract. c. 21.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Peter's tears and repentance.\ef*
\c 27
\cd The chief of the Jews accuse him to Pilate the Gentile (his betrayer, and the Judge, and the Judge's wife, testifying in the mean time manifoldly his innocency:) 20. and persuade the common people also not only to prefer the murderer Barabbas, but also to cry, CRUCIFIGE: (All, to the reprobation of their whole nation, and nothing but fulfulling the Scriptures.) 27. After many illusions, 31. he is crucified by the Gentiles. 38. which the Jews seeing, do triumph as if they had now the victory. 45. But even then by many wonderful works he declareth his might, to their confusion 57. Finally being buried, they to make all sure, set soldiers to keep \it his sepulcher.\it*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 15, 1. Lu. 23, 1. Io. 18, 28.\x*\f 2 \fr 27.1 \ft PRIME, or Hora prima, in the Church's Service.\f* when morning was come; all the chief Priests and ancients of the people consulted together against \sc Jesus\sc*, that they might put him to death.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 27.2 \ft GOOD FRIDAY.\f* And they brought him bound and delivered him to Ponce Pilate the President.
\v 3 Then Judas that betrayed him, seeing that he was condemned, repenting him, returned the thirty silver pieces to the chief Priests and ancients, \ef - \fr 27.3 \fq Repenting him. \ft ¹ Note how speedily the plague of God falleth after sin, and specially men must note what torment of conscience, and desperation often followeth the shedding of innocent blood. \fq ¹ \ft Horror of conscience.\ef*
\v 4 saying: I have sinned, betraying just blood. But they said: What is that to us? look thou to it.
\v 5 And casting down the silver pieces in the temple, he departed: and went and hanged himself with an halter. \ef - \fr 27.5 \fq Hung himself. \ft ¹ If he had rightly repented, notwithstanding his horrible treason, he might have obtained mercy: but by hanging himself he took away all means of mercy and salvation, because he died finally impenitent. \fq ¹ \ft Desperation.\ef*
\v 6 And the chief Priests having taken the silver pieces, said: It is not lawful to cast them into the \f 1 \fr 27.6 \ft This Corbana was a place about the Temple, which received the people's gifts or offerings. See Mar. 12, v. 42.\f* Corbana, because it is the price of blood.
\v 7 And after they had consulted together, they bought with them the potter's field, to be a burying place for strangers.
\v 8 For this cause that field was called \it Haceldama,\it* that is, \it the field of blood,\it* even to this present day.
\v 9 Then was fulfilled that which was spoken by Jeremie the Prophet, saying: \x - \xt Zach. 11, 12.\x* \it And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of the priced, whom they did price of the children of Israel.\it*
\v 10 \it And they gave them into the potter's field, as our Lord did appoint to me.\it*
\v 11 And \sc Jesus\sc* stood before the President, and the President asked him, saying: Art thou the King of the Jews? \sc Jesus\sc* saith to him: Thou sayest.
\v 12 And when he was accused of the chief Priests and Ancients, he answered nothing.
\v 13 Then Pilate saith to him: Doest thou not hear how many testimonies they allege against thee?
\v 14 And he answered him not to any word: so that the President did marvel exceedingly.
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 27.15 \ft HORA TERTIA in the Church's Service.\f* And upon the solemn day the President had accustomed to release unto the People one prisoner, whom they would.
\v 16 And he had then a notorious prisoner, that was called Barabbas.
\v 17 They therefore being gathered together, Pilate said: Whom will you that I release to you, Barabbas, or \sc Jesus\sc* that is called Christ?
\v 18 For he knew that for envy they had delivered him.
\v 19 And as he was sitting in place of judgement, his wife sent unto him, saying: Have thou nothing to do with that just man. For I have suffered many things this day in my sleep for him.
\v 20 But the chief Priests and ancients persuaded the people, that they should ask Barabbas, and make \sc Jesus\sc* away.
\v 21 And the President answering, said to them: Whether will you of the two to be released unto you? But they said, Barabbas.
\v 22 Pilate saith to them: What shall I do then with \sc Jesus\sc* that is called Christ? They say all: Let him be crucified.
\v 23 The President said to them: Why what evil hath he done? But they cried the more, saying: Let him be crucified.
\v 24 And Pilate seeing that he nothing prevailed, but rather tumult was toward; taking water he washed his hands before the People, saying: I am innocent of the blood of this just man: look you to it. \ef - \fr 27.24 \fq Innocent of his blood. \ft ¹ Though Pilate was much more innocent than the Jews, and would have been free from the murder of our Saviour, seeking all the means that he could (without offending the people and the Emperor's laws) to dimiss him: Yet he is damned for being the minister of the people's wicked will against his own conscience. Even as all Officers be, and specially the Judges and Juries which execute laws of temporal Princes against Catholic men: for all such be guilty of innocent blood, and be nothing excused by that they execute other men's will according to the laws, which be unjust. For they should rather suffer death themselves, than put an innocent man to death. \fq ¹ \ft They that execute laws against their conscience, are like to Pilate.\ef*
\v 25 And the whole people answering, said: His blood be upon us, and upon our children.
\v 26 Then he released to them Barabbas, and having scourged \sc Jesus\sc*, delivered him unto them for to be crucified.
\v 27 Then the President's soldiers taking \sc Jesus\sc* into the Palace, gathered together unto him the whole band:
\v 28 \x - \xt Io. 19, 2.\x* and stripping him, put a scarlet cloak about him.
\v 29 and platting a crown of thorns, put it upon his head, and a reed in his right hand. And bowing the knee before him, they mocked him, saying: Hail King of the Jews.
\v 30 And spitting upon him, they took the reed, and smote his head.
\v 31 And after they had mocked him, they took off the cloak from him, and put on him his own garments, and led him away to crucify him.
\v 32 And in going they found a man of Cyrene, named Simon: him they forced to take up his Cross.
\v 33 \f 1 \fr 27.33 \ft HORA SEXTA in the Church's Service.\f* And they came into the place that is called Golgotha, which is, the place of Calvary.
\v 34 And they gave him wine to drink mingled with gall. And when he had tasted, he would not drink.
\v 35 And after they had crucified him; they divided his garments, casting lots; that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the Prophet, saying: \x - \xt Ps. 21, 19.\x* \it They divided my garments among them; and upon my vesture they did cast lots.\it*
\v 36 And they sat and watched him.
\v 37 And they put over his head his cause written: \sc This is Jesus The King of The Jews\sc*.
\v 38 Then were crucified with him two thieves; one on the right hand, and one on the left.
\v 39 And they that passed by, blasphemed him, wagging their heads,
\v 40 and saying: Vah, thou that destroyest the Temple of God and in three days doest re-edify it; save thine own self: if thou be the Son of God, come down from the Cross. \ef - \fr 27.40 \fq If thou be the Son. \ft ¹ Marvel not, when thou hearest our Saviour in the B. Sacrament mocked at, or seest him abused of wicked men, that he straight revengeth not such blasphemies: or that he sheweth not himself there visibly and to the senses, when faithless Heretics will say, Let me see him, taste him, &c. for he suffered here the like on the Cross, when he might at his will have come down with as much ease as he rose when he was dead. \fq ¹ \ft Christ derided in the B. Sacrament, even as upon the Cross.\ef*
\v 41 In like manner also the chief Priests with the Scribes and ancients mocking, said:
\v 42 He saved other; himself he cannot save: if he be the King of Israel, let him now come down from the Cross, and we will believe him.
\v 43 \x - \xt Ps. 21, 9. Sap. 2, 18.\x* He trusted in God; let him now deliver him if he will: for he said, that I am the Son of God.
\v 44 And the self same thing the thieves also that were crucified with him, reproached him withal.
\v 45 And from the sixth hour, there was darkness made upon the whole earth, until the ninth hour.
\v 46 And about the ninth hour \sc Jesus\sc* cried with a mighty voice, saying: \it Eli, Eli, lamma-sabacthani?\it* that is, \it My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\it* \ef - \fr 27.46 \fq Why hast thou forsaken me? \ft Beware here of the detestable blasphemy of Calvin and the Calvinists ¹, who thinking not the bodily death of Christ sufficient, say, that he was also here so forsaken and abandoned of his Father, that he sustained in soul and conscience the very fears and torments of the damned. Which strange doctrine we find also avouched in an English Catechism set forth by R. H. Anno. 1583. in these words: \+it Quest. By what means hath Christ appeased his Father wrath, and ransomed us? Answer. By suffering death of the Cross, and the torment of Hell in soul, and conscience.\+it* Quite contrary to the holy Scriptures, which always attribute our Redemption to Christ's death, and shedding of his most precious blood, as to a most sufficient, & only ransom for all mankind. Ro. 5, 10. Heb. 9, 14. 1. Pet. 1, 19. 1. Io. 1, 7. and in innumerable other places. But Calvin and his Disciples hold this wicked paradox, to take away the Article of Christ's descending into Hell after his death, saying that his descending was nothing else, but that his soul (remaining as yet in his body) suffered the very pains of Hell upon the Cross ². Whereas indeed his descending was in soul (parting forth of the body) into that place, where the Fathers of the old Testament were detained, expecting his coming to deliver them (and that with triumph, and not in pain) as is proved by the Scriptures, and many ancient Fathers, in the Annotations upon S. Luke. c. 16, 22. Act. 2, 27. 1. Pet. 3, 19. and in other places. And concerning the true sense of these words: \+it Why hast thou forsaken me?\+it* as well by conferring them with other holy Scriptures, as by the uniform consent of the ancient Fathers, our Saviour would signify hereby, that his pains (being now so long on the Cross and ready to die) were very great, and therefore according to the infirmity of his human nature, for very anguish (as before in the garden he sweat blood, when he was but toward his Passion) he saith he was forsaken, for two causes. First because it was the will of God not to deliver him, but that he should die. Secondly, because his divine nature did so repress itself for the time, that he felt no comfort thereof at all, but was left to die in extreme pains as a mere man. Yea destitute of such consolation, as his holy Martyrs commonly have in their last agony. See Origin, Tract. 35. in Mat. S. Hilary li. 10. de Trinit. S. Leo ser. 17. de Passione S. Bede and others upon this place. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Catechis. Calv. & Instit. li. 2. 16. 5. 10.\+it* \fq ² \ft Detestable blasphemy of Calvin. And other Ministers of his school.\ef*
\v 47 And certain that stood there and heard, said: He calleth Elias.
\v 48 And incontinent one of them running, took a sponge, & filled it with vinegar; and put it on a reed, and gave him to drink.
\v 49 And other said: Let be, let us see whether Elias come to deliver him.
\v 50 And \sc Jesus\sc* again crying with a mighty voice, yielded up the ghost.
\v 51 \f 1 \fr 27.51 \ft HORA NONA in the Church's Service.\f* And behold the veil of the Temple was rent in two pieces, from the top even to the bottom, and the earth did quake, and the rocks were rent,
\v 52 And the graves were opened: and many bodies of the saints that had slept, rose.
\v 53 And they going forth out of the graves after his resurrection, came into the holy city; and appeared to many.
\v 54 And the Centurion and they that were with him watching \sc Jesus\sc*, having seen the earth-quake and the things that were done, were sore afraid, saying: Indeed this was the Son of God.
\v 55 And there were there many women a far off, which had followed \sc Jesus\sc* from Galilee, ministering unto him:
\v 56 among whom was Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the Sons of Zebedee.
\v 57 \f 1 \fr 27.57 \ft HORA VESPERARUM, or, Evensong.\f* And when it was evening, there came a certain rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was disciple to \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 58 He went to Pilate, and asked the body of \sc Jesus\sc*. Then Pilate commanded that the body should be delivered.
\v 59 \f 1 \fr 27.59 \ft HORA COMPLETORII, or, Compline.\f* And Joseph taking the body, wrapt it in clean sindon, \ef - \fr 27.59 \fq Wrapped. \ft ¹ This honour and duty done to Christ's body being dead, was marvelous grateful and meritorious. And this wrapping of it in clean sindon may signify by S. Hierom ², that the Body of our Lord is to be wrapped not in gold, precious stones, and silk, but in pure linen. And so in the whole Church it is observed by ³ S. Silvester's constitution, that the Corporal ⁴ whereupon our Lord's body lieth on the altar, must be pure and plain linen. \fq ¹ \ft Reverent using of our Lord's Body. \fq ² \ft \+it S. Hier. in hunc locum.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * to. 1. Concil.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Corporals.\ef*
\v 60 & laid it in his own new monument, which he had hewed out in a rock. And he rolled a great stone to the door of the monument, & went his way.
\v 61 And there was there Mary Magdalene, & the other Mary, sitting over against the sepulchre.
\v 62 \f 1 \fr 27.62 \ft SATURDAY called Sabbatum sanctum.\f* And the next day, which is after the Parasceve, the chief Priests and the Pharisees came together to Pilate,
\v 63 saying: Sir, we have remembered, that that seducer said yet living, After three days I will rise again.
\v 64 Command therefore the sepulchre to be kept until the third day; lest perhaps his Disciples come, and steal him, & say to the people, he is risen from the dead: and the last error shall be worse than the first.
\v 65 Pilate said to them: You have a guard: go, guard it as you know.
\v 66 And they departing, made the sepulchre sure; sealing up the stone, with watchmen.
\c 28
\cd He riseth again the third day, and (the blind most obstinate Jews by bribery working to their own reprobation) he appeareth to his Disciples in Galilee (as both before his Passion he foretold them Mat. 26, and now after his Resurrection, first the Angel, then also himself appointed by the women) 18. and sendeth them to all Nations, to build his Church among the Gentiles.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \x - \xt Mr. 16, 1. Luc. 24, 1. Io. 20, 1.\x*\f 2 \fr 28.1 \ft The Gospel for the night Mass of Christ's Resurrection, which is now used to be said on Easter eve.\f*\f 3 \fr 28.1 \ft EASTER day.\f* in the evening of the Sabboth which dawneth on the first of the Sabboth, came Mary Magdalene, and the other Mary to see the sepulchre. \ef - \fr 28.1 \fq To see the Sepulcher. \ft ¹ The devout women came to visit our Saviour's sepulcher, and for their devotion first deserved to know the Resurrection, and to see him risen. The honour of the which Sepulcher and the Pilgrimage thereunto in the Primitive Church, S. Hierom ² declareth in these words, \+it The Jews sometime honoured Sancta Sanctorum, because there were the Cherubs, and Propitiatory, and the Ark of the Testament, Manna, Aaron's rod, and the golden altar. Doth not the Sepulcher of our Lord seem unto thee more honourable? Which as often as we enter into, so often do we see our Saviour lie in the sindon: and staying there a while, we see the Angel again sit at his feet, and at his head the napkin wrapped together. The glory of whose Sepulcher, we know was long prophesied\+it* ³ \+it before Joseph hewed it out, by Esay saying, And his rest shall be honour: to wit, because the place of our Lord's burial should be honoured of all men\+it*. And at this present, notwithstanding the Turks' dominion, yet do the Religious Christian Catholic men by God's mighty providence keep the holy Sepulcher, which is within a goodly Church, and Christians come out of all the world in Pilgrimage to it. \fq ¹ \ft The holy Sepulcher, and Pilgrimage thereunto. \fq ² \ft \+it In Ep. 17. Paulæ & Eustoch. ad Marcel. to. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Esa. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 2 And behold there was made a great earth-quake. For an Angel of our Lord descended from Heaven: and coming, rolled back the stone, and sat upon it:
\v 3 and his countenance was as lightning, and his garment as snow.
\v 4 And for fear of him, the watchmen were frighted, and became as dead.
\v 5 And the Angel answering said to the women: Fear not you. For I know that you seek \sc Jesus\sc* that was crucified.
\v 6 He is not here, for he is risen, \x - \xt Mt. 24, 32.\x* as he said. Come, and see the place where our Lord was laid.
\v 7 And going quickly, tell ye his Disciples that he is risen: and behold he goeth before you into Galilee: there you shall see him. Lo I have foretold you.
\v 8 And they went forth quickly out of the monument with fear and great joy, running to tell his Disciples.
\v 9 And behold \sc Jesus\sc* met them, saying: All hail. But they came near and took hold of his feet, and adored him.
\v 10 Then \sc Jesus\sc* said to them: Fear not. Go, tell my brethren that they go into Galilee, there they shall see me.
\v 11 Who when they were departed, behold certain of the watchmen came into the city, and told the chief Priests all things that had been done.
\v 12 And being assembled together with the Ancients, taking counsel, they gave a great sum of money to the soldiers,
\v 13 saying: Say you, that his Disciples came by night, and stole him away when we were asleep.
\v 14 And if the President shall hear of this, we will persuade him, and make you secure.
\v 15 But they taking the money, did as they were taught. And this word was bruited abroad among the Jews, even unto this day.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 28.16 \ft The Gospel upon Friday in Easter week.\f* And the eleven Disciples went into Galilee, unto the mount where \sc Jesus\sc* had appointed them.
\v 17 And seeing him they adored, but some doubted.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 28.18 \ft The Gospel in the feast of the B. Trinity.\f* And \sc Jesus\sc* coming near spake unto them, saying: All power is given to me in Heaven and in earth.
\v 19 going therefore teach ye all Nations: \sc Baptizing Them in The Name of The Father, And of The Son, And of The Holy Ghost\sc*, \ef - \fr 28.19 \fq Going therefore. \ft ¹ Commission to baptize and preach to all Nations given to the Apostles, and grounded upon Christ's sovereign authority, to whom was given all power in heaven and in earth. \fq ¹ \ft a. The Catholic Church to be gathered of all Nations.\ef*
\v 20 teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and behold I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the world. \ef - \fr 28.20 \fq With you all days. \ft ¹ Here Christ doth promise his concurrence with his Apostles and their successors, as well in preaching as ministering the Sacraments, and his protection of the Church never to cease till the world's end: contrary to our Adversaries, saying that the Church hath failed many hundred years till Luther and Calvin. \fq ¹ \ft b. Christ's continual protection of the same Church.\ef*
